{"version":1,"lang":"en","places":{"Q7296":{"t":"Africa's rooftop, a snowy island above the plains","s":"Kilimanjaro belongs to no range: it rises alone, a broad dark volcano swelling out of the savanna. Its summit, Uhuru Peak on the Kibo cone, is 5,895 m high — the top of Africa, yet still about five kilometres below you. Look for the white rim of ice on Kibo, shrinking year by year but still there, just three degrees south of the equator.","w":"A vast lone dome with a flat, white-streaked top and the jagged Mawenzi peak on its east flank, near the Kenya border.","f":["Kilimanjaro is built from three volcanic cones: Kibo, Mawenzi and Shira.","It is the highest free-standing mountain in the world, rising about 4,900 m above the plains at its base.","Its ice fields have shrunk by more than 80% since the early 20th century."],"q":{"q":"Which of Kilimanjaro's three volcanic cones holds its summit?","a":"Kibo","x":["Mawenzi","Shira"]}},"Q39231":{"t":"Japan's lone volcano, white-capped and nearly symmetrical","s":"On a clear day Fuji is unmistakable: one broad, even cone standing by itself west of Tokyo, its upper slopes white from autumn to spring. It last erupted in 1707. The summit, 3,776 m, sits roughly seven kilometres below you, and in summer the snow shrinks to thin streaks in the gullies, leaving a dark, ash-coloured mountain.","w":"A lone snow-topped cone about 100 km south-west of Tokyo, with small lakes strung along its northern foot.","f":["The five lakes around Fuji's northern foot formed where old lava flows dammed the valleys.","The 1707 eruption blew out a side crater on the south-east flank that breaks the cone's symmetry.","Fuji became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 2013 — as a cultural site, not a natural one."],"q":{"q":"How many lakes make up the famous group at Fuji's northern foot?","a":"Five","x":["Three","Seven"]}},"Q513":{"t":"The world's highest point, only about 2 km below you","s":"Everest rarely looks tallest from the air: it hides among giant neighbours, a dark pyramid often trailing a plume of snow blown east by the jet stream. Its summit, 8,848 m, is only about two kilometres below cruising height. The rock at the very top is limestone that formed on an ancient sea floor and was lifted as India pushed into Asia.","w":"A dark three-sided pyramid, often with a snow plume, rising above the glaciers on the Nepal–Tibet border.","f":["Tibetans call it Chomolungma; in Nepal it is Sagarmatha.","Tenzing Norgay and Edmund Hillary first reached the summit on 29 May 1953.","The English name, adopted in 1865, honours George Everest, a British surveyor-general of India."],"q":{"q":"Who reached the summit of Everest with Tenzing Norgay in 1953?","a":"Edmund Hillary","x":["George Mallory","Reinhold Messner"]}},"Q43512":{"t":"Earth's second summit, a pyramid of the Karakoram","s":"The name K2 is just a surveyor's label — peak 2 on a Karakoram list drawn up in 1856, when no widely used local name was found. It is the second-highest mountain on Earth, 8,611 m, a steep, almost perfect pyramid ringed by rivers of ice. Look for a sharp summit at the head of a long glacier, far from any road or town.","w":"A lone sharp pyramid at the head of broad glacier tongues, on the border crest of the Karakoram.","f":["The Italians Lino Lacedelli and Achille Compagnoni made the first ascent in 1954.","The first winter ascent came only in January 2021, by a team of Nepali climbers.","Its pyramid rises more than 3,000 m above the glaciers at its foot."],"q":{"q":"What does the 'K' in K2 stand for?","a":"Karakoram","x":["Kashmir","Kunlun"]}},"Q1374":{"t":"The Alps' sharpest tooth, facing all four compass points","s":"Even from cruise height the Matterhorn looks carved: a hooked pyramid whose four faces point almost exactly north, south, east and west. It stands on the Swiss–Italian border above Zermatt, 4,478 m high and roughly six and a half kilometres below you. On a sunny morning its east face is lit while the north face stays in shadow, so the peak looks sliced in two.","w":"A lone, hooked rock pyramid among glaciers, west of the big Monte Rosa massif on the Swiss–Italian border.","f":["Edward Whymper's party made the first ascent on 14 July 1865.","Italians call it Monte Cervino; in French it is Mont Cervin.","A summit ridge about 100 m long links two tops, a Swiss one and an Italian one."],"q":{"q":"From which Swiss village is the Matterhorn most often climbed?","a":"Zermatt","x":["Grindelwald","Saas-Fee"]}},"ne-pk-1159105947":{"t":"The volcano that buried Pompeii, still watching Naples","s":"Look for a cone inside a broken ring: Vesuvius grew within the collapsed crater of an older volcano, Monte Somma, whose ridge wraps around its north side. Naples spreads right up to its lower slopes, a dense grey carpet along the bay. On a clear day the summit crater, 1,281 m high, shows as a dark hole. Vesuvius has been quiet since 1944, but it is watched around the clock.","w":"A dark two-humped volcano on the Bay of Naples, with the city spread along the shore to its north-west.","f":["In AD 79 its ash buried Pompeii and Herculaneum, preserving the Roman towns for archaeologists.","It is the only volcano on mainland Europe to have erupted in the last hundred years.","Its observatory, founded in 1841, is the oldest volcano observatory in the world."],"q":{"q":"Which Roman town, buried in AD 79, lies at the foot of Vesuvius?","a":"Pompeii","x":["Ostia","Paestum"]}},"Q583":{"t":"Western Europe's highest dome, white all year","s":"Mont Blanc is less a spike than a huge white shoulder: a broad dome of ice on a massif shared by France, Italy and Switzerland. At about 4,807 m it is the highest point of the Alps, yet still more than six kilometres below you. On its north side long glaciers pour down toward the valley of Chamonix; on the Italian side the walls drop steeply to Courmayeur.","w":"A broad, rounded white dome above deep valleys, with long glacier tongues reaching toward Chamonix on its north-west side.","f":["Jacques Balmat and Michel-Gabriel Paccard first reached the summit in 1786.","The summit is a cap of ice, and its height shifts by a metre or two from year to year.","The Mont Blanc road tunnel, 11.6 km long, runs beneath the massif between France and Italy."],"q":{"q":"In which year was Mont Blanc first climbed?","a":"1786","x":["1865","1741"]}},"ne-pk-1159107423":{"t":"Europe's restless giant, often trailing a plume","s":"Etna is the dark giant of eastern Sicily, a broad volcano whose summit, about 3,300 m high, often smokes. Its lower slopes are green with orchards and vineyards, the upper ones black with fresh lava, and in winter snow lies over both. Look for a thin plume drifting downwind: Etna erupts so often that its height changes every few years.","w":"A vast dark cone above Sicily's east coast, north of Catania, often with a steam plume and snow on top.","f":["Etna covers about 1,200 km², and its base is roughly 140 km around.","It is the highest active volcano in Europe outside the Caucasus.","Etna became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 2013."],"q":{"q":"Which city lies at the southern foot of Etna?","a":"Catania","x":["Palermo","Messina"]}},"ne-gl-antarctic-ice-sheet":{"t":"Most of the world's ice, on one frozen continent","s":"If your route crosses Antarctica, the whiteness below is not snow on land but ice kilometres thick. The Antarctic Ice Sheet covers about 14 million km² and holds roughly 90% of all the ice on Earth. Its surface is so smooth and bright that the horizon can vanish; only nunataks — rocky peaks poking through the ice — break the white.","w":"An endless white surface with faint wind ripples; dark rock shows only near the coast and along mountain ranges.","f":["If it all melted, global sea level would rise by about 58 metres.","The ice is about two kilometres thick on average.","Its weight has pressed the bedrock beneath it down by hundreds of metres."],"q":{"q":"Roughly what share of Earth's ice lies in the Antarctic Ice Sheet?","a":"About 90%","x":["About 50%","About 70%"]}},"ne-gl-greenland-ice-sheet":{"t":"A 3-kilometre-thick lid on the world's largest island","s":"Crossing the North Atlantic, many flights pass over this white desert. The ice sheet covers about 80% of Greenland, some 1.7 million km², and rises into a dome more than 3,000 m high. Near the coast it breaks into crevassed outlet glaciers that snake down to fjords full of icebergs; in summer, bright blue meltwater lakes dot its western edge.","w":"Flat white expanse inland; at the edges, dark mountains, glacier tongues and fjords speckled with icebergs.","f":["If the whole ice sheet melted, sea level would rise by roughly 7 metres.","Its weight has pushed the bedrock in the centre of Greenland below sea level.","Ice cores drilled through it hold a climate record reaching back more than 100,000 years."],"q":{"q":"How much would sea level rise if Greenland's ice sheet melted?","a":"About 7 metres","x":["About 1 metre","About 60 metres"]}},"Q1001294":{"t":"The coldest high desert on the planet","s":"Around the South Pole the ice rises into a vast, gently sloping plateau, mostly around 3,000 m high and as flat as a frozen sea. It is a true desert: each year the snowfall adds up to only a few centimetres of water. Vostok Station, on this plateau, recorded −89.2 °C in 1983 — the lowest air temperature ever measured at the Earth's surface.","w":"A featureless white plain to the horizon, scored only by long wind-carved snow ridges called sastrugi.","f":["The South Pole itself lies on the plateau, about 2,835 m above sea level.","Antarctica's fierce coastal winds start here, as heavy cold air slides off the plateau toward the sea.","Its cold, dry, still air makes it one of the best places on Earth for telescopes."],"q":{"q":"What temperature did Vostok Station record in 1983?","a":"−89.2 °C","x":["−71.2 °C","−79.8 °C"]}},"Q165440":{"t":"The white mountain that once topped the world's list","s":"Dhaulagiri means 'white mountain' in Sanskrit, and from the air it earns the name: a massive snowy wall rising straight out of central Nepal. Its main summit, about 8,170 m, is the seventh-highest on Earth. Just east of it the Kali Gandaki river cuts through the Himalaya between Dhaulagiri and Annapurna, carving one of the deepest valleys on the planet.","w":"A huge snowy massif west of the deep Kali Gandaki gorge, with Annapurna's white ridges on the other side.","f":["After surveys in 1808 it was believed for some 30 years to be the highest mountain in the world.","A Swiss-Austrian expedition made the first ascent in 1960.","The Kali Gandaki gorge beside it was for centuries a trade route between Tibet and India."],"q":{"q":"Which river gorge separates Dhaulagiri from Annapurna?","a":"Kali Gandaki","x":["Arun","Trisuli"]}},"Q130736":{"t":"The 'naked mountain' at the western end of the Himalaya","s":"Nanga Parbat stands apart: the westernmost 8,000-metre peak of the Himalaya, with the Indus curving around its foot. Its name means 'naked mountain', perhaps for walls too steep to hold snow. The Rupal Face on its south side rises about 4,600 m from valley to summit, one of the tallest mountain walls on Earth. The top, 8,125 m, is less than three kilometres below you.","w":"An isolated giant massif south of a bend in the Indus, with a sheer southern wall and glaciers spilling north.","f":["Hermann Buhl made the first ascent in 1953, reaching the summit alone.","It is the ninth-highest mountain on Earth.","The Indus flows past its northern flank at around 1,100 m — seven kilometres below the summit."],"q":{"q":"What does the name Nanga Parbat mean?","a":"Naked mountain","x":["White mountain","Mother of snows"]}},"Q82019":{"t":"Five treasures of snow above Sikkim and Nepal","s":"Kangchenjunga is not one summit but a crowded crown of them, five main tops on a single massif. At 8,586 m it is the third-highest mountain on Earth, and until the 1850s it was thought to be the highest. Look for a broad white wall towering over the green hills of Darjeeling and Sikkim; at sunrise its snow turns pink long before the valleys wake.","w":"A massive white massif rising above green foothills, north of Darjeeling on the Nepal–India border.","f":["Its name is usually translated from Tibetan as 'Five Treasures of the Snow'.","The first climbers, in 1955, stopped just short of the top out of respect for local beliefs.","From the hill town of Darjeeling, about 75 km away, it fills the northern horizon."],"q":{"q":"Where does Kangchenjunga rank among the world's highest mountains?","a":"Third","x":["Second","Fifth"]}},"Q604846":{"t":"A red tableland of some of Earth's oldest rock","s":"For hours on a flight across Australia the ground barely changes: rust-red sand, pale salt lakes and dark scrub, a few hundred metres above sea level. This is the Western Plateau, spread over most of the western two-thirds of the continent. Its bedrock is among the oldest on Earth, and the deserts on top — the Gibson, the Great Sandy, the Great Victoria — are combed with long dune lines.","w":"Endless red and ochre ground streaked with parallel dunes and dotted with white salt lakes that shine like mirrors.","f":["Zircon crystals from the Jack Hills in Western Australia are about 4.4 billion years old, the oldest known pieces of Earth.","Its dunes often run in parallel lines for tens of kilometres, aligned with the prevailing winds.","Salt lakes such as Lake Carnegie hold water only after rare heavy rains."],"q":{"q":"What record do zircons from Western Australia's Jack Hills hold?","a":"Oldest known Earth minerals","x":["Largest gold nuggets","Most radioactive rocks"]}},"Q179842":{"t":"The roof of the world, averaging over 4,500 m","s":"Below you lies the highest large plateau on Earth, averaging more than 4,500 m and covering about 2.5 million km². From cruise it looks like another planet: brown and ochre plains, turquoise lakes with salt-white shores, snowy ranges ruled across it. Asia's great rivers — the Yangtze, Yellow, Mekong, Indus and Brahmaputra among them — begin up here.","w":"Bare brown highland with scattered turquoise lakes and white ridges; the Himalaya forms its sharp southern rim.","f":["It holds so much glacier ice that it is often called the Third Pole.","Lhasa, in the south of the plateau, lies at about 3,650 m.","The plateau is still rising as India keeps pushing north into Asia."],"q":{"q":"Which nickname does the Tibetan Plateau get for its glaciers?","a":"The Third Pole","x":["The White Continent","The Frozen Crown"]}},"ne-gl-flade-isblink":{"t":"Greenland's biggest ice cap that stands on its own","s":"Near Greenland's north-eastern tip a separate dome of ice sits apart from the main ice sheet: Flade Isblink, some 8,000 km² of white. Its Danish name means roughly 'flat iceblink' — the glow that ice casts on low clouds. The ice is up to about 600 m thick, and parts of its edge surge forward from time to time, pushing ice out to sea.","w":"A smooth, low white dome on a peninsula of north-east Greenland, cut off from the main ice sheet by bare land.","f":["It is the largest ice cap in Greenland that is not joined to the main ice sheet.","Station Nord, the only inhabited place in the area, lies just to its north-west.","The whole area is part of Northeast Greenland National Park, the largest national park in the world."],"q":{"q":"What does the word 'iceblink' describe?","a":"The glow of ice on clouds","x":["A crack in sea ice","A glacier's calving wall"]}},"Q163758":{"t":"The highest point of the frozen continent","s":"Vinson is the summit of Antarctica, 4,892 m, a long snowy massif in the Sentinel Range of the Ellsworth Mountains, some 1,300 km from the South Pole. US Navy aircraft spotted it in 1958, and climbers first reached the top in 1966. From above the range looks like a spine of dark rock and ice breaking out of the ice sheet, with glaciers flowing away on both sides.","w":"A long, dark rock-and-ice ridge rising sharply above the flat ice sheet in the Ellsworth Mountains.","f":["It is named after Carl Vinson, a US congressman who backed Antarctic exploration.","An American expedition made the first ascent in December 1966.","Climbers fly in to a blue-ice runway at Union Glacier to start their approach."],"q":{"q":"In which mountains does Vinson Massif rise?","a":"Ellsworth Mountains","x":["Transantarctic Mountains","Prince Charles Mountains"]}},"Q1537198":{"t":"Twin summits of ice above Ladakh's Suru valley","s":"Nun and its twin, Kun, form the Nun Kun massif, a knot of ice in the western Himalaya of Ladakh. Nun is the higher of the pair at 7,135 m, only about four kilometres below you. Between the two tops lies a snowy plateau some 4 km long, and from above it looks like a white saddle slung between two peaks. Glaciers pour off both sides toward the Suru valley.","w":"Two white summits joined by a snowy plateau, with long glaciers running down to a green valley at their foot.","f":["Nun was first climbed in 1953 by Claude Kogan and Pierre Vittoz of a French–Swiss expedition.","Kun, the lower twin, is a little over 7,000 m high.","The Parkachik Glacier flows off the massif almost down to the road in the Suru valley."],"q":{"q":"What is the name of Nun's twin summit?","a":"Kun","x":["Stok Kangri","Kang Yatse"]}},"Q811064":{"t":"The crown of the Karakoram's jagged western wall","s":"Batura Sar tops a long, serrated wall of ice in the far west of the Karakoram, the Batura Muztagh. At 7,795 m it is the highest point of that wall, yet it is little known outside climbing circles. Below it lies one of the longest glaciers outside the polar regions, the Batura: a grey-and-white river of ice that ends right beside the Karakoram Highway in the Hunza valley.","w":"A long, serrated ice ridge north-west of the Hunza valley, with a broad glacier sliding east toward the river.","f":["The Batura Glacier is about 57 km long.","Muztagh means 'ice mountain' in Turkic languages.","The Karakoram Highway, one of the highest paved roads in the world, passes the glacier's snout."],"q":{"q":"Which famous road passes the snout of the Batura Glacier?","a":"Karakoram Highway","x":["Pamir Highway","Grand Trunk Road"]}},"Q6654097":{"t":"The Kunlun's highest peak, lost in a cold desert","s":"This is one of the emptiest corners of Asia. Liushi Shan, also called the Kunlun Goddess, is the highest peak of the Kunlun Mountains at 7,167 m, rising from bleak high desert on the border of Tibet and Xinjiang. From above, look for a cluster of white ice domes and glaciers in a sea of brown, wind-scoured ridges.","w":"A cluster of white ice domes on the northern edge of the Tibetan Plateau, surrounded by bare brown ridges.","f":["A Japanese university team made the first ascent in 1986.","In Chinese it is also known as Kunlun Nüshen Feng, 'Kunlun Goddess Peak'.","The Keriya River rises in this part of the Kunlun, flows north and vanishes into the Taklamakan Desert."],"q":{"q":"Liushi Shan is the highest peak of which mountain system?","a":"Kunlun Mountains","x":["Tian Shan","Karakoram"]}},"Q1050238":{"t":"Where the Himalaya ends and a great river turns","s":"Namcha Barwa marks the eastern end of the Himalaya, beside one of the strangest river bends on Earth: the Yarlung Tsangpo, flowing east across Tibet, swings around the mountain and plunges south through a gorge far deeper than the Grand Canyon. The peak is 7,782 m high, a steep white pyramid above dark, forested valleys, often wrapped in monsoon cloud.","w":"A steep white peak where the brown Tibetan plateau gives way to green, cloudy gorges; a river loops around its foot.","f":["It was the highest unclimbed peak in the world until a Chinese–Japanese team reached the top in 1992.","Gyala Peri, 7,294 m, rises only about 20 km away on the other side of the gorge.","Downstream from the gorge, the Yarlung Tsangpo becomes the Brahmaputra."],"q":{"q":"Which river bends sharply around Namcha Barwa?","a":"Yarlung Tsangpo","x":["Salween","Mekong"]}},"Q26794321":{"t":"A goddess inside a fortress of peaks","s":"Nanda Devi rises inside a natural fortress: a ring of mountains, broken only by a narrow gorge, encloses the high glacial basin known as the Sanctuary. The double-topped peak, 7,817 m, is the highest mountain lying entirely within India and is revered as the goddess Nanda. From above, look for the ring itself — a near-circle of white summits around a bowl of ice.","w":"A double-topped white peak inside a near-circle of snowy summits, in the Himalaya west of the Nepal border.","f":["Bill Tilman and Noel Odell made the first ascent in 1936.","Eric Shipton and Tilman were the first to force a way up the Rishi Ganga gorge into the Sanctuary, in 1934.","Nanda Devi National Park and the nearby Valley of Flowers form a UNESCO World Heritage site."],"q":{"q":"What is the ring-walled basin around Nanda Devi called?","a":"The Sanctuary","x":["The Amphitheatre","The Citadel"]}},"Q41413":{"t":"The Pamirs' high point, a peak of three names","s":"For decades this was the highest point of the Soviet Union, first as Stalin Peak, then as Communism Peak. Since 1998 it has carried the name of Ismoil Somoni, a Samanid ruler of the 9th and 10th centuries. The summit, 7,495 m, crowns a massive white wall in the heart of the Pamirs, close to the Fedchenko Glacier, one of the great ice rivers of Asia.","w":"A massive white peak at a junction of ridges, west of the long, straight Fedchenko Glacier in the high Pamirs.","f":["Yevgeny Abalakov made the first ascent in 1933.","The Fedchenko Glacier nearby is about 77 km long, the longest outside the polar regions.","Tajikistan's currency, the somoni, is named after the same ruler."],"q":{"q":"What was the peak called from 1962 to 1998?","a":"Communism Peak","x":["Lenin Peak","Stalin Peak"]}},"Q1537157":{"t":"The easternmost 7,000-metre giant, above Sichuan","s":"Gongga, also known as Minya Konka, stands where the Tibetan highlands drop toward the Sichuan Basin. At 7,556 m it is the highest peak in Sichuan and the easternmost 7,000-metre summit on Earth. Look for a sharp white pyramid ringed by glaciers, with the deep Dadu River valley to the east — more than six kilometres of relief in a short distance.","w":"A sharp white pyramid with a skirt of glaciers, west of the deep Dadu River gorge in western Sichuan.","f":["An American team made the first ascent in 1932.","The Hailuogou Glacier on its eastern side reaches down into forest below 3,000 m.","Gongga is the highest point of the Daxue Shan, the 'Great Snowy Mountains'."],"q":{"q":"Which glacier descends from Gongga's east side into forest?","a":"Hailuogou","x":["Baltoro","Rongbuk"]}},"Q6362901":{"t":"A cloud-wrapped giant of the eastern Himalaya","s":"East of Bhutan the Himalaya grows wilder and wetter, and Kangto is one of its tallest summits, just over 7,000 m. It sits on the main crest, where the dry Tibetan uplands meet steep valleys draining south toward the Brahmaputra plains of Assam. Thick forest climbs its lower flanks, and monsoon cloud hides the peak for much of the summer, so a clear view is a rare prize.","w":"A white summit on the Himalayan crest north of the Brahmaputra plains, between dry brown highlands and forested valleys.","f":["Kangto remained unclimbed for decades; the first recorded ascent came only in 2025.","Its southern valleys drain toward the Kameng River, a tributary of the Brahmaputra.","The eastern Himalaya receives far more monsoon rain than the ranges around Everest."],"q":{"q":"Toward which great river do Kangto's southern valleys drain?","a":"Brahmaputra","x":["Ganges","Irrawaddy"]}},"Q1109058":{"t":"A mountain god guarding a turquoise lake","s":"Nyenchen Tanglha is the highest peak of a long range running across central Tibet north-west of Lhasa. Look for its white summit, 7,162 m, just south-east of Namtso, a huge salt lake of startling turquoise. In Tibetan tradition the mountain is a protector deity, and he and the lake are often spoken of as a couple.","w":"A white ridge just south-east of a large turquoise lake, Namtso, on the brown plateau north-west of Lhasa.","f":["Namtso, at its foot, lies about 4,700 m above sea level.","The lake covers nearly 2,000 km², one of the largest in Tibet.","The railway and highway from Qinghai to Lhasa run along the range's southern foot."],"q":{"q":"Which lake lies at the foot of Nyenchen Tanglha?","a":"Namtso","x":["Manasarovar","Yamdrok"]}},"Q1560829":{"t":"The 'great ice mountain' of Tibet's empty north","s":"Ulugh Muztagh means 'great ice mountain' in Uyghur, and it rules one of the loneliest places on Earth: the high, cold uplands on the border of Tibet and Xinjiang, far from any town. The summit, 6,973 m, crowns a compact knot of ice-covered ridges. Around it the ground is pale brown desert, dotted with salt lakes that flash white and blue.","w":"A compact cluster of white summits and glaciers rising from pale brown desert and scattered salt lakes.","f":["After his journey of 1895, the explorer George Littledale guessed it was over 7,700 m high.","A Chinese–American expedition made the first ascent in 1985 and measured its true height.","Its meltwater drains into closed basins and never reaches the sea."],"q":{"q":"What does the name Ulugh Muztagh mean?","a":"Great ice mountain","x":["White goddess","Father of rivers"]}},"Q229107":{"t":"A sacred rock pyramid that no one has climbed","s":"Kailash looks almost designed: a lone, four-sided dome of dark rock streaked with snow, standing apart in the far west of Tibet. It is sacred to Hindus, Buddhists, Jains and followers of Bon, and climbing it is not permitted. Pilgrims instead walk around it, a circuit of about 52 km. Just to the south, two big lakes lie side by side: Manasarovar and Rakshastal.","w":"A lone snow-streaked rock dome north of two large lakes lying side by side on the brown plateau.","f":["The Indus, Sutlej, Brahmaputra and Karnali all rise within about 100 km of it.","Lake Manasarovar, at about 4,590 m, is fresh, while its neighbour Rakshastal is salty.","The pilgrim circuit crosses a pass at about 5,600 m."],"q":{"q":"Which sacred freshwater lake lies just south of Mount Kailash?","a":"Manasarovar","x":["Namtso","Yamdrok"]}},"Q207132":{"t":"King of the Hindu Kush, above the Chitral valley","s":"Tirich Mir is the highest mountain of the Hindu Kush, 7,708 m, and it rules the valley of Chitral in northern Pakistan. From cruise height it looks like a great snowy tent with long ridges, standing north of the green thread of the Chitral river. Beyond it the land runs north toward Afghanistan's Wakhan Corridor, a narrow strip squeezed between high ranges.","w":"A massive snowy peak with long ridges, north of the town of Chitral and close to the Afghan border.","f":["A Norwegian expedition made the first ascent in 1950.","The Hindu Kush stretches about 800 km from northern Pakistan into central Afghanistan.","The town of Chitral lies about 40 km to its south."],"q":{"q":"Tirich Mir is the highest peak of which range?","a":"Hindu Kush","x":["Karakoram","Pamir"]}},"Q332762":{"t":"The northernmost 7,000-metre peak on Earth","s":"Jengish Chokusu, long known as Victory Peak, is the highest point of the Tian Shan, 7,439 m, on the border of Kyrgyzstan and China. No mountain over 7,000 m stands farther north. Look for a long, heavy ridge of ice rather than a spike; just to its north rises the sharper pyramid of Khan Tengri, whose marble summit glows pink at sunset.","w":"A long, heavy white ridge on the Kyrgyz–Chinese border, with the sharp pyramid of Khan Tengri about 16 km north.","f":["It was recognised as the Tian Shan's highest peak only in the 1940s; Khan Tengri had long been thought higher.","The South Inylchek Glacier at its foot is about 60 km long.","Jengish Chokusu is Kyrgyz for 'Victory Peak'."],"q":{"q":"Which sharp peak rises just north of Jengish Chokusu?","a":"Khan Tengri","x":["Lenin Peak","Muztagh Ata"]}},"Q271700":{"t":"Afghanistan's highest summit, on the Hindu Kush crest","s":"Noshaq is the highest point of Afghanistan and the second-highest mountain of the Hindu Kush, 7,492 m, standing right on the border with Pakistan. From above it shows as a broad white massif with several summits in a row, a short way north of Tirich Mir. Its northern glaciers drain into the Wakhan valley, where the rivers gather that later form the Amu Darya.","w":"A broad white massif with a row of summits on the Afghan–Pakistani border, just north of Tirich Mir.","f":["A Japanese expedition made the first ascent in 1960.","It stands just south of the Wakhan Corridor, a thin arm of Afghanistan reaching east to China."],"q":{"q":"Noshaq is the highest point of which country?","a":"Afghanistan","x":["Tajikistan","Iran"]}},"Q8536063":{"t":"The top of the Transhimalaya, Tibet's second wall","s":"North of the great Himalayan wall runs a second, lesser-known chain: the Transhimalaya, or Gangdise. Its highest summit is Lunpo Gangri, 7,095 m, an ice-capped massif in south-western Tibet. Look for a line of white peaks rising from brown uplands, with the upper Yarlung Tsangpo — the future Brahmaputra — winding through a broad valley to the south.","w":"An ice-capped massif north of the broad Yarlung Tsangpo valley, part of a white chain parallel to the Himalaya.","f":["Mount Kailash belongs to the same Gangdise range, about 300 km to the west.","The Swedish explorer Sven Hedin named the Transhimalaya after travelling through it in 1906–1908."],"q":{"q":"Which explorer gave the Transhimalaya its name?","a":"Sven Hedin","x":["Francis Younghusband","Nikolai Przhevalsky"]}},"ne-pk-1159105819":{"t":"The ice giant above the Karakoram Highway","s":"Kongur Tagh, 7,649 m, is a massive dome of ice at the western edge of the Tarim Basin, not far from Kashgar. Its neighbour to the south, Muztagh Ata, is rounder and more famous, but Kongur is higher. Between the two the Karakoram Highway runs past Karakul Lake, a blue mirror at about 3,600 m that reflects both peaks on calm days.","w":"A huge ice massif east of the Karakoram Highway, with the rounder Muztagh Ata to the south and a small blue lake between.","f":["A British team including Chris Bonington made the first ascent in 1981.","Kashgar, the old Silk Road oasis, lies roughly 100 km to the north-east.","Its summit rises more than 6,000 m above the floor of the Tarim Basin."],"q":{"q":"Which famous peak stands just south of Kongur Tagh?","a":"Muztagh Ata","x":["Khan Tengri","Tirich Mir"]}},"Q1322521":{"t":"The northern sentinel of Tibet's great gorge","s":"Gyala Peri and Namcha Barwa face each other across the Yarlung Tsangpo, only about 20 km apart. Between them the river squeezes through a gorge of extraordinary depth before turning south toward India. Gyala Peri, 7,294 m, is the northern guard: a white dome above slopes so wet and steep that dense forest climbs far up its sides.","w":"A white dome on the north side of a tight river bend, facing the higher Namcha Barwa across a dark gorge.","f":["Between Gyala Peri and Namcha Barwa the gorge is more than 5,000 m deep.","The botanist Frank Kingdon-Ward explored the gorge in 1924 in search of new plants."],"q":{"q":"Which peak faces Gyala Peri across the Yarlung Tsangpo gorge?","a":"Namcha Barwa","x":["Kangchenjunga","Nyenchen Tanglha"]}},"Q219986":{"t":"The highest mountain no one has climbed","s":"Gangkhar Puensum, 7,570 m, is the highest point of Bhutan and very likely the highest unclimbed mountain on Earth. Attempts in the 1980s failed, and since 1994 Bhutan has banned climbing peaks over 6,000 m out of respect for the belief that mountains are home to spirits. Look for a broad white summit on the northern border, above deep, forested valleys.","w":"A broad white summit on Bhutan's northern border with Tibet, above deep, green, forested valleys.","f":["Its name is usually translated as 'White Peak of the Three Spiritual Brothers'.","Since 2003 Bhutan has banned mountaineering altogether.","In 1999 a Japanese team climbed a neighbouring summit, Liankang Kangri, from the Tibetan side."],"q":{"q":"Since which year has Bhutan banned climbing peaks over 6,000 m?","a":"1994","x":["1974","2010"]}},"Q1286":{"t":"A white crescent across the heart of Europe","s":"From cruise height the Alps look like crumpled white paper laid across Europe: sharp ridges, dark valleys and glaciers poured into the gaps. The arc runs about 1,200 km, from the Mediterranean near Nice almost to Vienna. Even Mont Blanc, the highest summit at about 4,800 m, sits some six kilometres below you.","w":"White ridges broken by deep valleys, with long lakes such as Garda, Como and Geneva at their feet.","f":["The Aletsch Glacier in Switzerland is the longest in the Alps: about 20 km of ice.","Around 14 million people live in the Alps.","The Gotthard Base Tunnel, opened in 2016, runs 57 km beneath the Alps — the longest railway tunnel in the world."],"q":{"q":"Which is the longest glacier in the Alps?","a":"The Aletsch Glacier","x":["The Mer de Glace","The Pasterze"]}},"Q5451":{"t":"The highest wall on Earth, almost at your altitude","s":"No other mountains come this close to your wing. The Himalayas rise from the green plains of India in steps — foothills, forested ridges, then a white wall — and Everest, at 8,849 m, reaches to within about two kilometres of cruise height. The range is still growing as India keeps pushing north into Asia.","w":"A white wall of peaks between the green plains of India and the brown, high Tibetan Plateau.","f":["Himalaya means “abode of snow” in Sanskrit.","Ten of the world’s fourteen peaks above 8,000 m are in the Himalayas; the other four are in the Karakoram.","Marine fossils are found near Everest’s summit: its top layers are limestone laid down on an ancient sea floor."],"q":{"q":"What does the Sanskrit name “Himalaya” mean?","a":"Abode of snow","x":["Roof of the world","Mountain of the gods"]}},"Q5456":{"t":"A 7,000-km spine down the whole of South America","s":"Seven thousand kilometres long, the Andes change character as you fly along them: glaciers and fjords in Patagonia, a wall of snow above Santiago, and in Bolivia a high plateau, the Altiplano, where Salar de Uyuni lies like a sheet of white paper. No mountain chain on any continent is longer.","w":"Snowy peaks and volcanic cones on one side, desert or rainforest on the other; in Bolivia, dazzling white salt flats.","f":["Aconcagua, at 6,961 m, is the highest mountain outside Asia.","Salar de Uyuni is so flat that it is used to calibrate satellite altimeters.","La Paz, in the Bolivian Andes at around 3,600 m, is the highest seat of government in the world."],"q":{"q":"Which is the highest peak of the Andes?","a":"Aconcagua","x":["Chimborazo","Huascarán"]}},"Q5463":{"t":"Where North America’s rivers choose their ocean","s":"Somewhere along these ridges runs the Continental Divide: rain on one side flows to the Pacific, on the other towards the Atlantic or the Arctic. From the air the Rockies are range after range of grey-brown peaks, dark with pine forest and streaked with snow well into summer. They stretch some 4,800 km, from northern British Columbia to New Mexico.","w":"Parallel ridges of grey rock and dark forest, snowfields on the peaks, and flat sagebrush basins between the ranges.","f":["Mount Elbert in Colorado, 4,401 m, is the highest peak of the Rockies.","Yellowstone, in the Wyoming Rockies, holds about half of the world’s active geysers.","By mountain standards the Rockies are young: their main uplift began around 80 million years ago."],"q":{"q":"Which is the highest peak of the Rocky Mountains?","a":"Mount Elbert","x":["Pikes Peak","Longs Peak"]}},"Q42070":{"t":"Stony, cold and vast — a desert of dinosaur eggs","s":"Expect beige, grey and rust rather than golden dunes: most of the Gobi is bare rock and gravel, scored by dry riverbeds. It is a cold desert, lying high on a plateau across Mongolia and northern China, with winters that can drop below −30 °C. Sand seas do exist, in patches, and from above they stand out as golden ripples in the grey.","w":"Flat grey-brown gravel plains with dry riverbeds, patches of golden dunes and white salt pans.","f":["In 1923 explorers at the Gobi’s Flaming Cliffs found nests of eggs that were the first to be recognised as dinosaur eggs.","In Badain Jaran, in the Chinese Gobi, dunes rise to about 500 m, with small lakes lying between them.","Wild Bactrian camels survive in the Gobi; they are a separate species, not runaway domestic camels."],"q":{"q":"What covers most of the Gobi’s surface?","a":"Bare rock and gravel","x":["Sand dunes","Salt marsh"]}},"Q6583":{"t":"Almost the size of the USA — and once green","s":"You can fly for hours and still be over the Sahara. At about 9 million km² it is the largest hot desert on Earth, painted in every shade from white salt to black basalt and orange sand. Dunes cover only part of it; the rest is stony plateaus, gravel plains and dark volcanic mountains.","w":"Endless tan and orange ground: look for rippled dune fields, dark volcanic massifs and pale dry lakebeds.","f":["Until about 5,000 years ago much of the Sahara was savanna with lakes; rock paintings show giraffes and cattle.","Saharan dust blown across the Atlantic helps fertilise the Amazon rainforest.","“Sahara” simply comes from the Arabic word for “desert”, so “Sahara Desert” says the same thing twice."],"q":{"q":"What does the name “Sahara” mean in Arabic?","a":"Desert","x":["Sand","Sun"]}},"Q5474":{"t":"Celestial Mountains around a lake that never freezes","s":"Tian Shan means “Celestial Mountains” in Chinese, and from above the name fits: snowy ranges roll for some 2,500 km across Central Asia. In their Kyrgyz heart lies Issyk-Kul, a deep blue lake about 180 km long sitting at 1,600 m. Despite the altitude and harsh winters, it never freezes — its water is slightly salty.","w":"Rows of snowy ridges running east–west; north of them, the long blue oval of Issyk-Kul.","f":["Jengish Chokusu, also called Victory Peak, is the highest summit of the Tian Shan at 7,439 m.","Issyk-Kul is one of the deepest lakes in the world, reaching about 670 m.","Khan Tengri glows pinkish-red at sunset because its summit pyramid is made of marble."],"q":{"q":"Which Tian Shan lake never freezes despite its altitude?","a":"Issyk-Kul","x":["Song-Kul","Balkhash"]}},"Q35600":{"t":"A modest ridge that divides two continents","s":"Don’t expect peaks: the Urals are so old and worn down that from cruise height they look like low, forested wrinkles. Yet this line, running some 2,500 km from the Arctic to the steppes of Kazakhstan, is where Europe traditionally ends and Asia begins. Around Yekaterinburg the mountains are so low that the Trans-Siberian crosses them almost unnoticed.","w":"A band of dark taiga and low ridges running north–south; east of Yekaterinburg and Chelyabinsk, a scatter of lakes.","f":["The Urals rose around 300 million years ago, when Europe and Siberia collided — long before the Alps existed.","The highest peak, Narodnaya, reaches only 1,895 m.","The malachite columns of St Isaac’s Cathedral in St Petersburg are faced with stone from the Urals."],"q":{"q":"Which is the highest peak of the Urals?","a":"Narodnaya","x":["Yamantau","Manaraga"]}},"Q5477":{"t":"A snowy wall between the Black Sea and the Caspian","s":"Between two seas rises a single, sharp-edged barrier: the Greater Caucasus runs about 1,200 km from the Black Sea to the Caspian, with a white crest along its spine. In the east, over Dagestan, it breaks into a maze of deep canyons and high plateaus. Elbrus, a dormant volcano with two summits, is usually counted as Europe’s highest peak at 5,642 m.","w":"A continuous line of snowy peaks running north-west to south-east between two seas.","f":["Dagestan means “land of mountains”, and more than 30 languages are spoken in its valleys.","Elbrus’s two summits stand about 1.5 km apart and differ in height by only about 20 m.","Kazbek, 5,054 m, is also an old volcano; its white cone rises above the Darial Gorge."],"q":{"q":"Which peak is usually counted as Europe’s highest?","a":"Elbrus","x":["Mont Blanc","Kazbek"]}},"Q60140":{"t":"A former island continent that crashed into Asia","s":"The land below you was once an island continent. Tens of millions of years ago it drifted north across the ocean and rammed into Asia, crumpling the crust into the Himalayas — and it is still pushing, a few centimetres a year. From above, look for the flat Ganges plain in the north and the old, worn Deccan plateau in the south.","w":"Flat, intensely farmed plains in the north; brown plateaus, rivers and reservoirs further south; at night, countless clusters of lights.","f":["The Indian plate still moves north by about 5 cm a year.","The Deccan Traps, layers of lava up to 2 km thick, poured out about 66 million years ago.","Around a quarter of all people on Earth live on the subcontinent."],"q":{"q":"Which mountains rose when India collided with Asia?","a":"The Himalayas","x":["The Urals","The Zagros"]}},"Q31945":{"t":"The world’s largest peninsula, tilted towards the Gulf","s":"Seen from above, Arabia is a giant tilted slab: mountains rise along the Red Sea in the west, and the land slopes gently east to the Gulf. In between lie gravel plains, black lava fields and the dunes of the Empty Quarter. Saudi Arabia, which fills most of the peninsula, has no permanent rivers at all.","w":"Beige plains and orange dune seas, dark mountain ridges along the Red Sea coast and black lava fields inland.","f":["At more than 3 million km², Arabia is the largest peninsula on Earth.","Arabia is slowly pulling away from Africa: the Red Sea widens by about a centimetre a year.","The Empty Quarter in its south is the largest continuous sand desert on Earth."],"q":{"q":"Which is the largest peninsula in the world?","a":"The Arabian Peninsula","x":["The Indochina Peninsula","Scandinavia"]}},"Q27611":{"t":"A land bridge built by volcanoes","s":"Two oceans, one narrow strip of land: on a clear day you may see water on both sides of the plane. A chain of volcanoes runs along the Pacific side, many of them near-perfect cones, while the Caribbean coast is low, green and swampy. When this land bridge closed about 3 million years ago, animals began crossing between the Americas.","w":"A string of volcanic cones along the Pacific coast, with lakes such as Nicaragua and Atitlán shining between them.","f":["Lake Nicaragua is the largest lake in Central America, and bull sharks swim in it.","Tajumulco in Guatemala, 4,220 m, is the highest peak in Central America.","Guatemala alone has more than 30 volcanoes."],"q":{"q":"Which Central American lake is home to bull sharks?","a":"Lake Nicaragua","x":["Lake Atitlán","Lake Yojoa"]}},"Q185605":{"t":"Antarctica’s long finger pointing at South America","s":"Ice-capped mountains, glaciers spilling into the sea and a coast shattered into islands: the Antarctic Peninsula reaches some 1,300 km north towards Cape Horn. It is the mildest and most visited corner of the continent, and one of the fastest-warming places on Earth. Along its eastern side, vast ice shelves float on the Weddell Sea.","w":"Jagged, snow-covered peaks along a narrow spine, and glaciers flowing into a dark sea dotted with icebergs.","f":["In 2002 part of the Larsen Ice Shelf larger than Luxembourg broke apart in about five weeks.","The Drake Passage between the peninsula and Cape Horn is about 800 km wide.","Most tourists who reach Antarctica come here, on ships from Ushuaia."],"q":{"q":"Which sea lies east of the Antarctic Peninsula?","a":"The Weddell Sea","x":["The Ross Sea","The Amundsen Sea"]}},"Q319671":{"t":"A 3,500-km wall holding back the polar ice","s":"Dark rock peaks break through the white here, marking one of the longest mountain ranges on Earth. The Transantarctic Mountains cross the whole continent, splitting East Antarctica from West, and hold back the thick East Antarctic ice sheet like a dam. Glaciers squeeze through the gaps between the summits and flow down to the Ross Ice Shelf.","w":"Rows of dark, flat-topped peaks poking through the ice, with broad glaciers flowing between them.","f":["Both Shackleton and Scott reached the polar plateau by climbing the Beardmore Glacier, about 200 km long.","Bones of a crested dinosaur, Cryolophosaurus, were found high on Mount Kirkpatrick in this range.","Coal seams and fossil Glossopteris leaves found here show that Antarctica was once forested."],"q":{"q":"Which glacier did Shackleton and Scott climb to the polar plateau?","a":"The Beardmore Glacier","x":["The Axel Heiberg Glacier","The Lambert Glacier"]}},"Q192583":{"t":"Australia’s green seam between coast and outback","s":"This is less a wall than a long, worn backbone: the Great Dividing Range runs some 3,500 km down eastern Australia, and from the air you notice the change of colour more than the mountains. On the coast side lie green forest and river valleys; inland, the land fades to dry brown plains. Its western rivers flow away from the nearby sea, towards the Murray–Darling or the dry heart of the continent.","w":"Green, forested ridges near the east coast; inland of them, farmland and scrub turning to brown plains.","f":["Mount Kosciuszko, 2,228 m, is its highest point and the highest in mainland Australia.","The Blue Mountains near Sydney are part of the range; their blue haze comes from eucalyptus oil in the air."],"q":{"q":"Which is the highest peak of the Great Dividing Range?","a":"Mount Kosciuszko","x":["Mount Townsend","Mount Bogong"]}},"Q21195":{"t":"A peninsula still rising after the Ice Age","s":"From cruise height Scandinavia is a mosaic of dark forest and countless lakes, with mountains along the Norwegian side and deep fjords cutting into the Atlantic coast. All of it once lay under an ice sheet up to about 3 km thick. Freed of that weight, the land is still rebounding — in places by nearly a centimetre a year.","w":"Dark green forest speckled with hundreds of lakes; to the west, snowy mountains and fjords like narrow blue cracks.","f":["Sweden’s High Coast has risen about 285 m since the ice melted, the greatest such rebound known.","Sweden alone has close to 100,000 lakes.","At North Cape the midnight sun shines for more than two months each summer."],"q":{"q":"Why is the land of Scandinavia still rising?","a":"The weight of Ice Age ice has gone","x":["Volcanic activity below it","A collision of tectonic plates"]}},"Q27617163":{"t":"Between India and China, a land of ridges and rivers","s":"Its old name, Indochina, says it all: the land between India and China. From above you can read its structure — long mountain ridges fanning south from the Himalayas, with great rivers in the valleys between. The Mekong, Chao Phraya, Salween and Irrawaddy all run roughly north to south. In the dry season, smoke from burning fields often softens the view.","w":"Parallel green ridges running north–south, brown rivers winding between them, rice fields shining in the valleys.","f":["Cambodia’s Tonle Sap River reverses its flow every rainy season, and the lake it drains swells several times over.","The Mekong flows through or along six countries: China, Myanmar, Laos, Thailand, Cambodia and Vietnam.","Thailand and Vietnam are among the world’s largest rice exporters."],"q":{"q":"Which river reverses its flow every rainy season?","a":"The Tonle Sap","x":["The Chao Phraya","The Irrawaddy"]}},"Q12837":{"t":"A sun-baked tableland at the edge of Europe","s":"On the map Iberia is almost a square, and much of its interior is a lofty tableland, the Meseta, where ochre wheat fields and olive groves run to the horizon. Mountain ranges ring it and cross it, and the Pyrenees seal it off from the rest of Europe. At the southern tip, only about 14 km of sea separate it from Africa.","w":"Ochre and green plains broken by bare, rocky sierras, and reservoirs with branching, finger-like shapes.","f":["Madrid, at about 650 m, is one of the highest capital cities in Europe.","Spain has more than 1,000 large dams, one of the highest counts in the world.","Near Almería, plastic greenhouses cover so much land that they show up as a white patch from space."],"q":{"q":"How wide is the Strait of Gibraltar at its narrowest?","a":"About 14 km","x":["About 4 km","About 40 km"]}},"Q5513":{"t":"The deepest lake on Earth, a blue crescent in Siberia","s":"Look for a long blue crescent pressed between dark mountain ridges — that's Baikal, more than 600 km from end to end. Its deepest point is 1,642 m down, deeper than any other lake on Earth. In late winter the ice turns glassy and pale turquoise, split by cracks that run for kilometres.","w":"A narrow crescent of deep blue, or white ice in winter, running south-west to north-east between mountains.","f":["Baikal holds roughly a fifth of all the unfrozen fresh water on Earth's surface.","More than 300 rivers flow into it, but only one — the Angara — flows out.","It is home to the nerpa, a seal that lives nowhere else in the world."],"q":{"q":"Which river is the only one flowing out of Lake Baikal?","a":"The Angara","x":["The Selenga","The Yenisei"]}},"Q23883":{"t":"The lowest water on Earth, and it keeps sinking","s":"The shore of the Dead Sea is the lowest dry land on the planet, more than 430 m below sea level — and the water drops by roughly a metre a year. It is so salty that people float without trying. From above, the deep-blue northern basin gives way to pale geometric salt pans in the south.","w":"A slim blue lake in a deep desert trough east of Jerusalem, with turquoise and white salt ponds at its southern end.","f":["Its water is roughly ten times saltier than the ocean — too salty for fish.","The Jordan River is its main source, and nothing flows out: water leaves only by evaporation.","The southern basin is now mostly a patchwork of evaporation ponds for potash and salt."],"q":{"q":"Roughly how far below sea level is the Dead Sea's shore?","a":"More than 400 m","x":["About 100 m","About 250 m"]}},"Q788":{"t":"A frozen ocean where the ice itself drifts","s":"From a polar flight the Arctic Ocean looks like a cracked white plate: pack ice broken by dark lanes of open water called leads. It is the smallest and shallowest ocean, yet under the North Pole the sea is more than 4 km deep. In summer, pools of meltwater dot the ice with blue.","w":"Endless white pack ice laced with dark cracks; in summer, turquoise meltwater ponds and patches of open sea.","f":["There is no land at the North Pole — only drifting sea ice a few metres thick.","In 1893–1896 Nansen's ship Fram was deliberately frozen in and drifted with the ice across the Arctic.","Near the pole the sun stays up for about six months, then stays below the horizon for six."],"q":{"q":"What lies under the ice at the North Pole?","a":"Ocean more than 4 km deep","x":["A rocky island","A shallow shelf sea"]}},"Q7354":{"t":"The ocean that runs all the way around the world","s":"Few flights ever cross it, which makes this view rare. The Southern Ocean rings Antarctica with no land in its way, so its current circles the globe endlessly from west to east. Look for flat-topped icebergs — some are tens of kilometres long — and, in winter, a white apron of sea ice larger than the continent itself.","w":"Dark, wind-streaked water with scattered white icebergs; farther south, a solid rim of sea ice around Antarctica.","f":["The Antarctic Circumpolar Current carries more water than any other current on Earth.","Sailors named its stormy latitudes the Roaring Forties, the Furious Fifties and the Screaming Sixties.","Its northern boundary is usually drawn at 60° South."],"q":{"q":"In which direction does the Antarctic Circumpolar Current flow?","a":"West to east","x":["East to west","South to north"]}},"Q98":{"t":"Bigger than all the world's land put together","s":"Hours of blue with barely a speck of land: this is the Pacific, covering about a third of the planet. Its deepest point, the Challenger Deep, lies nearly 11 km down — roughly as far below the surface as you are above it. Magellan called it the “peaceful sea” after the calm water he met in 1520.","w":"Deep navy water to every horizon, broken only by rows of cloud and, now and then, the pale ring of an atoll.","f":["The Pacific is larger than all the land on Earth combined.","Volcanoes and earthquake zones rim much of it — the so-called Ring of Fire.","Across the tropics it stretches nearly halfway around the globe."],"q":{"q":"Who named the Pacific after its calm, “peaceful” water?","a":"Ferdinand Magellan","x":["James Cook","Vasco da Gama"]}},"Q1239":{"t":"The warm ocean that powers the monsoon","s":"Sun glint on the Indian Ocean can blaze like hammered metal for minutes on end. This is the warmest of the oceans, and it is closed off in the north by Asia rather than open to the Arctic. That shape drives the monsoon: each summer the heating continent pulls in moist ocean air, bringing the rains to South Asia.","w":"Open blue ocean with a bright path of sun glint; in the tropics, rows of puffy cumulus over the water.","f":["It covers about a fifth of the world's ocean area.","The Maldives, in its north, are some 1,200 small coral islands grouped into 26 atolls.","It is the only ocean named after a country — India."],"q":{"q":"How does the Indian Ocean rank in size among the oceans?","a":"Third largest","x":["Second largest","Fourth largest"]}},"Q97":{"t":"An ocean still growing, a few centimetres a year","s":"Down the middle of the Atlantic, hidden under kilometres of water, runs the Mid-Atlantic Ridge — a volcanic seam where the sea floor is pulling apart. The ocean grows wider by a few centimetres every year, roughly as fast as your fingernails. At the surface, the sun's glint picks out the long, even lines of swell.","w":"Open ocean ribbed with long swell, under drifting cloud fields; the sun's reflection shows the texture of the waves.","f":["Iceland and the Azores are parts of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge that rise above the waves.","The North Atlantic is the busiest stretch of oceanic airspace in the world.","Its name comes from Atlas, the Titan of Greek myth who held up the sky."],"q":{"q":"Which island sits right on the Mid-Atlantic Ridge?","a":"Iceland","x":["Ireland","Madeira"]}},"Q4918":{"t":"The sea in the middle of the land","s":"Its famous deep blue comes from poverty, not richness: the water carries few nutrients and little plankton, so sunlight sinks deep. The Mediterranean joins the Atlantic only through the 14-km gap at Gibraltar. About six million years ago its link to the ocean closed and the sea almost dried out, leaving thick salt layers still buried under its floor.","w":"Intense cobalt-blue water between Europe and Africa, often with a sharp silver sun glint and scattered rocky islands.","f":["Its name comes from the Latin for “in the middle of the land”.","Tides across most of it are small, usually well under half a metre.","More water evaporates than rivers bring in, so it is saltier than the open Atlantic."],"q":{"q":"About how wide is the Strait of Gibraltar at its narrowest?","a":"About 14 km","x":["About 3 km","About 50 km"]}},"Q5484":{"t":"A “sea” that is really the world's largest lake","s":"Call it a sea, but it is the largest body of water on Earth with no outlet to the ocean — bigger than Germany. Its two ends are nothing alike: the shallow north, fed by the Volga, often freezes in winter, while the south plunges past 1,000 m. On the eastern shore look for Kara-Bogaz-Gol, a vivid turquoise lagoon.","w":"A huge inland sea with a pale, silty north and deep blue south; on its east side, the round turquoise Kara-Bogaz-Gol.","f":["Its surface lies about 28 m below the level of the world ocean.","The Volga brings in roughly four-fifths of all the river water the Caspian receives.","For centuries it has been the world's main source of sturgeon and black caviar."],"q":{"q":"Which river supplies most of the Caspian Sea's water?","a":"The Volga","x":["The Ural","The Kura"]}},"Q166":{"t":"A turquoise surface over an oxygen-free deep","s":"In late spring the Black Sea can turn milky turquoise, as blooms of chalk-plated plankton swirl across it. The depths are another world: below about 200 m there is no oxygen at all, and ancient wooden ships lie there almost untouched. It reaches the Mediterranean only through the narrow Bosphorus and Dardanelles.","w":"A broad oval sea between Turkey, the Balkans and the Caucasus; in early summer, pale turquoise swirls on dark water.","f":["Nearly 90% of its water holds no oxygen, only dissolved hydrogen sulphide.","In 2018 an intact Greek trading ship about 2,400 years old was found on its floor.","The Danube reaches it through a vast delta, one of Europe's largest wetlands."],"q":{"q":"What is missing from the Black Sea's deep water?","a":"Oxygen","x":["Salt","Nitrogen"]}},"Q159183":{"t":"A warm, deep nursery for typhoons","s":"This is some of the warmest open water on the planet, and it has a stormy talent: many of the Pacific's fiercest typhoons are born here. The sea is fenced by island chains — the Philippines, Taiwan, the Ryukyus and Japan to the west and north, the Marianas to the east. Far below the surface, trenches cut more than 10 km deep.","w":"Deep indigo ocean dotted with towering cumulus; island arcs mark its edges — Luzon and Taiwan to the west, the Marianas to the east.","f":["The warm Kuroshio current sets off along its western edge and runs north past Japan.","The Philippine Trench, along its western side, drops to more than 10 km.","The sea sits on a tectonic plate of its own, the Philippine Sea Plate."],"q":{"q":"Which warm current runs north along the Philippine Sea's western edge?","a":"The Kuroshio","x":["The Oyashio","The Humboldt Current"]}},"Q82931":{"t":"Home of the Great Barrier Reef","s":"The Coral Sea earns its name on its western side, where the Great Barrier Reef runs for about 2,300 km along Australia's coast. From above, the reefs look like turquoise brushstrokes and pale rings scattered on deep blue, with white surf on their seaward edges. Farther east the water deepens to a dark indigo.","w":"Turquoise reef patches and white surf lines off north-east Australia, fading into deep open blue to the east.","f":["The Great Barrier Reef is made of nearly 3,000 separate reefs and hundreds of islands.","It is the largest structure built by living things, and it can be seen from orbit.","The reef has been a UNESCO World Heritage site since 1981."],"q":{"q":"About how long is the Great Barrier Reef?","a":"About 2,300 km","x":["About 600 km","About 5,000 km"]}},"Q33254":{"t":"“The Ditch” between Australia and New Zealand","s":"Australians and New Zealanders call it “the Ditch”, but it is roughly 2,000 km across — about three hours in the air. The Tasman is known for rough water, stirred up by westerly winds from the Southern Ocean. Its name honours Abel Tasman, the Dutch navigator who crossed it in 1642 and sighted New Zealand.","w":"Open grey-blue water between two coastlines, often streaked with whitecaps and bands of cloud moving in from the west.","f":["Lord Howe Island, in its western part, is a UNESCO World Heritage site.","Nearby Ball's Pyramid, a volcanic stack 562 m tall, rises straight out of the sea.","On its floor lies the Lord Howe Rise, part of Zealandia, a continent that is mostly underwater."],"q":{"q":"What do Australians and New Zealanders nickname the Tasman Sea?","a":"The Ditch","x":["The Puddle","The Gap"]}},"Q38684":{"t":"The world's largest bay, fed by the Ganges","s":"At the head of the bay, the Ganges and Brahmaputra pour out so much silt that the sea turns café-au-lait brown far from shore. This is the largest bay on Earth, and at its top lies the world's biggest delta, fringed by the tangled green of the Sundarbans mangroves. Farther south the silt settles and the water turns deep blue.","w":"A vast triangle of sea between India and Myanmar; near Bangladesh, brown silt plumes swirl into the blue.","f":["The Sundarbans on its northern shore are the largest mangrove forest in the world.","Beneath the bay lies the Bengal Fan, a pile of river sediment some 3,000 km long.","River water makes its surface noticeably less salty than the open ocean."],"q":{"q":"Which rivers build the huge delta at the head of the Bay of Bengal?","a":"Ganges and Brahmaputra","x":["Indus and Narmada","Irrawaddy and Salween"]}},"Q37660":{"t":"One of the busiest seaways on the planet","s":"In clear weather, look for faint white scratches on the blue — the wakes of ships. A huge share of the world's sea trade crosses this water between the Strait of Malacca, China, Japan and Korea. Scattered across the middle are low coral reefs and atolls, which show as turquoise rings where deep water suddenly gives way to shallows.","w":"Wide blue sea between Vietnam, the Philippines and southern China, with ship wakes and small turquoise reef rings.","f":["Roughly a third of all global shipping passes through it.","The Mekong and the Pearl River both empty into it — the latter right beside Hong Kong.","Its deepest basin goes down more than 5,000 m."],"q":{"q":"Which great river's delta opens onto the South China Sea?","a":"The Mekong","x":["The Irrawaddy","The Ganges"]}},"Q27092":{"t":"Cloud streets by day, squid-boat lights by night","s":"On a winter flight, look for neat parallel lines of cloud streaming across the water from the Asian mainland: icy monsoon winds pick up moisture here and dump it as deep snow on Japan's western coast. At night the sea can sparkle with points of light — fishing boats using bright lamps to lure squid.","w":"An enclosed sea between Japan and the Asian mainland; in winter, rows of cloud stripes blowing towards Japan.","f":["It connects to the open ocean only through a handful of narrow straits.","Its deepest basin reaches about 3,700 m.","In winter, sea ice forms in its far north, near the Tatar Strait."],"q":{"q":"What makes the Sea of Japan glitter at night?","a":"Lamps on squid-fishing boats","x":["Offshore oil platforms","Floating solar farms"]}},"Q58705":{"t":"Monsoon sea between Arabia and India","s":"Every summer the south-west monsoon sweeps across the Arabian Sea, stirring cold, nutrient-rich water to the surface off Oman — plankton blooms can then paint the sea green. For thousands of years sailors rode these seasonal winds between Arabia and India in wooden dhows. In the north, the Indus fans out into a muddy delta.","w":"A wide sea between the Arabian Peninsula and India; in summer, greenish plankton swirls may show off Oman.","f":["The Indus reaches the sea through a broad delta on Pakistan's coast.","Below the surface lies one of the largest oxygen-poor zones in any ocean.","A 1st-century Greek sailing guide, the Periplus of the Erythraean Sea, describes trade across it."],"q":{"q":"Which great river flows into the Arabian Sea?","a":"The Indus","x":["The Ganges","The Brahmaputra"]}},"Q131274":{"t":"Where the Mackenzie meets the Arctic ice","s":"For most of the year the Beaufort Sea is a white plain of drifting ice, turned slowly clockwise by a vast ocean eddy. In summer the ice pulls back from the coast, and the Mackenzie River spills a brown plume of silt from a delta braided with countless lakes. Look for that tea-coloured stain against the ice.","w":"Sea ice north of Alaska and Canada; in summer, a brown river plume and a lake-speckled delta on the Canadian shore.","f":["It is named after Admiral Francis Beaufort, who devised the wind-force scale.","The Mackenzie, Canada's longest river, builds a huge delta on its shore.","Bowhead whales pass through it every year on their seasonal migration."],"q":{"q":"The Beaufort Sea's namesake is known for devising what?","a":"A wind-force scale","x":["A map projection","A tide table"]}},"Q1247":{"t":"Turquoise shallows beside deep blue water","s":"The famous colours are real: over sandbanks and reefs the Caribbean glows turquoise and jade, with white sand showing through. Just off those shallows the floor drops away to deep blue — in the Cayman Trough it lies about 7,700 m down. The sea is fenced in by the arc of the Antilles, a chain of islands curving from Cuba almost to Venezuela.","w":"Turquoise and emerald shallows around islands, white sand bars, and a sharp edge where the water turns deep blue.","f":["It takes its name from the Caribs, a people of the Lesser Antilles.","The Mesoamerican Barrier Reef off Belize and Mexico is the largest reef system in the Americas.","The Panama Canal links it to the Pacific across a narrow isthmus."],"q":{"q":"Which people is the Caribbean Sea named after?","a":"The Caribs","x":["The Maya","The Taíno"]}},"Q12630":{"t":"A warm basin with a bird's-foot delta","s":"Where the Mississippi reaches the Gulf, its delta splays out like a bird's foot, trailing brown silt into the blue. The Gulf is a warm, nearly round basin, and at night hundreds of offshore oil and gas platforms scatter lights across it. Buried off Yucatán lies the crater of the asteroid that ended the age of the dinosaurs.","w":"A broad, rounded gulf; off Louisiana, the Mississippi's fingered delta and brown plume; at night, scattered platform lights offshore.","f":["The Chicxulub crater, about 180 km across, lies partly beneath its floor.","Padre Island, off the Texas coast, is the longest barrier island in the world.","The warm Loop Current flows in between Yucatán and Cuba and leaves past Florida."],"q":{"q":"What shape is the Mississippi Delta famous for?","a":"A bird's foot","x":["A perfect fan","A crescent"]}},"Q184189":{"t":"Iceberg highway between Greenland and Labrador","s":"Many flights between Europe and North America cross this cold sea between Greenland and Labrador. In spring and early summer, look for white specks on dark water: icebergs calved from Greenland's glaciers, riding the Labrador Current south towards Newfoundland. In winter, surface water here grows so cold and dense that it sinks deep, helping drive the ocean's slow global circulation.","w":"Dark open water between Greenland's southern tip and the Labrador coast; in spring, scattered white icebergs and drifting pack ice.","f":["Norse sagas describe Markland, a forested coast thought to be Labrador, reached around AD 1000.","The cold Labrador Current flows south along its western side, carrying icebergs and sea ice.","Cape Farewell, the southern tip of Greenland, marks its north-eastern corner."],"q":{"q":"Where do most icebergs in the Labrador Sea come from?","a":"Greenland's glaciers","x":["Antarctica","Hudson Bay"]}},"Q3040":{"t":"An inland sea that freezes over every winter","s":"Hudson Bay is a true sea set inside a continent, and each winter it freezes over almost completely. Look at its eastern coast: it bends in a smooth, near-perfect arc, as if traced around a giant plate. The land around the bay is still slowly rising, springing back now that the weight of the ice-age glaciers is gone.","w":"A vast, roughly round bay in northern Canada — white ice in winter, dark water in late summer; note the smooth eastern arc.","f":["The smooth curve of its eastern shore is known as the Nastapoka Arc.","The land around it is still rising by about a centimetre a year since the last ice age.","Each autumn polar bears gather near Churchill, waiting for the bay to freeze so they can hunt seals."],"q":{"q":"What are Churchill's polar bears waiting for each autumn?","a":"The bay to freeze over","x":["The salmon run","The first snowfall"]}},"Q37040":{"t":"Iceberg-strewn water between Baffin Island and Greenland","s":"On a clear day Baffin Bay is all contrast: Greenland's white ice cap and glacier tongues on one side, the snowy mountains of Baffin Island on the other. Icebergs from glaciers such as Ilulissat drift across the water like scattered sugar cubes. In winter most of the bay freezes — except the North Water in the far north, which stays open.","w":"A long bay between Greenland's ice-covered coast and Baffin Island; white icebergs dot the dark water in summer.","f":["The North Water is one of the largest patches of Arctic sea that stays open through the winter.","Ilulissat Icefjord on its Greenland shore, a UNESCO site, sends huge icebergs into the bay.","It is named after William Baffin, who sailed and charted it in 1616."],"q":{"q":"What is the North Water in Baffin Bay?","a":"Sea that stays ice-free in winter","x":["A deep undersea canyon","A freshwater lake on an island"]}},"Q180531":{"t":"Where glaciers pour down to the sea","s":"Few coasts look wilder from the air. Along the Gulf of Alaska, ice fields spill glaciers straight towards the sea, and the Malaspina Glacier spreads across the coastal plain in a huge lobe striped like marble cake. Where silty meltwater meets clear ocean, you may see a sharp line between milky green and deep blue.","w":"The curving southern coast of Alaska: snowy peaks, glacier tongues, and the vast rounded lobe of the Malaspina Glacier.","f":["The Malaspina is one of the world's largest piedmont glaciers — ice that spreads out over flat land.","Mount Saint Elias, 5,489 m high, rises close to the gulf's northern shore.","Kodiak Island, on its western side, is home to the huge Kodiak brown bear."],"q":{"q":"About how high is Mount Saint Elias, above the Gulf of Alaska?","a":"About 5,500 m","x":["About 3,500 m","About 7,500 m"]}},"Q23406":{"t":"A young ocean being born between Africa and Arabia","s":"The Red Sea is an ocean in the making: Africa and Arabia are slowly pulling apart, and the crack between them has flooded. From the air it's a long, narrow blue channel between desert coasts, fringed with turquoise reefs. In the north it splits around the Sinai into two horns — the Gulfs of Suez and Aqaba.","w":"A long, slender sea between brown desert shores, with turquoise coral fringes; at its top, two arms around the Sinai triangle.","f":["No permanent river flows into it, and it is one of the saltiest seas connected to the ocean.","Africa and Arabia are drifting apart here by roughly a centimetre a year.","Since 1869 the Suez Canal has linked its northern tip to the Mediterranean."],"q":{"q":"Which two gulfs form the Red Sea's northern “horns”?","a":"Suez and Aqaba","x":["Aden and Oman","Aqaba and Sidra"]}},"Q41602":{"t":"A northern sea whose ice reaches Hokkaido","s":"In winter the Sea of Okhotsk turns white far to the south: its drift ice reaches the coast of Hokkaido, at roughly the latitude of Venice. The sea is walled in by Kamchatka to the east, Sakhalin to the west and the long chain of Kuril volcanoes to the south-east. In summer, fog often hides the water.","w":"A broad northern sea between Kamchatka, Sakhalin and the Kuril arc; white with ice in winter, often under fog in summer.","f":["Winter drift ice here reaches Hokkaido's coast at around 44° North.","The Kuril Islands, a chain of volcanoes, separate it from the open Pacific.","Okhotsk, founded on its shore in the 1640s, was the first Russian settlement on the Pacific."],"q":{"q":"Which island's coast does Okhotsk drift ice reach in the south?","a":"Hokkaido","x":["Honshu","Kyushu"]}},"Q173343":{"t":"Home of the clearest seawater ever measured","s":"The Weddell Sea is ruled by ice. Its pack ice turns in a slow clockwise gyre that trapped Shackleton's ship Endurance in 1915 and crushed it; the crew all survived, and the wreck was found in 2022 some 3 km down. To the south lies the Filchner–Ronne Ice Shelf, a floating plain of ice ending in cliffs tens of metres high.","w":"A vast sheet of pack ice east of the Antarctic Peninsula, ending at the flat white cliff of a floating ice shelf.","f":["In 1986 a white disc lowered into its water stayed visible to 79 m — the clearest seawater ever recorded.","The Filchner–Ronne Ice Shelf at its southern end is one of the largest floating ice shelves on Earth.","It is named after James Weddell, a British sealer who sailed deep into it in 1823."],"q":{"q":"Whose ship was crushed by the Weddell Sea's ice in 1915?","a":"Ernest Shackleton's","x":["Roald Amundsen's","Robert Scott's"]}},"Q34675":{"t":"Shallow, warm, and dotted with man-made islands","s":"This is a shallow, bath-warm sea — on average only about 50 m deep, with summer surface water often above 30 °C. Look off Dubai for the unmistakable fronds of the palm-shaped artificial islands, and for Qatar jutting north like a thumb. At night, gas flares and city lights ring the water. Before oil, its wealth came from pearls.","w":"A shallow turquoise gulf between Iran and Arabia; spot Qatar's thumb-shaped peninsula and Dubai's palm-shaped islands.","f":["The Tigris and Euphrates reach it together through the Shatt al-Arab.","Its only outlet to the ocean is the Strait of Hormuz, less than 40 km wide at its narrowest.","For centuries pearl diving was the main livelihood of towns on its Arabian shore."],"q":{"q":"Which peninsula juts into the Persian Gulf like a thumb?","a":"Qatar","x":["Sinai","Kathiawar"]}},"Q164466":{"t":"The sea at the foot of the great ice barrier","s":"The Ross Sea ends in a wall. Its southern edge is the Ross Ice Shelf, a floating slab of ice roughly the size of France, whose cliffs early explorers simply called “the Barrier”. On Ross Island, Mount Erebus sends up steam from a lava lake in its crater. Nowhere does open sea reach closer to the South Pole.","w":"Dark water and pack ice ending at a flat, sharp-edged white plateau of floating ice; to the west, snowy volcanic Ross Island.","f":["The ice shelf's front rises tens of metres above the sea and runs for some 600 km.","Mount Erebus, 3,794 m high, is the southernmost active volcano on Earth.","Much of the Ross Sea has been a marine protected area since 2017."],"q":{"q":"The Ross Ice Shelf is roughly the size of which country?","a":"France","x":["Belgium","Ireland"]}},"Q5525":{"t":"The biggest lake wholly in Canada, on the Arctic Circle","s":"Great Bear Lake sprawls across the northern forest and tundra in five long arms, like a starfish pressed into the land. The Arctic Circle crosses its northern part, and ice often lingers on the water into July. It is the largest lake lying entirely inside Canada, and it is almost untouched: only one community, Déline, sits on its shore.","w":"A huge, many-armed lake in the far north-west of Canada, white or streaked with ice for much of the year.","f":["Its outflow, the Great Bear River, runs into the Mackenzie.","A 33-kg lake trout caught here in 1995 set a rod-and-reel world record.","The lake and its watershed form Tsá Tué, a UNESCO biosphere reserve."],"q":{"q":"Which line crosses Great Bear Lake?","a":"The Arctic Circle","x":["The 60th parallel","The Continental Divide"]}},"Q5539":{"t":"North America's deepest lake","s":"Great Slave Lake has two faces. The main western basin is broad and open, while the East Arm is a maze of cliffs, islands and deep channels — the lake reaches 614 m there, deeper than any other in North America. The territorial capital, Yellowknife, sits on the north shore, and the Mackenzie River slips out of the western end.","w":"A wide lake with a long, island-studded eastern arm; at night, Yellowknife's lights on the north shore.","f":["Its name comes from the Slavey, a Dene people, not from slavery.","In winter an ice road across the frozen lake links Yellowknife with the community of Dettah.","Ice covers the lake for roughly half of every year."],"q":{"q":"How deep is Great Slave Lake at its deepest?","a":"614 m","x":["214 m","1,014 m"]}},"Q3272":{"t":"A shallow giant left behind by an ice-age lake","s":"Lake Winnipeg stretches north for over 400 km, pinched at the waist where its northern and southern basins meet at the Narrows. For all its size it is shallow — about 12 m on average — and in summer, algae blooms can swirl across it in shades of green visible from the air. Its name means “muddy water” in Cree.","w":"A long lake north of the city of Winnipeg, pinched in the middle; often greenish in late summer.","f":["It is a remnant of glacial Lake Agassiz, which once covered more area than all of today's Great Lakes.","Its outflow, the Nelson River, carries its water north to Hudson Bay.","Its drainage basin reaches all the way to the Rocky Mountains."],"q":{"q":"What does the Cree name “Winnipeg” mean?","a":"Muddy water","x":["Big water","Lake of spirits"]}},"Q5492":{"t":"The shallow, warm Great Lake that feeds Niagara","s":"Lake Erie is the shallowest of the Great Lakes, so it warms fastest in summer and is the one most likely to freeze over in winter. From above, look for Long Point, a slender sand spit curving about 40 km out from the Canadian shore. The lake's water leaves eastward, over Niagara Falls.","w":"A long oval lake between Ontario and Ohio; look for the thin hooked spit of Long Point on the northern shore.","f":["It averages only about 19 m deep — far less than the other Great Lakes.","Point Pelee, jutting into its western part, is the southern tip of mainland Canada.","In late summer, green algae blooms in its western basin can be seen from space."],"q":{"q":"Which of the Great Lakes is the shallowest?","a":"Lake Erie","x":["Lake Ontario","Lake Huron"]}},"Q1062":{"t":"The last Great Lake before the sea","s":"This is the final step in the Great Lakes' staircase: water arrives down the Niagara River and leaves through the St Lawrence towards the Atlantic. Lake Ontario is the smallest of the five by area, yet deep enough that it rarely freezes over. At night, look for the dense glow of Toronto and its suburbs wrapped around the western shore.","w":"An oval lake with Toronto's urban sprawl on its north-west shore; at its eastern end, a scatter of islands.","f":["The St Lawrence leaves it among the Thousand Islands — in fact there are more than 1,800.","Its deepest point is about 244 m.","Winds crossing it dump heavy lake-effect snow on New York's Tug Hill Plateau."],"q":{"q":"Which river carries Lake Ontario's water to the Atlantic?","a":"The St Lawrence","x":["The Hudson","The Ottawa"]}},"Q7343":{"t":"Turquoise lace stitched along Australia's coast","s":"Look for patches of turquoise and pale lace scattered over deep navy water, far off the Queensland coast. Each is a reef built by living coral, and together they form the largest coral reef system on Earth, running for about 2,300 km. It is so big that astronauts can pick it out from orbit.","w":"Turquoise and cream patches on dark blue sea, well offshore, lined up parallel to the Queensland coast.","f":["It is made up of roughly 3,000 separate reefs and about 900 islands.","UNESCO listed the reef as a World Heritage site in 1981.","Once a year, a few nights after a full moon in October or November, its corals release their eggs almost all at once."],"q":{"q":"Roughly how long is the Great Barrier Reef?","a":"About 2,300 km","x":["About 800 km","About 5,000 km"]}},"Q118841":{"t":"A mile-deep gash with Earth's history on its walls","s":"Even from 11 km up the Grand Canyon looks like a wound in the plateau: a jagged chasm of red, orange and cream layers, with the Colorado River a thin green-brown thread at the bottom. It is about 446 km long and up to 29 km wide. In places the river runs some 1,800 metres below the rim.","w":"A branching maze of red and cream cliffs cutting the flat plateau, the river hidden deep inside.","f":["The oldest rocks at the bottom of the canyon are nearly two billion years old.","The North Rim is about 300 m higher than the South Rim, gets far more snow and closes to visitors in winter.","The Grand Canyon became a US national park in 1919."],"q":{"q":"Which river carved the Grand Canyon?","a":"The Colorado","x":["The Rio Grande","The Snake"]}},"Q43278":{"t":"A waterfall that announces itself with a cloud","s":"Before you see the falls you may see their cloud: a white plume of spray rising above the Zambezi, visible from dozens of kilometres away when the river is high. Below it the water drops into a narrow chasm about 1.7 km long, then zigzags through a chain of gorges. Locally it is called Mosi-oa-Tunya, 'the smoke that thunders'.","w":"A white mist above the Zambezi, then a sharp zigzag of gorges below it, on the Zambia–Zimbabwe border.","f":["About 1,700 m wide and over 100 m high, it is one of the largest sheets of falling water in the world.","David Livingstone reached the falls in 1855 and named them after Queen Victoria.","The zigzag gorges downstream are former lips of the falls, left behind as the water cut its way back upstream."],"q":{"q":"What does the local name Mosi-oa-Tunya mean?","a":"The smoke that thunders","x":["The river that sings","The great white wall"]}},"Q34221":{"t":"Where Lake Erie spills over a cliff towards Ontario","s":"From the air, look for a white smudge of spray between Lake Erie and Lake Ontario: that is the Horseshoe Falls, curved just like its name. The Niagara River carries the outflow of four Great Lakes over the edge of the Niagara Escarpment here. The falls formed roughly 12,000 years ago, as the ice sheets melted back.","w":"A short river linking Lake Erie and Lake Ontario; the falls are a white patch where it bends.","f":["There are three falls: the big Horseshoe Falls on the Canadian side, and the American and Bridal Veil Falls next door.","The falls are about 50 m high; creeping upstream, they have cut a gorge some 11 km long.","At night much of the river is diverted to hydroelectric plants, so less water goes over the falls."],"q":{"q":"Which two lakes does the Niagara River connect?","a":"Erie and Ontario","x":["Huron and Erie","Superior and Michigan"]}},"Q36332":{"t":"Some 275 falls strung along one jungle cliff","s":"Iguaçu is not one waterfall but a whole horseshoe of them: around 275 separate cascades spread along nearly 3 km of cliff, on the border of Brazil and Argentina. From above, look for a brown river breaking into white foam inside green forest. The deepest point, the U-shaped Devil's Throat, swallows about half of the river's flow.","w":"White foam in green forest, just east of where the Iguaçu River meets the wide Paraná.","f":["The river's name comes from the Guarani words for 'big water'.","Both the Argentine and the Brazilian national parks around the falls are UNESCO World Heritage sites.","Downstream the Iguaçu joins the Paraná at a point where Brazil, Argentina and Paraguay meet."],"q":{"q":"Which two countries share Iguaçu Falls?","a":"Brazil and Argentina","x":["Brazil and Paraguay","Argentina and Uruguay"]}},"Q3783":{"t":"A café-au-lait river in an ocean of green","s":"Below you the Amazon, here still called the Solimões, is a broad café-au-lait band wandering through unbroken green, with side channels, islands and lakes along both banks. It carries about a fifth of all the river water that reaches the world's oceans. Not a single bridge crosses its main stream.","w":"A wide brown river with pale sandbars and dark lakes on either side, cutting through unbroken forest.","f":["Its average flow is around 200,000 cubic metres per second, more than the next several largest rivers combined.","At Manaus the dark Rio Negro joins it, and the two waters run side by side for kilometres before mixing.","In 1500 Vicente Yáñez Pinzón found fresh water at sea off its mouth and named the river the 'Sweet Sea'."],"q":{"q":"What is the Amazon called in Brazil above Manaus?","a":"Solimões","x":["Madeira","Tapajós"]}},"Q3392":{"t":"A green thread stitched through the Sahara","s":"Few sights from the air are as stark as this: a single blue-green line with a narrow fringe of fields and palms, and bare desert on both sides. Here in northern Sudan the Nile makes its Great Bend, flowing south-west for a few hundred kilometres before turning north again. Nubian kings built pyramids along this stretch.","w":"A thin dark line with a green fringe across pale desert, curving in a big S.","f":["At about 6,650 km, the Nile is one of the two longest rivers on Earth, alongside the Amazon.","Sudan has more pyramids than Egypt, around two hundred of them, smaller and steeper than Giza's.","Its water comes from two sources: the White Nile from the lakes of East Africa and the Blue Nile from Ethiopia."],"q":{"q":"Where do the White Nile and the Blue Nile meet?","a":"Khartoum","x":["Aswan","Juba"]}},"Q932289":{"t":"Africa's great green heart, cut by brown rivers","s":"For hundreds of kilometres there is almost nothing but forest: a dense green carpet, cut by brown rivers that loop and braid through it. This is the heart of the Congo basin, home to the second-largest tropical rainforest on Earth after the Amazon. By afternoon towering thunderclouds often build over it, fed by moisture the trees themselves breathe out.","w":"Unbroken dark green forest with meandering brown rivers; in the afternoon, clusters of tall white storm clouds.","f":["The Congo River crosses the equator twice as it curves round the basin.","Bonobos live only here, south of the Congo River.","In 2017 scientists mapped a huge peatland beneath its swamp forests, the largest tropical peatland known."],"q":{"q":"Which ape lives only south of the Congo River?","a":"Bonobo","x":["Chimpanzee","Orangutan"]}},"Q244451":{"t":"The greatest river basin on Earth, roofed in forest","s":"Look down and count the shades of green: canopy, swamp forest, pale patches of grassland, and rivers the colour of tea or milky coffee. This is the Amazon lowland, where forest still runs to the horizon in most directions. In places, straight roads with side tracks cut into it in a fishbone pattern, the mark of clearing for farms.","w":"Endless forest crossed by winding rivers; near roads, rectangular clearings arranged like a fishbone.","f":["The Amazon's drainage basin covers about 7 million km², the largest of any river on Earth.","Much of the rain here was first evaporated by the forest itself, recycled again and again as air moves west.","Dust blown across the Atlantic from the Sahara helps fertilise the forest's poor soils."],"q":{"q":"Where does much of the dust that fertilises the Amazon come from?","a":"The Sahara","x":["The Kalahari","The Atacama"]}},"Q797":{"t":"America's biggest state, and its emptiest","s":"Below you is the Alaskan interior: dark spruce forest, a glitter of lakes and the wide braided channels of the Yukon and its tributaries, with hardly a road in sight. Alaska is more than twice the size of Texas. On a clear day the white bulk of Denali, North America's highest peak at 6,190 m, may stand out far to the south.","w":"Dark forest, silvery lakes and braided rivers with almost no roads; snowy ranges on the southern horizon.","f":["The United States bought Alaska from Russia in 1867 for 7.2 million dollars.","Alaska has a longer coastline than all the other US states combined.","In Fairbanks, in the interior, midsummer days last almost 22 hours."],"q":{"q":"From which country did the US buy Alaska in 1867?","a":"Russia","x":["Britain","Denmark"]}},"Q5428":{"t":"Taiga from horizon to horizon, for hours on end","s":"You can fly for hours over Siberia and see little but taiga: dark larch and pine forest, winding rivers and, in winter, snow that turns the whole land white and blue. Here, on the Central Siberian Plateau, the Lena, the Lower Tunguska and their tributaries cut deep valleys. Siberia covers roughly three-quarters of Russia.","w":"Endless dark forest and looping rivers in deep valleys; in winter, white snow with grey larch woods showing through.","f":["Siberia covers about 13 million km², more than Canada or China.","Much of the taiga here is larch, which sheds its needles, so in winter the forest looks grey rather than green.","Lake Baikal, in southern Siberia, holds about a fifth of the world's unfrozen fresh surface water."],"q":{"q":"Which Siberian lake is the deepest on Earth?","a":"Baikal","x":["Ladoga","Taymyr"]}},"Q866405":{"t":"The bigger, older half of Antarctica's ice","s":"If you ever see this, it will be a white emptiness without edges: the East Antarctic ice sheet, more than 4 km thick in places and rising to over 4,000 m. Near here stands Russia's Vostok Station, where −89.2 °C was recorded in 1983, the lowest air temperature ever measured by a weather station.","w":"Flat white ice to every horizon, with no landmarks at all; blinding in the summer sun.","f":["About 4 km beneath Vostok Station lies Lake Vostok, a body of liquid water sealed under the ice.","The East Antarctic ice sheet holds enough water to raise world sea level by more than 50 metres.","This ice sheet has been building up for more than 30 million years."],"q":{"q":"What lies about 4 km beneath Vostok Station?","a":"A liquid lake","x":["An active volcano","A buried meteorite"]}},"Q530917":{"t":"An ice sheet resting on a floor below the sea","s":"West Antarctica looks like its eastern twin from above, white, silent and endless, but beneath the ice it is very different. Much of its bedrock lies far below sea level, so the ice sheet rests in a deep basin that the ocean could reach. Scientists watch its glaciers closely, because they are among the fastest-changing on Earth.","w":"An unbroken white plain; towards the coast, dark mountain ridges and crevassed glaciers break through the ice.","f":["Vinson Massif, Antarctica's highest peak at 4,892 m, rises in West Antarctica's Ellsworth Mountains.","If all its ice melted, world sea level would rise by several metres.","The Transantarctic Mountains separate it from East Antarctica."],"q":{"q":"What is Antarctica's highest peak?","a":"Vinson Massif","x":["Mount Erebus","Mount Kirkpatrick"]}},"Q76034":{"t":"Billions of years old, and speckled with lakes","s":"From the air the Canadian Shield looks as if someone had shattered a mirror over the land: thousands of lakes of every shape, bare pink-grey rock and dark patches of spruce or open tundra, all scraped and polished by ice-age glaciers. This is the ancient core of North America. Some of its rocks formed when Earth was barely half a billion years old.","w":"A jigsaw of countless lakes and bare rock with streaks of forest; in winter, white lakes framed by dark trees.","f":["The shield covers roughly half of Canada, wrapped around Hudson Bay like a horseshoe.","The Acasta Gneiss in the Northwest Territories, about 4 billion years old, is among the oldest known rocks on Earth.","Its rocks hold rich deposits of nickel, gold, copper and diamonds."],"q":{"q":"Which bay does the Canadian Shield wrap around?","a":"Hudson Bay","x":["Bay of Fundy","Gulf of St. Lawrence"]}},"Q147304":{"t":"Land built grain by grain by the Yellow River","s":"Below spreads one of the flattest and most crowded farmlands on Earth: a fine mosaic of fields, villages scattered like grains of rice, and often a grey-brown haze over it all. Most of the plain was laid down as silt by the Yellow River, which has shifted its course across it many times. In June, ripe winter wheat turns it gold.","w":"A vast flat patchwork of small fields and villages, often under haze; green in spring, gold in early summer.","f":["The Grand Canal, the longest artificial waterway in the world, crosses the plain between Beijing and Hangzhou.","In places the Yellow River flows higher than the fields around it, held between tall levees.","Around 1855 the Yellow River moved its mouth from south of the Shandong Peninsula to north of it."],"q":{"q":"Which river laid down most of the North China Plain?","a":"The Yellow River","x":["The Yangtze","The Pearl River"]}},"Q602925":{"t":"Grassland to every horizon, in the heart of Eurasia","s":"Here the land opens up completely: rolling grassland, green in May and straw-gold by midsummer, crossed by dirt tracks and dotted with salt lakes that flash white in the sun. The huge rectangular fields in the north date from the Soviet Virgin Lands campaign of the 1950s. In winter everything is white and scoured by wind.","w":"Flat to rolling tan grassland, straight dirt tracks, white salt pans and huge rectangular fields further north.","f":["In the Virgin Lands campaign, begun in 1954, millions of hectares of Kazakh steppe were ploughed for wheat.","The lakes of Korgalzhyn, west of Astana, hold one of the world's most northerly flamingo colonies.","Saiga antelope, with their swollen, trunk-like noses, still roam the Kazakh steppe."],"q":{"q":"Which antelope with a trunk-like nose roams the Kazakh Steppe?","a":"Saiga","x":["Oryx","Nilgai"]}},"Q560549":{"t":"Flat land from the North Sea all the way to the Urals","s":"You are over the north-eastern end of Europe's great lowland, which runs from the North Sea through Germany and Poland and across Russia to the Urals. Out here it is taiga and bog: dark forest, brown peatland glinting with pools, and rivers like the Pechora wandering in broad loops. In the west the same plain is a quilt of fields and towns.","w":"Dark taiga, brown bogs with glinting pools and looping rivers, stretching flat to the horizon.","f":["On average the plain lies less than 200 m above sea level.","Its gentle hills and chains of lakes in Germany and Poland are moraines left by Ice Age glaciers.","The Pechora, which drains its north-eastern part, flows into the Barents Sea."],"q":{"q":"Which mountains mark the plain's eastern end?","a":"The Urals","x":["The Carpathians","The Caucasus"]}},"Q12707":{"t":"Where the land is ruled into squares a mile wide","s":"Look for the grid: roads a mile apart, square fields in green, gold and brown, and here and there perfect circles where pivot sprinklers water the crops. The Great Plains roll from Canada to Texas, east of the Rockies. Once shortgrass prairie grazed by tens of millions of bison, they are now one of the great grain belts of the world.","w":"A ruler-straight grid of roads and fields; to the west, the crumpled badlands of the Little Missouri River.","f":["Much of the southern Plains is watered from the Ogallala Aquifer, one of the largest aquifers in the world.","The mile-square grid goes back to the Land Ordinance of 1785, which set out how US land was surveyed.","The Plains tilt gently east, losing well over a kilometre of height from the foot of the Rockies."],"q":{"q":"Why do many fields on the Great Plains look like perfect circles?","a":"Centre-pivot irrigation","x":["Ancient earthworks","Grazing patterns"]}},"Q223":{"t":"An island that is mostly one enormous dome of ice","s":"On flights between Europe and North America you often cross an island that is almost all ice. The ice sheet covers about four-fifths of Greenland and is up to 3 km thick, so its crest sits more than 3,000 m above the sea. At the edges, dark peaks and fjords break through, and in summer the ice is freckled with turquoise meltwater lakes.","w":"A vast featureless white plateau, edged by dark mountains, deep fjords and icebergs drifting off the coast.","f":["Greenland is the world's largest island, about 2.2 million km² — roughly four times the size of France.","If all its ice melted, the world's oceans would rise by about 7 metres.","Only about 56,000 people live here, nearly all on the ice-free coast."],"q":{"q":"What share of Greenland is covered by its ice sheet?","a":"About four-fifths","x":["About half","About a third"]}},"Q35942":{"t":"Thousands of kilometres of ocean, stitched with atolls","s":"Below you is mostly deep blue, broken now and then by a ring of turquoise — an atoll — or the green ridges of a volcanic island. Polynesia is a vast triangle with Hawaii, New Zealand and Easter Island at its corners. People settled all of it in canoes, reading stars, swells and birds, long before Europeans arrived.","w":"Scattered turquoise rings of reef with pale lagoons, and now and then a dark-green volcanic peak wrapped in cloud.","f":["The name comes from the Greek for 'many islands'.","New Zealand, the triangle's south-western corner, was settled only about 700 years ago — one of the last large lands people reached.","Polynesian languages are so close that 'moana', the ocean, is the same word in Hawaii and New Zealand."],"q":{"q":"Which island marks a corner of the Polynesian Triangle?","a":"Easter Island","x":["Fiji","Guam"]}},"Q669037":{"t":"Columbus's mistaken 'Indies', strung across turquoise seas","s":"The islands curve for thousands of kilometres from the Bahamas and Cuba to Trinidad, and from above the colours give them away: shallow banks glowing turquoise, white sand, green mountains. The name is an old mistake — Columbus believed he had reached the Indies of Asia when he landed here in 1492. The big islands lie to the west; a chain of small volcanic ones closes off the Caribbean to the east.","w":"Big mountainous islands to the west, a curving chain of small volcanic ones to the east, bright turquoise shallows in between.","f":["The highest peak, Pico Duarte on Hispaniola, rises to about 3,100 m.","The Caribbean Sea takes its name from the Caribs, a people of the Lesser Antilles."],"q":{"q":"On which island is the highest peak of the West Indies?","a":"Hispaniola","x":["Cuba","Jamaica"]}},"Q928":{"t":"More than 7,000 islands, reefs and volcano cones","s":"Green, mountainous islands break the sea into straits and bays, each coast fringed with a pale line of reef. There are more than 7,000 of them, though Luzon in the north and Mindanao in the south hold over two-thirds of the land. The archipelago sits on the Pacific Ring of Fire, so look for volcanic cones and crater lakes among the forested ridges.","w":"A maze of green islands and narrow straits, with pale reef edges along the coasts.","f":["The islands were named in the 1540s after Prince Philip of Spain, later King Philip II.","On Bohol more than 1,200 rounded hills turn brown in the dry season — the famous Chocolate Hills.","The Philippines lies in the Coral Triangle, the region with the richest variety of reef life on Earth."],"q":{"q":"Which Spanish king gave his name to the Philippines?","a":"Philip II","x":["Charles V","Ferdinand II"]}},"Q17":{"t":"A 3,000-km arc of mountains rising from the Pacific","s":"From cruise height Japan is a long, crumpled arc of forested mountains, with cities squeezed onto the narrow plains between the slopes and the sea. Four big islands carry almost everyone, and thousands of smaller ones are scattered around them. At night the Pacific coast from Tokyo to Osaka glows as one nearly unbroken ribbon of light.","w":"Rugged green mountains down the middle, crowded plains along the coast, and white-capped Fuji standing alone south-west of Tokyo.","f":["About three-quarters of Japan is mountainous, which is why its cities crowd onto the coastal plains.","The country has more than 100 active volcanoes.","A 2023 recount using digital maps found about 14,000 islands — twice the long-quoted figure of 6,852."],"q":{"q":"Roughly how much of Japan is mountainous?","a":"About three-quarters","x":["About a third","About half"]}},"Q85377":{"t":"A frozen jigsaw at the top of North America","s":"Seen from above, this is a jigsaw of brown and white land fitted together with channels of sea ice. Tens of thousands of islands spread north of the Canadian mainland towards Greenland, and for much of the year the sea between them is frozen solid. Explorers spent centuries looking for a way through; the Northwest Passage threads these channels.","w":"Big, flat islands split by straight channels, white with sea ice most of the year; glaciers and ice caps in the east.","f":["Three of the world's ten largest islands are here: Baffin, Victoria and Ellesmere.","Roald Amundsen was the first to sail the whole Northwest Passage, in 1903–1906.","More than 36,000 islands make up the archipelago."],"q":{"q":"Which sea route winds between these islands?","a":"The Northwest Passage","x":["The Northern Sea Route","The Drake Passage"]}},"Q37394":{"t":"Steep green islands where hundreds of languages are spoken","s":"Here the Pacific islands turn tall and green: steep volcanic ridges cloaked in rainforest and rimmed by reef, from New Guinea east to Fiji. Some volcanoes still smoke, so look for a grey plume drifting off a summit. Melanesia is also the most linguistically diverse corner of the planet — villages a few ridges apart can speak unrelated languages.","w":"Large, mountainous, forested islands with reef-fringed coasts; now and then a volcanic plume trailing downwind.","f":["Papua New Guinea alone has more than 800 living languages.","Vanuatu has about 110 languages for some 300,000 people — the densest mix of languages per person on Earth."],"q":{"q":"Papua New Guinea alone has roughly how many languages?","a":"More than 800","x":["About 100","About 300"]}},"Q3359409":{"t":"Thousands of tiny islands above the deepest sea","s":"Micronesia is mostly ocean: thousands of small islands, many of them low atolls whose rings of reef glow turquoise against deep blue. Many rise only a few metres above the waves. On its western edge lies the Mariana Trench, where the sea floor drops almost 11 km — about as far below the surface as you are above it.","w":"Small rings of reef with pale-green lagoons, scattered far apart across dark-blue ocean; occasionally a higher, forested island.","f":["The name means 'small islands' in Greek.","On Yap, huge stone discs called rai, some wider than a person is tall, served as money."],"q":{"q":"What does the name 'Micronesia' mean?","a":"Small islands","x":["Black islands","Many islands"]}},"Q208643":{"t":"The world's largest archipelago, where Asia meets Australia","s":"Between mainland Asia and Australia lies the largest group of islands on Earth — about 25,000 of them, from Sumatra to the Philippines. Look for chains of volcanic cones, rainforest cut into clearings, and shallow seas glowing green over coral. Alfred Russel Wallace travelled here for eight years and noticed that the animals on either side of a narrow strait belonged to different worlds.","w":"Big green islands and strings of volcanic peaks in warm, shallow seas; brown river plumes spreading into turquoise water.","f":["The Wallace Line runs between Bali and Lombok, dividing mostly Asian wildlife to the west from Australian-type wildlife to the east.","While travelling in these islands in 1858, Wallace hit on natural selection independently of Darwin."],"q":{"q":"Between which two islands does the Wallace Line run?","a":"Bali and Lombok","x":["Java and Sumatra","Borneo and Java"]}},"Q38272":{"t":"A green, cloud-dappled cluster off Europe's north-west edge","s":"Two large islands and thousands of small ones sit off the north-west coast of Europe, and on a clear day they look intensely green, patched with fields and fringed by rocky headlands. Cloud is common: Atlantic air brings frequent rain. Until roughly 8,000 years ago you could have walked here from the continent, across a plain now drowned beneath the North Sea.","w":"Great Britain's long north–south shape with Ireland to its west, a patchwork of green fields, and ragged island clusters in the north.","f":["The group has more than 6,000 islands, most of them small and uninhabited.","The drowned land that once joined Britain to Europe is called Doggerland, after the Dogger Bank.","Warm water carried by the Gulf Stream keeps winters here mild for such a northern latitude."],"q":{"q":"What is the drowned land between Britain and Europe called?","a":"Doggerland","x":["Lyonesse","Hyperborea"]}},"Q664":{"t":"One of the last large lands people reached","s":"Two long islands and hundreds of small ones lie alone in the South Pacific, about 2,000 km east of Australia. Look for the snowy spine of the Southern Alps in the south and volcanic cones in the north. Polynesian voyagers arrived only about 700 years ago, making this one of the last large lands on Earth to be settled.","w":"Snowy peaks in the south, glacier-blue lakes and fjords in the south-west, green farmland on the plains.","f":["Cook Strait, between the two main islands, is only about 22 km wide at its narrowest.","New Zealand sits on Zealandia, a sunken continent that is about 94% under the sea.","Aotearoa, the Māori name, is often translated as 'land of the long white cloud'."],"q":{"q":"What is the Māori name for New Zealand?","a":"Aotearoa","x":["Rapa Nui","Hawaiki"]}},"Q3740828":{"t":"Fire and ice on a rift in the Atlantic","s":"Iceland looks unfinished from above: black lava fields and grey outwash plains, white ice caps, and a thin green rim along the coast. The island sits right on the Mid-Atlantic Ridge, where North America and Eurasia pull apart, which is why it has so many volcanoes. Glacial rivers spread across the sand in braids, like pale veins.","w":"Dazzling white ice caps inland, dark lava and sand plains, braided rivers and a narrow green strip by the sea.","f":["The two plates move apart by about 2 cm a year, and Iceland slowly widens with them.","Vatnajökull, the largest ice cap, covers about 8% of the country and hides active volcanoes beneath it.","Geologists everywhere use the Icelandic word 'sandur' for a glacial outwash plain."],"q":{"q":"Which two plates pull apart across Iceland?","a":"North American and Eurasian","x":["African and Eurasian","Pacific and North American"]}},"Q7463928":{"t":"The Red Island, where life went its own way","s":"Madagascar's nickname, the Red Island, makes sense from above: bare laterite soil shows through in rust and orange, and after rain the rivers pour red silt into the sea. It split from Africa, and later from India, tens of millions of years ago, and life here evolved in isolation. Look for green rainforest along the east coast and dry plains in the west.","w":"A huge island with a green eastern escarpment, rust-red highlands, and rivers bleeding red sediment into the sea.","f":["At about 587,000 km², it is the world's fourth-largest island.","Lemurs evolved only here, and about 90% of Madagascar's wildlife is found nowhere else.","Malagasy, the island's language, is closest to languages of southern Borneo, more than 6,000 km away."],"q":{"q":"Malagasy is most closely related to languages from which island?","a":"Borneo","x":["Sri Lanka","Zanzibar"]}},"Q710":{"t":"A country of atolls in all four hemispheres","s":"Kiribati is 33 tiny islands scattered across thousands of kilometres of ocean. From above, most are thin rings of land around pale lagoons, often only a few metres above the sea. It is the only country in all four hemispheres, and in 1995 it moved the date line so that all its islands share the same calendar day.","w":"Thin rings of land enclosing jade-green lagoons, tiny against the deep blue, spread far apart along the equator.","f":["'Kiribati' is pronounced 'Kiribas' — the local form of 'Gilberts', the islands' old British name.","About half of the country's people live on South Tarawa, the capital atoll."],"q":{"q":"'Kiribati' is the local form of which name?","a":"Gilbert Islands","x":["Caroline Islands","Marshall Islands"]}},"Q192626":{"t":"Volcanoes on a conveyor belt across the Pacific","s":"The islands line up from south-east to north-west, and their shapes tell the story. In the south-east the Big Island is broad and young, its volcanoes still active; further north-west the islands grow older, more eroded and more deeply cut by green valleys. Beyond them the chain dwindles into atolls and sunken seamounts. It formed as the Pacific Plate slid over a fixed hotspot.","w":"A diagonal line of islands: broad dark volcanoes to the south-east, older, green, deeply furrowed islands to the north-west.","f":["Hawaii is about 3,800 km from California, one of the most isolated island groups on Earth.","Kauai, near the north-western end of the main islands, is about 5 million years old; the Big Island is under 1 million.","A new volcano, Kamaʻehuakanaloa (formerly Lōʻihi), is growing on the sea floor south-east of the Big Island."],"q":{"q":"What created the Hawaiian Islands?","a":"A hotspot under the Pacific Plate","x":["A subduction zone","A mid-ocean ridge"]}},"Q234796":{"t":"Where the new year arrives before anywhere else","s":"A loose line of coral atolls runs across the equator in the middle of the Pacific, hundreds of kilometres apart. The largest, Kiritimati, is hard to miss: a huge ring of land with a maze of shallow, milky-blue lagoons inside. The islands that belong to Kiribati keep time 14 hours ahead of Greenwich, so they greet each new year first.","w":"Tiny, isolated atolls strung north to south; Kiritimati stands out as a big pale ring full of patchy lagoons.","f":["Kiritimati has the largest land area of any coral atoll, about 390 km².","Its name is the local spelling of 'Christmas': Captain Cook reached it on Christmas Eve 1777.","Of the 11 Line Islands, 8 belong to Kiribati; the rest are United States territories."],"q":{"q":"Which Line Island is the largest coral atoll by land area?","a":"Kiritimati","x":["Tabuaeran","Palmyra"]}},"Q118350":{"t":"Hundreds of atolls and a few high green islands","s":"The Carolines are scattered over some 3,000 km of the western Pacific, just north of the equator. Most are low atolls, rings of reef around turquoise lagoons, but Pohnpei, Kosrae and the peaks of Chuuk rise as green, cloud-capped islands. Their navigators crossed open ocean without instruments, and their knowledge helped revive Polynesian voyaging in the 1970s.","w":"Small turquoise-ringed atolls far apart; Chuuk shows as a huge reef ring with green peaks inside.","f":["The islands are named after King Charles II of Spain.","Nan Madol, a town of basalt built on about 100 artificial islets off Pohnpei, is a UNESCO World Heritage site.","Master navigator Mau Piailug of Satawal guided the canoe Hōkūleʻa from Hawaii to Tahiti in 1976."],"q":{"q":"Which king are the Caroline Islands named after?","a":"Charles II of Spain","x":["Charles I of England","Charles III of Spain"]}},"Q165481":{"t":"Four giant islands on a drowned edge of Asia","s":"Borneo, Sumatra, Java and Sulawesi make up the Greater Sunda Islands, and three of them rise from a shallow shelf that was dry land during the ice ages. The seas between them are rarely deeper than about 100 m, so the water often looks green rather than deep blue. Look for volcano chains along Sumatra and Java and dark rainforest on Borneo.","w":"Big green islands in shallow, greenish seas; lines of volcanic cones along Sumatra and Java.","f":["Java is the world's most populous island, home to more than 150 million people.","In the ice ages people and animals could walk from mainland Asia to Java, Sumatra and Borneo.","Sulawesi is the odd one out: it lies beyond the Wallace Line and has a strange mix of wildlife."],"q":{"q":"Which is the largest of the Greater Sunda Islands?","a":"Borneo","x":["Sumatra","Sulawesi"]}},"Q1042":{"t":"Granite islands left behind by a drifting continent","s":"Most islands in open ocean are volcanoes or coral, but the Seychelles' inner islands are granite — a sliver of an ancient continent, stranded when India drifted away to the north. From above, Mahé and Praslin show steep green hills and grey boulders tumbling into turquoise bays. Far to the south-west, the outer islands are flat coral atolls lost in the blue.","w":"Steep green granite islands with turquoise bays in the north-east; low, far-flung coral atolls to the south-west.","f":["The coco de mer palm, native to Praslin and Curieuse, bears the heaviest seed of any plant.","Aldabra atoll is home to roughly 100,000 giant tortoises.","The Seychelles has the smallest population of any African country."],"q":{"q":"What rock are Mahé and Praslin made of?","a":"Granite","x":["Basalt","Coral limestone"]}},"Q4526612":{"t":"A green teardrop off India's southern tip","s":"Sri Lanka hangs off the tip of India like a teardrop, its narrow northern end pointing at a chain of sandbanks that almost reaches the mainland. The south-central highlands rise as a dark-green knot, with tea estates climbing their slopes. Around them lie rice paddies, reservoirs built by ancient kings, and a golden rim of beaches.","w":"A teardrop-shaped island: mountains in the south-centre, and a thin chain of sandbanks reaching toward India from the north-west.","f":["The word 'serendipity' comes from Serendip, an old name for the island.","Adam's Bridge, a chain of shoals about 50 km long, almost links Sri Lanka to India.","A footprint-shaped hollow on top of Adam's Peak is revered by Buddhists, Hindus, Muslims and Christians alike."],"q":{"q":"Which English word comes from an old name for Sri Lanka?","a":"Serendipity","x":["Cinnamon","Paradise"]}},"Q3593416":{"t":"A heart-shaped island facing the Southern Ocean","s":"Tasmania is shaped a little like a heart, and from above its west is wild: dark forest, buttongrass plains, jagged mountains and lakes, with hardly a road. The east is gentler, with farmland and white-sand bays. The next land to the south is Antarctica, and the westerlies of the Roaring Forties arrive straight off thousands of kilometres of open ocean.","w":"A heart-shaped island south of Australia: rugged dark-green west, drier farmland and bright bays along the east coast.","f":["Abel Tasman sighted it in 1642, and for two centuries it was called Van Diemen's Land.","Air measured at Cape Grim, on the north-west tip, is among the cleanest on Earth.","The Tasmanian devil now survives in the wild only here."],"q":{"q":"What was Tasmania's old European name?","a":"Van Diemen's Land","x":["New Holland","Terra Australis"]}},"Q40285":{"t":"Rainforest, great rivers and ice beside the equator","s":"The world's second-largest island is a green giant with a mountain spine running its full length, usually wrapped in cloud. Rivers such as the Sepik and the Fly loop through swamp forest in wide brown meanders you can follow for hundreds of kilometres. In the west the peaks reach almost 4,900 m, high enough for small glaciers to have survived right beside the equator.","w":"A huge green island with a long, cloud-wrapped mountain spine and wide, snaking brown rivers through lowland swamp.","f":["Puncak Jaya, 4,884 m, is the highest mountain on any island in the world.","At about 786,000 km², New Guinea is second only to Greenland among the world's islands.","Most of the world's birds-of-paradise, famous for their dancing males, are found on New Guinea."],"q":{"q":"Which is the highest peak on New Guinea?","a":"Puncak Jaya","x":["Mount Wilhelm","Mount Kinabalu"]}},"Q36117":{"t":"One island, three countries and a granite giant","s":"Borneo is the third-largest island on Earth, and from the air you can read its story in its textures. Deep, crinkled rainforest covers the interior, brown rivers coil towards the coast, and across many lowlands neat grids of oil-palm plantations have replaced the jungle. In the north-east, Mount Kinabalu's bare granite summit pokes above the clouds at 4,095 m.","w":"Dark rainforest, coiling brown rivers and geometric plantation grids; in the north-east, Kinabalu's rocky summit above the clouds.","f":["Borneo is shared by three countries: Indonesia, Malaysia and Brunei.","The Kapuas, about 1,140 km long, is Indonesia's longest river.","Wild orangutans live only on Borneo and Sumatra."],"q":{"q":"How many countries share the island of Borneo?","a":"Three","x":["Two","Four"]}},"Q3492":{"t":"Home of Toba, the world's largest volcanic lake","s":"Sumatra stretches almost 1,800 km across the equator, with a chain of volcanoes running down its western side. The unmissable landmark is Lake Toba: a blue oval about 100 km long, filling the crater of a supervolcano that erupted some 74,000 years ago. In its middle sits Samosir, nearly as big as Singapore — an island in a lake on an island.","w":"A long green island with a volcanic ridge along its west; look for Toba, a vast blue lake with a big island inside.","f":["It is the only place where tigers, rhinos, orangutans and elephants still live side by side in the wild.","At about 473,000 km², Sumatra is the world's sixth-largest island.","The equator crosses Sumatra, so the island lies in both hemispheres."],"q":{"q":"What is the island in the middle of Lake Toba called?","a":"Samosir","x":["Nias","Siberut"]}},"Q13989":{"t":"Fuji, the Japanese Alps and Tokyo on one long island","s":"Honshu is the long, bent heart of Japan, and from the window its variety is striking: snow on the Japanese Alps down the middle, rice plains and city sprawl along the coasts. On a clear day Fuji's lone white cone is unmistakable, south-west of Tokyo's vast grey-brown carpet. Tunnels and bridges tie Honshu to the other three main islands.","w":"A long curved island: snowy peaks down the centre, Fuji's solitary cone, and dense cities crowding the Pacific coast.","f":["About 100 million people live on Honshu — more than on any island except Java.","At about 228,000 km², it is the world's seventh-largest island.","The Seikan Tunnel, which runs under the sea to Hokkaido, is about 54 km long."],"q":{"q":"Which island has more people than Honshu?","a":"Java","x":["Great Britain","Luzon"]}},"Q22890":{"t":"The Emerald Isle lives up to its name","s":"On a clear day Ireland really is emerald: small fields in a hundred shades of green, stitched together by hedges and, in the west, by grey stone walls. The Atlantic coast is ragged with bays and headlands; the midlands are low, dotted with lakes and brown bog. Look for the River Shannon swelling into long lakes as it runs south towards the ocean.","w":"A green island west of Great Britain: ragged Atlantic coast, flat lake-dotted midlands and the long thread of the Shannon.","f":["The Shannon, about 360 km long, is the longest river in Ireland or Britain.","Ireland has no native snakes: after the Ice Age the sea cut it off before they could arrive.","Lough Neagh, in the north-east, is the largest lake in the British Isles."],"q":{"q":"What is the longest river in Ireland?","a":"The Shannon","x":["The Liffey","The Boyne"]}},"Q23666":{"t":"Europe's largest island, chalk cliffs to Highland lochs","s":"Great Britain runs almost 1,000 km from south to north, and flying along it you watch the land change. The south is a soft patchwork of fields and towns around London's huge sprawl; the Pennines form a brown spine through northern England; Scotland breaks up into mountains and long, narrow lochs. At night the cities blaze orange while the Highlands go almost dark.","w":"A long island narrowing northward: green farmland in the south, a brown upland spine, then Scotland's mountains and slender lochs.","f":["It is the ninth-largest island in the world and the largest in Europe.","Ben Nevis, its highest mountain at 1,345 m, is still almost 10 km below cruising height.","The Channel Tunnel, about 50 km long, links it to France beneath the Strait of Dover."],"q":{"q":"What is Great Britain's highest mountain?","a":"Ben Nevis","x":["Snowdon","Scafell Pike"]}},"Q186233":{"t":"A forested chain between the Bay of Bengal and Andaman Sea","s":"A narrow chain of islands, almost entirely cloaked in dark tropical forest, runs north to south between the Bay of Bengal and the Andaman Sea. It belongs to India but lies much closer to Myanmar. The water around it is exceptionally clear, so reefs show as pale turquoise halos. East of the main chain, tiny Barren Island is South Asia's only active volcano.","w":"A slim north–south chain of densely forested islands fringed by turquoise reef, with scattered small islets to the east.","f":["Barren Island, only about 3 km across, is the sole confirmed active volcano in South Asia.","North Sentinel Island is home to one of the last peoples on Earth living without contact with the outside world."],"q":{"q":"Which active volcano lies in the Andaman Islands?","a":"Barren Island","x":["Krakatoa","Mount Popa"]}},"Q157286":{"t":"Scattered green islands at the gateway to Malacca","s":"South of the Andamans, across the Ten Degree Channel, the Nicobars scatter in a loose line towards Sumatra. Some are flat coral islands ringed with palms; others, like Great Nicobar, are hilly and cloaked in rainforest. Most are closed to tourists to protect the Nicobarese and Shompen peoples, so the dark-green shapes and turquoise reefs below are seen by few outsiders.","w":"A loose chain of islands between the Andamans and Sumatra: flat, palm-fringed ones in the north, hilly rainforest ones in the south.","f":["The Ten Degree Channel, about 150 km wide, separates them from the Andamans along the 10th parallel.","The Nicobars lie far closer to Sumatra than to mainland India."],"q":{"q":"Which channel separates the Nicobars from the Andamans?","a":"Ten Degree Channel","x":["Six Degree Channel","Nine Degree Channel"]}},"Q3827":{"t":"The original Spice Islands","s":"For centuries these scattered islands were the world's only source of cloves and nutmeg, and ships sailed halfway round the globe to reach them. From above they are green cones and ridges rising steeply out of very deep blue water, their shores rimmed with coral. Ternate and Tidore, two volcanic cones facing each other across a narrow strait, are the classic view.","w":"Steep green volcanic islands and cones, rimmed with coral, rising from unusually dark-blue water.","f":["Under a 1667 treaty the Dutch kept Run, a tiny nutmeg island here, and the English kept Manhattan.","Nutmeg and mace both come from the same fruit, native to the Banda Islands.","The Banda Sea plunges to more than 7 km deep in the Weber Deep."],"q":{"q":"Which spice came originally only from the Banda Islands?","a":"Nutmeg","x":["Cinnamon","Black pepper"]}},"Q119285":{"t":"A 1,900-km arc of volcanoes reaching toward Asia","s":"Flights between North America and East Asia often skirt the Aleutians, a 1,900-km arc of volcanic islands trailing from Alaska towards Kamchatka. Fog and cloud hide them much of the time; when it clears, look for snow-streaked cones — Shishaldin on Unimak Island is one of the most symmetrical on Earth. North of the chain lies the Bering Sea, south of it the open Pacific.","w":"A long curving chain of rugged islands and snowy volcanic cones, often poking through a lid of fog.","f":["The chain crosses the 180th meridian, so Alaska reaches into the Eastern Hemisphere.","The arc has dozens of volcanoes, and some erupt every few years.","The Unangan (Aleut) people have lived on these islands for about 9,000 years."],"q":{"q":"Which sea lies north of the Aleutian Islands?","a":"The Bering Sea","x":["The Sea of Okhotsk","The Chukchi Sea"]}},"Q216533":{"t":"The top of Canada: ice caps, bare rock, almost no one","s":"At the northern edge of Canada lies a cluster of large islands with bare brown plateaus, glaciers spilling from ice caps, and channels choked with sea ice for most of the year. Only a few hundred people live here, in two small hamlets. The group was named in 1953 to mark the coronation of Queen Elizabeth II.","w":"Big, flat-topped islands of bare rock, white ice caps and glaciers, separated by channels of pack ice.","f":["Devon Island is the largest uninhabited island on Earth.","On Axel Heiberg Island, stumps of trees that grew about 45 million years ago still stand, preserved but not fossilised.","Scientists use Devon Island's Haughton impact crater to rehearse exploring Mars."],"q":{"q":"Which is the largest uninhabited island on Earth?","a":"Devon Island","x":["Ellesmere Island","Melville Island"]}},"Q68740":{"t":"Taller than Everest, if you start from the sea floor","s":"The Big Island is made of five volcanoes fused together, and from above you can see their broad, gentle domes and dark lava flows streaking down to the sea. Mauna Kea and Mauna Loa both top 4,100 m, and in winter their summits can be white with snow. Measured from its base on the ocean floor, Mauna Kea is over 10,000 m tall — more than Everest.","w":"Two huge, gentle domes, snow-dusted in winter, with black lava flows running to the coast and perhaps a volcanic plume.","f":["Mauna Loa is the largest active volcano on Earth by volume.","Lava from Kīlauea has added new land to the island's south-east coast within recent decades.","The Big Island is larger than all the other Hawaiian islands combined."],"q":{"q":"How many volcanoes make up the island of Hawaii?","a":"Five","x":["Two","Eight"]}},"Q120755":{"t":"Snowy Alps, turquoise lakes and fjords","s":"The Southern Alps run like a white wall down the length of the island, and on their dry eastern side glacial lakes glow an unreal milky turquoise — Tekapo and Pukaki are the brightest. To the west, glaciers tumble towards rainforest and the sea; in the south-west, Fiordland's drowned valleys cut deep into the mountains. East of the range, braided rivers fan across the Canterbury Plains.","w":"A white mountain wall down the island, vivid turquoise lakes on its eastern side, fjords slicing the south-west coast.","f":["Aoraki / Mount Cook, 3,724 m, is New Zealand's highest mountain.","The lakes' colour comes from 'rock flour', fine silt ground by glaciers and suspended in the water.","At about 150,000 km², it is the world's 12th-largest island."],"q":{"q":"What gives South Island's glacial lakes their turquoise colour?","a":"Rock flour from glaciers","x":["Algae blooms","Copper minerals"]}},"Q118863":{"t":"Volcanoes, a caldera lake and a perfect lone cone","s":"The centre of the North Island is a volcanic plateau, and it shows. Lake Taupō, the country's largest lake, fills a caldera left by enormous eruptions; south of it rise Ruapehu, Tongariro and the neat cone of Ngauruhoe, often snow-capped. Out on the west coast, Taranaki stands alone: a near-perfect cone ringed by a sharp circle of forest amid green farmland.","w":"A large lake in the centre with snowy volcanoes to its south; on the west coast, a lone cone inside a circle of forest.","f":["Lake Taupō, about 616 km², fills a volcanic caldera; its last great eruption was around 1,800 years ago.","About three-quarters of New Zealanders live on the North Island.","Auckland is built on a field of some 50 small volcanoes."],"q":{"q":"Which lake fills a volcanic caldera in the centre of the North Island?","a":"Lake Taupō","x":["Lake Wakatipu","Lake Tekapo"]}},"Q709":{"t":"Two chains of atolls, barely above the waves","s":"The Marshalls are two parallel chains of coral atolls — the 'sunrise' and 'sunset' chains — strung across the central Pacific. From above, each atoll is a necklace of narrow islets around a lagoon, bright turquoise inside and deep blue outside. The land averages only about 2 m above sea level, so the islands can look like lines painted on the water.","w":"Necklaces of thin islets around wide turquoise lagoons, lined up in two roughly parallel north-west to south-east chains.","f":["Marshallese navigators made stick charts — frames of sticks and shells mapping the ocean swells around the islands.","The country has 29 atolls and 5 single islands, with only about 180 km² of land in all."],"q":{"q":"What did Marshallese navigators map with sticks and shells?","a":"Ocean swells","x":["Star positions","Fish migrations"]}},"Q65":{"t":"A city poured into a basin between peaks and the Pacific","s":"Los Angeles doesn't so much end as run out of room: its sprawl fills a broad basin from the Pacific shore to the foot of the San Gabriel Mountains, then spills into the valleys beyond. In winter those peaks can wear snow above a city of palm trees. It all began in 1781 as a small Spanish farming pueblo.","w":"A vast grey-beige sprawl between mountains and Santa Monica Bay; the long harbour breakwaters of Long Beach to the south.","f":["The Los Angeles River runs mostly in a concrete channel, lined from the 1930s onward to tame its floods.","The neighbouring ports of Los Angeles and Long Beach together form the busiest container port complex in the US.","The highest of the San Gabriels, Mount San Antonio (about 3,070 m), is known locally as Mount Baldy."],"q":{"q":"Which mountain range walls in Los Angeles to the north?","a":"The San Gabriel Mountains","x":["The Sierra Nevada","The Cascade Range"]}},"Q61":{"t":"A capital drawn on paper first, then built on the Potomac","s":"Washington was planned before it existed: in 1791 Pierre L'Enfant laid broad diagonal avenues over a street grid on farmland beside the Potomac. The district began as a square ten miles a side, set on its corner like a diamond. Virginia took back its share in 1846, so today the river bites off the south-west corner.","w":"Where the Anacostia meets the broad Potomac; a low city without a skyscraper cluster, Reagan National airport right on the riverbank.","f":["Forty stone markers were set along the original diamond-shaped boundary in 1791–92; many still stand.","Congress first met in Washington in 1800.","A law of 1910 caps building heights, which is why the city looks so strikingly low."],"q":{"q":"Which river forms Washington's south-western edge?","a":"The Potomac","x":["The Delaware","The Hudson"]}},"Q60":{"t":"Look for the green rectangle in the middle of the island","s":"Find Manhattan — a long, thin island between the Hudson and the East River — and in its middle a neat green rectangle: Central Park, about four kilometres long. Around it the street grid laid out in 1811 runs block after block up the island. The Dutch called this port New Amsterdam until the English took it in 1664 and renamed it after the Duke of York.","w":"A narrow island pointing south into a wide harbour, a green rectangle at its heart, long bridges fanning out to the east.","f":["Central Park measures about 4 km by 0.8 km — big enough to pick out from cruise on a clear day.","The Verrazzano-Narrows Bridge at the harbour mouth had the world's longest suspension span when it opened in 1964.","Manhattan's street grid was fixed by a plan of 1811, long before most of the island was built up."],"q":{"q":"What was New York called before 1664?","a":"New Amsterdam","x":["New Rotterdam","Fort Orange"]}},"Q1489":{"t":"A megacity on a lake that isn't there any more","s":"The grey-brown sea of roofs below fills an old lake bed 2,240 m above sea level. The Aztecs founded Tenochtitlan on an island in Lake Texcoco around 1325; the Spanish later drained most of the water. The clay beneath is still settling, and parts of the city sink every year. On a clear day, look south-east for two snow-capped volcanoes.","w":"A vast city filling a flat high valley ringed by mountains; Popocatépetl and Iztaccíhuatl rise to the south-east.","f":["Popocatépetl, about 5,400 m high, is an active volcano and often trails a plume of steam.","In places the city sinks by more than 20 cm a year as the old lake clay dries out.","Iztaccíhuatl means 'white woman' in Nahuatl, and its long ridge is said to look like a sleeping figure."],"q":{"q":"What did Mexico City's centre stand on in Aztec times?","a":"An island in a lake","x":["A volcanic crater","A river delta"]}},"Q2841":{"t":"A capital at 2,640 m, pressed against a green mountain wall","s":"Bogotá lies on a high, flat savanna in the Andes, about 2,640 m up, with a steep ridge of hills along its eastern edge. The city fills the plain westward from that wall. Look around it for glinting white patches: greenhouses of the flower farms that make Colombia one of the world's great exporters of cut flowers.","w":"A large city on a flat green plateau, hard against a dark ridge to the east; white greenhouse roofs scattered across the fields.","f":["The Spanish conquistador Gonzalo Jiménez de Quesada founded the city in 1538.","The legend of El Dorado grew from a Muisca ritual at Lake Guatavita, about 50 km north-east of the city.","Monserrate, the hill above the old town, rises to about 3,150 m."],"q":{"q":"At which lake did the El Dorado legend begin?","a":"Lake Guatavita","x":["Lake Titicaca","Lake Tota"]}},"Q2887":{"t":"A city in a bowl, with the Andes rising straight behind","s":"Santiago fills a broad valley between the low Coastal Range and the Andes, which climb to snowy peaks over 5,000 m just east of the suburbs. In winter a haze often pools in the bowl while the summits shine above it. Pedro de Valdivia founded the city in 1541 beside a rocky hill on the Mapocho River.","w":"A large city on a flat valley floor at the foot of the Andes' snowy wall; the thin line of the Mapocho River across it.","f":["Aconcagua, at 6,961 m the highest peak outside Asia, stands about 100 km north-east of the city.","Cerro Santa Lucía, the hill where the city was founded, is now a park in the very centre.","The border with Argentina follows the crest of the Andes less than 100 km from the city."],"q":{"q":"Who founded Santiago in 1541?","a":"Pedro de Valdivia","x":["Francisco Pizarro","Diego de Almagro"]}},"Q8678":{"t":"Green granite domes rising straight out of the sea","s":"Few cities are so tangled up with rock: steep, forested granite domes rise between the neighbourhoods and straight out of Guanabara Bay. Portuguese sailors arrived in January 1502 and took the bay for a river mouth — hence 'River of January'. From 1808 to 1821 the Portuguese court lived here, a European monarchy ruling its empire from the Americas.","w":"A wide bay with a narrow mouth, green domes of rock among the buildings, and long pale beaches facing the open Atlantic.","f":["The Rio–Niterói Bridge crosses Guanabara Bay for about 13 km; from cruise it's a fine line over the water.","Much of the Tijuca Forest above the city was replanted by hand from the 1860s on old coffee land.","Sugarloaf Mountain rises about 396 m straight from the water at the mouth of the bay."],"q":{"q":"Which bay does the Rio–Niterói Bridge cross?","a":"Guanabara Bay","x":["All Saints Bay","Paranaguá Bay"]}},"Q174":{"t":"The Southern Hemisphere's biggest city, up on a plateau","s":"From the window São Paulo looks endless: grey and white rooftops to the horizon, stitched together by rivers in straight concrete channels. It sits on a plateau about 760 m up, only some 70 km from the Atlantic, behind the steep forested wall of the Serra do Mar. Jesuit missionaries founded it as a small mission in 1554.","w":"A boundless grey city; large dark reservoirs on its southern edge, the green escarpment and the coast beyond to the south-east.","f":["The Tietê River rises near the coast yet flows inland, over 1,000 km, to join the Paraná.","The Billings and Guarapiranga reservoirs on the southern edge are easy to spot from cruise.","The coffee trade of the 1800s turned a modest hill town into Brazil's economic engine."],"q":{"q":"Which way does the Tietê River flow from São Paulo?","a":"Inland, towards the Paraná","x":["East, to the Atlantic at Santos","North, to the Amazon"]}},"Q62":{"t":"A hilly peninsula tip where fog pours through the Gate","s":"San Francisco fills the tip of a peninsula, with the Pacific on one side and a great bay on the other, joined by the narrow Golden Gate. On summer afternoons you may see fog pouring through that gap like a slow white river. The gold rush of 1849 turned a village of about a thousand people into a boom town within a couple of years.","w":"A peninsula tip between ocean and bay; thin bridge lines across the water; bright red and green salt ponds far to the south.","f":["The salt ponds of the south bay glow red, orange and green, coloured by salt-loving microorganisms.","The Golden Gate strait was named in 1846, almost a century before the famous bridge was built.","Mission San Francisco de Asís, the city's Spanish seed, was founded in 1776."],"q":{"q":"What colours the salt ponds of south San Francisco Bay?","a":"Salt-loving microorganisms","x":["Iron-rich clay","Copper minerals"]}},"Q16554":{"t":"Where the Great Plains run into the Rockies","s":"Denver marks the line where the flat country stops: to the east the plains roll away for a thousand kilometres, to the west the Front Range rises in a wall of peaks over 4,000 m. The city grew from a gold-rush camp of 1858 and sits about a mile above sea level — hence its nickname, the Mile High City.","w":"A city on flat tan plains at the very foot of the Rockies; its huge airport stands alone far out to the north-east.","f":["Denver International is the largest airport in North America by land area, about 135 km².","The city was named after James W. Denver, governor of Kansas Territory, which then included the site.","Longs Peak, about 4,350 m high, is the prominent summit on the north-western skyline."],"q":{"q":"What brought the first settlers to Denver in 1858?","a":"A gold rush","x":["A new railway line","A silver strike"]}},"Q16555":{"t":"Bayous, refineries and a ship channel on a flat plain","s":"Houston spreads across a coastal plain so flat that its slow brown bayous wander in loops through the suburbs. Look south-east for the Ship Channel, a dredged waterway of about 80 km lined with refineries and tanks, running out to Galveston Bay. Founded in 1836, the city was named after Sam Houston, hero of Texan independence.","w":"Endless flat sprawl ringed by freeway loops; to the south-east, a waterway lined with industry leading to a wide bay.","f":["Houston is famous as the largest US city without a formal zoning code.","NASA's Mission Control for crewed spaceflight is in the city's south-eastern suburbs.","At night the refineries along the Ship Channel glitter as a long, dense band of light."],"q":{"q":"Who is Houston named after?","a":"Sam Houston","x":["Stephen F. Austin","Davy Crockett"]}},"Q8652":{"t":"A thin city between the Everglades and turquoise water","s":"Miami is a long, narrow city pinned between two very different edges: the pale green marshes of the Everglades to the west and the turquoise shallows of Biscayne Bay to the east. Beyond the bay runs the thin barrier island of Miami Beach. The city was incorporated only in 1896, just after Henry Flagler's railway arrived.","w":"A strip of city along the coast; a straight line where streets stop and marsh begins; a bright ribbon of island offshore.","f":["Much of Miami lies only a couple of metres above sea level, on porous limestone.","Miami Beach was largely mangrove swamp before it was cleared and built up in the 1910s and 1920s.","The chain of small Venetian Islands across Biscayne Bay is largely man-made."],"q":{"q":"Which wetland lies directly west of Miami?","a":"The Everglades","x":["The Okefenokee Swamp","The Atchafalaya Basin"]}},"Q23556":{"t":"Towers in a forest, beside a giant of an airport","s":"Atlanta looks surprisingly green from above: a thick canopy of trees hides most of its suburbs, with clusters of towers rising out of it. South of downtown, look for the long parallel runways of Hartsfield–Jackson, for many years the world's busiest airport by passengers. The city began in 1837 as the end point of a railway, simply named Terminus.","w":"Clusters of towers in dense green forest; a huge airport with parallel east–west runways to the south; a pale granite dome to the east.","f":["Stone Mountain, a bare granite dome east of the city, rises about 250 m above the surrounding forest.","The Eastern Continental Divide crosses Atlanta: rain on one side flows to the Atlantic, on the other to the Gulf.","The name Atlanta was adopted in 1845, after a brief spell as Marthasville."],"q":{"q":"What was Atlanta called when it began in 1837?","a":"Terminus","x":["Peachtree","Stone Mountain"]}},"Q1297":{"t":"A perfect grid ruled against a lake like an inland sea","s":"Chicago's edge is one long, straight line: Lake Michigan, so wide that from the ground its far shore lies beyond the horizon. Inland the streets run in a strict grid, and at night it glows orange, block by block, far out across the prairie. The city exists because of a short portage here between the Great Lakes and the Mississippi basin.","w":"A huge city on the south-west shore of Lake Michigan; a ruler-straight lakefront, an even street grid, O'Hare to the north-west.","f":["In 1900 engineers reversed the Chicago River, sending it away from the lake towards the Mississippi.","The name comes from a local Native American word for a wild onion that grew along the river.","Most of Chicago's lakefront is kept as public parks and beaches rather than private land."],"q":{"q":"What did engineers do to the Chicago River in 1900?","a":"Reversed its flow","x":["Filled it in","Dammed it into a lake"]}},"Q1533":{"t":"A valley city, walled off from the sea by one mountain","s":"Caracas stretches for some 20 km along a narrow valley about 900 m up, parallel to the Caribbean coast yet hidden from it. Between city and sea rises the green ridge of El Ávila, over 2,700 m at its highest. So the airport sits on the coast at Maiquetía, on the far side of the mountain. The city was founded in 1567.","w":"A long, narrow city in an east–west valley; a steep green ridge to the north, the blue sea just beyond it.","f":["Simón Bolívar, who led much of South America to independence, was born in Caracas in 1783.","The city's full founding name was Santiago de León de Caracas."],"q":{"q":"What separates Caracas from the Caribbean Sea?","a":"The Ávila mountain ridge","x":["A wide coastal lagoon","A desert plain"]}},"Q2868":{"t":"A capital in the desert, under a lid of grey sea mist","s":"Lima is one of the driest capitals on Earth: a brown city on bare desert that gets barely a couple of centimetres of rain a year. Yet for months it hides under a low, grey sea fog, the garúa. Look for the cliffs where the city drops to the Pacific. Francisco Pizarro founded it in 1535 as the City of Kings.","w":"A vast tan city on a desert coast, edged by sea cliffs; bare brown hills inland and the green thread of the Rímac valley.","f":["For more than two centuries Lima was the capital of a Spanish viceroyalty covering most of Spanish South America.","The port of Callao and the international airport lie just west of the centre, on the same coastal plain.","In winter the garúa can keep the sky grey for weeks while barely wetting the ground."],"q":{"q":"What did Pizarro name Lima when he founded it in 1535?","a":"City of Kings","x":["City of the Sun","New Toledo"]}},"Q81033":{"t":"An industrial city in a ring of jagged grey mountains","s":"Monterrey sits in a valley of the Sierra Madre Oriental, and the mountains crowd right up to its suburbs. The most famous is Cerro de la Silla, whose twin-humped ridge really does look like a saddle. Founded in 1596, the city became Mexico's industrial powerhouse: in 1900 the first large steelworks in Latin America was founded here.","w":"A dense city in a valley hemmed by steep, folded grey ridges; a saddle-shaped mountain on its eastern edge.","f":["Cerro de la Silla rises to about 1,800 m, well over a kilometre above the city.","The Santa Catarina River that crosses the city is dry for most of the year.","The Huasteca Canyon on the western edge cuts deep into folded limestone ridges."],"q":{"q":"Which saddle-shaped mountain is the symbol of Monterrey?","a":"Cerro de la Silla","x":["Pico de Orizaba","Nevado de Toluca"]}},"Q24639":{"t":"A green peninsula city between the inlet and the peaks","s":"Vancouver is all edges: sea on three sides, and snowy Coast Mountains rising almost straight from the northern shore. Look for the dark green thumb of Stanley Park pointing into Burrard Inlet, and the flat, chequered farmland of the Fraser delta to the south, where the airport sits on an island. The city was incorporated in 1886.","w":"A city on a peninsula between two waters; a dark green wooded tip, snowy peaks to the north, flat delta to the south.","f":["Stanley Park covers about 400 hectares — more than New York's Central Park.","The inlet was charted in 1792 by Captain George Vancouver, whose name the city took.","The Canadian Pacific Railway arrived in 1887, making the city Canada's gateway to the Pacific."],"q":{"q":"Which river's delta lies on Vancouver's southern side?","a":"The Fraser","x":["The Columbia","The Skeena"]}},"Q172":{"t":"A lakefront city with islands cupping its harbour","s":"Toronto runs along the north shore of Lake Ontario, and the easiest way to spot it is a string of low green islands just offshore, curling round the harbour. Until a storm broke through in 1858 they were a sandy peninsula. Inland, look for deep wooded ravines snaking through the street grid. Until 1834 the town was called York.","w":"A dense city on the north shore of Lake Ontario; a curl of low islands off downtown, green ravines inland.","f":["One of the islands carries a small city airport, only a stone's throw from downtown.","The name is often traced to a Mohawk word for a place where trees stand in the water.","Toronto's network of wooded ravines is among the most extensive of any city in the world."],"q":{"q":"What was Toronto called before 1834?","a":"York","x":["Kingston","Newark"]}},"Q1486":{"t":"A grid of lights on a brown river as wide as a sea","s":"Buenos Aires faces the Río de la Plata, an estuary so broad that the Uruguayan shore lies roughly 50 km away, out of sight from the waterfront. The water is a milky coffee-brown, stained by silt from the Paraná and Uruguay rivers. Behind the shore, the city's square blocks march out across the flat pampas without a hill in sight.","w":"A vast grid city on the south bank of a wide brown estuary; the green maze of the Paraná Delta to the north-west.","f":["The city was founded twice: first in 1536, then for good by Juan de Garay in 1580.","The Río de la Plata is formed where the Paraná and Uruguay rivers meet.","At night the regular blocks glow like an orange chessboard reaching far inland."],"q":{"q":"Who refounded Buenos Aires for good in 1580?","a":"Juan de Garay","x":["Pedro de Mendoza","Juan Díaz de Solís"]}},"Q36091":{"t":"A city of lakes, split by the young Mississippi","s":"Minneapolis is dotted with lakes, several of them strung together inside the city like blue beads. Through the middle runs the Mississippi, still a modest river here, dropping over St. Anthony Falls, the only natural major waterfall on its whole course. The falls powered the flour mills that, around 1900, made this the milling capital of the world.","w":"A flat city freckled with round lakes; the Mississippi winding through its centre and meeting the Minnesota River by the airport.","f":["Minnesota has more than 11,000 lakes larger than 4 hectares — its '10,000 lakes' slogan undersells it.","St. Paul, the twin city and state capital, lies just downstream on the same river.","The airport sits right beside the confluence of the Mississippi and Minnesota rivers."],"q":{"q":"Which industry made Minneapolis famous around 1900?","a":"Flour milling","x":["Car making","Steelmaking"]}},"Q18094":{"t":"A tropical city between a crater and green fluted ridges","s":"Honolulu is squeezed along Oʻahu's south shore, between the reef-fringed sea and the steep, fluted green ridges of the Koʻolau Range. At its eastern end rises Diamond Head, a crater so round it looks stamped into the coast. The nearest point of mainland North America is roughly 3,800 km away, which makes this one of the remotest big cities on Earth.","w":"A strip of city along an island's south coast; a round crater at the east end, a long runway on the water to the west.","f":["Diamond Head is a volcanic tuff cone; sailors in the 1800s mistook glittering calcite crystals on its slopes for diamonds.","One of the airport's main runways, the Reef Runway, was built on landfill over a coral reef.","The name Honolulu is usually translated from Hawaiian as 'sheltered bay'."],"q":{"q":"What is Diamond Head in Honolulu?","a":"An extinct volcanic crater","x":["A coral island","A sea cliff"]}},"Q5083":{"t":"A city on a narrow neck of land, a giant volcano beyond","s":"Seattle sits on a slim strip of hilly land between the salt water of Puget Sound and the long, freshwater Lake Washington. On a clear day the view is stolen by Mount Rainier, a glacier-covered volcano of about 4,390 m some 90 km to the south-east. The city is named after Si'ahl, a leader of the local Duwamish and Suquamish peoples.","w":"A city between a fjord-like sound and a long lake; thin bridges floating across the lake; Rainier's white cone to the south-east.","f":["The bridges over Lake Washington float on concrete pontoons; one is the world's longest floating bridge, about 2.3 km.","The Klondike gold rush of 1897 made Seattle the main outfitting port for prospectors heading north.","Mount Rainier carries more glacier ice than any other peak in the contiguous United States."],"q":{"q":"Which volcano looms south-east of Seattle?","a":"Mount Rainier","x":["Mount Hood","Mount Shasta"]}},"Q16556":{"t":"A mile-square grid stamped across the Sonoran Desert","s":"Phoenix is geometry on sand: a vast grid of arterial roads a mile apart, stamped across a desert valley and broken by bare rocky mountains. Watch for canals and green squares of irrigated land. Long before the city, the Hohokam people dug hundreds of kilometres of canals here, and settlers of the 1860s named their new town after the bird that rises from its ashes.","w":"A huge, pale, regular grid in brown desert, with dark rocky peaks poking through; thin canals and green irrigated patches.","f":["The Hohokam canals, dug from about the 600s to the 1400s, formed one of the largest irrigation networks in pre-Columbian North America.","Summer afternoons here typically climb above 40 °C.","Camelback Mountain, surrounded by suburbs, is named for its hump-backed profile."],"q":{"q":"Why was Phoenix given its name?","a":"It rose on the ruins of ancient canals","x":["A bird-shaped mountain stands nearby","Its founder was called Phoenix"]}},"Q16552":{"t":"A deep blue bay, a sandy spit and Mexico next door","s":"San Diego wraps around a long, sheltered bay, closed off from the Pacific by the flat Coronado peninsula and the high ridge of Point Loma. Planes land almost among the downtown towers, at an airport right on the water. Spanish friars founded California's first mission here in 1769, and the Mexican border is only about 25 km to the south.","w":"A curved bay behind a pale sandy spit and a long ridge; the city spreads over canyon-cut mesas, merging with Tijuana to the south.","f":["In 1542 Juan Rodríguez Cabrillo sailed into this bay, the first European known to reach the US West Coast.","Coronado is not quite an island: a thin sand strip, the Silver Strand, ties it to the mainland."],"q":{"q":"What did Spanish friars found at San Diego in 1769?","a":"California's first mission","x":["California's first university","A silver mine"]}},"Q38022":{"t":"Where the muddy Missouri pours into the Mississippi","s":"Just north of St. Louis two of America's great rivers meet, and from above they often keep their colours for a while: the silt-laden Missouri alongside the darker Mississippi. French fur traders founded the town in 1764 and named it after King Louis IX. In 1804 Lewis and Clark set out from near the confluence to cross the continent.","w":"A city on the west bank of the Mississippi; to the north, two big brown rivers meet in a maze of channels and islands.","f":["The Eads Bridge of 1874 was one of the first major bridges built with steel as its main material.","A little further upstream the Illinois River joins the Mississippi too.","The Gateway Arch on the riverfront is 192 m tall but far too slender to pick out from cruise."],"q":{"q":"Which river joins the Mississippi just north of St. Louis?","a":"The Missouri","x":["The Ohio","The Arkansas"]}},"Q34404":{"t":"The Crescent City, cradled in a bend of the Mississippi","s":"New Orleans grew inside a tight curve of the Mississippi, which is why it's called the Crescent City. To its north spreads the broad, round Lake Pontchartrain, crossed by a bridge nearly 40 km long — a hairline over the water. The French founded the city in 1718; much of it now lies below sea level, ringed by levees.","w":"A city in a sharp bend of a wide brown river; a big round lake to the north crossed by a hairline bridge.","f":["The Lake Pontchartrain Causeway, about 38 km long, is one of the longest bridges over water in the world.","The city is named after Philippe, Duke of Orléans, who ruled France as regent when it was founded.","In 1803 the United States bought New Orleans as part of the vast Louisiana Purchase."],"q":{"q":"Why is New Orleans called the Crescent City?","a":"It sits in a bend of the Mississippi","x":["Its harbour is moon-shaped","Its main square is crescent-shaped"]}},"Q16557":{"t":"A prairie metropolis with an airport bigger than Manhattan","s":"Dallas rises from flat, open prairie, with no coast or mountain to give it shape — just freeways fanning out and a scatter of reservoirs. The Trinity River crosses it in a wide green floodway between levees. Towards Fort Worth, look for the runways of DFW, an airport that covers more ground than Manhattan. It all began with a trading post by a river ford in 1841.","w":"Flat sprawl without natural edges; a broad green floodway along the Trinity; a giant airport with long parallel runways to the north-west.","f":["Dallas–Fort Worth International covers about 70 km², more than the island of Manhattan.","Founder John Neely Bryan picked the spot for its natural ford across the Trinity River.","Almost all the lakes around Dallas are man-made reservoirs."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows through Dallas?","a":"The Trinity","x":["The Brazos","The Red River"]}},"Q171632":{"t":"On the neck of a vast lake studded with oil wells","s":"Maracaibo sits on the narrow strait where Lake Maracaibo, one of South America's biggest bodies of water, opens to the sea. Out on the lake stand countless oil wells, and at night their lights speckle the dark water. Look for the thin line of the bridge across the strait, over 8 km long. In 1499 an expedition sailing for Spain saw stilt houses here and called the land 'Little Venice' — Venezuela.","w":"A city on the west shore of a narrow strait; a long bridge across it; a vast lake opening to the south.","f":["Over the south-west of the lake, near the mouth of the Catatumbo River, lightning flickers on well over a hundred nights a year.","The General Rafael Urdaneta Bridge across the strait is about 8.7 km long and opened in 1962.","The lake is connected to the sea, so its water is slightly salty."],"q":{"q":"What gave Venezuela its name?","a":"Stilt villages on the lake","x":["A Venetian-born explorer","Canals in its capital"]}},"Q100":{"t":"A city that built its own ground out into the harbour","s":"Boston's outline is largely man-made. The Puritans who arrived in 1630 settled a small, hilly peninsula joined to the mainland by a thin neck; in the 1800s the mudflats around it were filled in to create whole districts. Look for the Charles River basin, the island-dotted harbour and the runways stretching into the sea just across from downtown.","w":"A compact city on a harbour full of islands; a wide river basin to the west; runways reaching into the water to the east.","f":["The Back Bay district stands on tidal flats that were filled in with gravel in the 1800s.","Harvard, founded in 1636 across the Charles River, is the oldest university in the United States.","Logan Airport was also built largely on land reclaimed from the harbour."],"q":{"q":"What was Boston's Back Bay before the 1800s?","a":"Tidal mudflats","x":["Farmland","Forest"]}},"Q49255":{"t":"A city at the head of a bay stitched with causeways","s":"Tampa sits at the top of a big, branching bay, and from above the whole area looks stitched together: long causeways and bridges run straight across the shallow water to St. Petersburg on the peninsula opposite. In 1886 cigar makers moved their factories here, and hand-rolled cigars made Tampa famous. Look for the dredged shipping channel running out towards the Gulf.","w":"A city at the northern head of a wide, branching bay; several thin causeways across the water; a peninsula city to the west.","f":["Tampa Bay is Florida's largest open-water estuary, covering about 1,000 km².","Ybor City, the old cigar district, once turned out hundreds of millions of cigars a year."],"q":{"q":"Which city faces Tampa across the bay?","a":"St. Petersburg","x":["Orlando","Jacksonville"]}},"Q1345":{"t":"Penn's grid, laid out between two rivers in 1682","s":"Philadelphia began as a plan on paper: in 1682 William Penn drew a neat grid between the Delaware and Schuylkill rivers, with a central square and four smaller ones. That rectangle is still the heart of the city. The Declaration of Independence was adopted here in 1776, and for a decade from 1790 Philadelphia served as the nation's capital.","w":"A dense grid between two rivers; the wide Delaware to the east with the airport on its bank; a long green park along the Schuylkill.","f":["Four of Penn's five original squares are still parks; the fifth holds City Hall.","The name is Greek for 'brotherly love'.","Fairmount Park, along the Schuylkill, is one of the largest urban park systems in the US."],"q":{"q":"Between which two rivers did Penn lay out Philadelphia?","a":"The Delaware and the Schuylkill","x":["The Hudson and the Harlem","The Potomac and the Anacostia"]}},"Q12439":{"t":"The US city with Canada to its south","s":"Detroit comes with a quirk of the map: across the Detroit River, due south of downtown, lies Canada. The river is really a strait linking Lake St. Clair to Lake Erie, and the French word for strait, détroit, gave the city its name. Antoine de la Mothe Cadillac founded a fort here in 1701. Two centuries later carmakers turned it into the Motor City.","w":"A city along a broad river channel between two lakes; Windsor on the opposite bank; a long wooded island in the river.","f":["Belle Isle, the island park in the Detroit River, is larger than New York's Central Park.","The Ambassador Bridge to Windsor carries more trade than any other US–Canada border crossing."],"q":{"q":"In which direction from downtown Detroit does Canada lie?","a":"South","x":["North","West"]}},"Q39450":{"t":"A frontier city between mudflats and the Chugach peaks","s":"Anchorage fills a wedge of flat land between two long arms of Cook Inlet, with the Chugach Mountains rising right behind it. At low tide the inlet drains to glistening grey mudflats: the tides here can rise and fall by more than 9 m. On a clear day, look north for Denali, about 200 km away. The city began in 1914 as a tent camp for railway builders.","w":"A grid city on a lowland wedge between two grey-brown inlets, backed by snowy mountains; a lake full of floatplanes beside the airport.","f":["Lake Hood, next to the international airport, is one of the world's busiest seaplane bases.","Turnagain Arm got its name in 1778, when James Cook's ships had to turn back there.","In Turnagain Arm the incoming tide sometimes arrives as a bore — a single travelling wave."],"q":{"q":"How did Turnagain Arm near Anchorage get its name?","a":"Cook's ships had to turn back there","x":["It bends back on itself","Whalers used it as a turning point"]}},"Q9022":{"t":"Between a deep canyon and Mexico's largest lake","s":"Guadalajara spreads across a high, open valley about 1,560 m above sea level. Its northern edge ends abruptly at a deep canyon cut by the Río Grande de Santiago, and to the south lies Lake Chapala, Mexico's largest freshwater lake. West of the city, look for blue-grey rows of agave: this is the country around the town of Tequila.","w":"A large city on a flat high valley; a dark canyon along its northern edge; a big lake to the south.","f":["The canyon at the northern edge, the Barranca de Huentitán, is roughly 600 m deep.","The city was moved several times before settling on its present site in 1542.","Blue agave for tequila covers the hills around the town of Tequila, about 60 km north-west."],"q":{"q":"Which lake lies just south of Guadalajara?","a":"Lake Chapala","x":["Lake Pátzcuaro","Lake Texcoco"]}},"Q125293":{"t":"A colonial grid ringed by great volcanoes","s":"Few cities have a volcanic horizon like Puebla's. To the west stand snowy Popocatépetl and Iztaccíhuatl, to the north-east the lone cone of La Malinche, and far to the east the white peak of Orizaba, Mexico's highest mountain. The Spanish founded Puebla in 1531 as a brand-new planned town, and its old centre is still a tidy grid.","w":"A large city on a high plain with big volcanoes on the horizon; La Malinche's isolated cone close by to the north-east.","f":["Next door in Cholula stands the largest pyramid in the world by volume, so overgrown it looks like a hill.","Pico de Orizaba, visible to the east on clear days, rises to about 5,600 m.","Puebla is famous for Talavera, a glazed pottery tradition brought from Spain in the 1500s."],"q":{"q":"Which nearby town has the largest pyramid in the world by volume?","a":"Cholula","x":["Teotihuacan","Tula"]}},"Q84":{"t":"A dark river coiling through a sea of lights","s":"Find the Thames and London falls into place: a brown ribbon looping east through an enormous grey-green sprawl, then widening towards the North Sea. The M25 motorway draws a rough ring around it all. The Romans bridged the river here almost two thousand years ago, and the city has spread outward from that crossing ever since.","w":"The looping Thames running east to its estuary; square reservoirs glint beside Heathrow on the western edge.","f":["The M25 ring motorway is about 188 km long; at night it traces a faint loop of lights around the city.","The Thames is tidal right through London: in the centre the water rises and falls by several metres twice a day.","The Romans founded Londinium on the north bank of the Thames around AD 50."],"q":{"q":"What was London called in Roman times?","a":"Londinium","x":["Lutetia","Eboracum"]}},"Q406":{"t":"One city, two continents, one blue thread between them","s":"Look for a thin blue line cutting a vast city in two: the Bosphorus, with Europe on one bank and Asia on the other. The Golden Horn curls into the old town on the European side. As Constantinople, this was an imperial capital for more than a thousand years.","w":"A narrow strait running north–south between the Black Sea and the Sea of Marmara.","f":["The Bosphorus is about 30 km long and well under a kilometre wide at its narrowest.","Three suspension bridges cross the strait; from cruise they show as fine pale lines.","At night the two shores glow as one ribbon of light along the water."],"q":{"q":"Which strait splits Istanbul between Europe and Asia?","a":"The Bosphorus","x":["The Dardanelles","The Strait of Messina"]}},"Q3692":{"t":"A grid of lights dropped into the Arabian desert","s":"Riyadh sits close to the middle of the Arabian Peninsula, hundreds of kilometres from any sea. From above it is a vast beige grid of broad avenues, edged on the west by the green thread of Wadi Hanifa. Its name comes from the Arabic for 'gardens' — the oases this valley once watered.","w":"A huge rectangular street grid in brown desert, with the long Tuwaiq escarpment running north–south to the west.","f":["The Tuwaiq escarpment west of the city curves for roughly 800 km across central Arabia.","On Riyadh's north-western edge, the mud-brick At-Turaif district of Diriyah is a UNESCO World Heritage site.","The city stands about 600 metres above sea level on the Najd plateau."],"q":{"q":"What does the name Riyadh mean in Arabic?","a":"Gardens","x":["Sands","Stars"]}},"Q5465":{"t":"A flat-topped mountain near the tip of Africa","s":"Look for a mountain sliced flat like a table, with the city curled around its feet and the sea on both sides. South of it, a rocky finger of land runs down to the Cape of Good Hope. On many summer days a white cloud spills over the summit like a tablecloth, then melts away on the way down.","w":"A flat grey mountain block above a curved bay, a rocky peninsula running south, and a low island out in the bay.","f":["Table Mountain's flat summit reaches about 1,085 metres — roughly a tenth of your cruising height.","The low island in Table Bay is Robben Island, a UNESCO World Heritage site since 1999.","Cape Town is South Africa's oldest city, founded in 1652 as a supply station for Dutch ships."],"q":{"q":"In which year did the Dutch East India Company found Cape Town?","a":"1652","x":["1588","1714"]}},"Q649":{"t":"Rings within rings, like a cut tree trunk","s":"Moscow is laid out like a tree trunk seen in cross-section: ring roads nested one inside another around the old centre, with radial highways running out like spokes. At night the whole pattern glows amber and white. Through it winds the Moskva River in big, lazy loops. The city first appears in a chronicle in 1147.","w":"Concentric road rings with straight spokes radiating out; the winding Moskva River crosses the centre from north-west to south-east.","f":["The MKAD, Moscow's outer ring motorway, is about 109 km long.","The inner Garden Ring runs for about 16 km around the historic centre.","The city and its river share one name: in Russian, both are called Moskva."],"q":{"q":"In which year is Moscow first mentioned in a chronicle?","a":"1147","x":["1240","1380"]}},"Q8673":{"t":"A megacity stitched together across a lagoon","s":"Lagos is as much water as land. Look for a broad lagoon behind a long, straight Atlantic beach, with the city spilling over the islands and shores all around it. A thin bridge strides across the water for kilometres. Portuguese sailors named the place after these lakes, and today it is one of Africa's largest cities.","w":"A wide brown-grey lagoon behind a ruler-straight surf coast, dense city on its islands and a long bridge across the water.","f":["The Third Mainland Bridge runs about 11.8 km across Lagos Lagoon, linking the islands to the mainland.","Lagos was Nigeria's capital until 1991, when the government moved to Abuja.","In Yoruba, the city's own name is Eko."],"q":{"q":"What does the name Lagos mean in Portuguese?","a":"Lakes","x":["Harbours","Palms"]}},"Q220":{"t":"The Eternal City, framed by old volcanoes","s":"Rome lies about 25 km inland on the Tiber, which snakes through the centre in tight bends. A motorway ring, the Grande Raccordo Anulare, loops around the city. Look beyond it: the round lakes to the north-west and south-east fill the craters of extinct volcanoes. Tradition dates the city's founding to 753 BC.","w":"A sprawl on a winding river, framed by the round crater lakes of Bracciano to the north-west and the Alban Hills to the south-east.","f":["The Grande Raccordo Anulare ring road is about 68 km long.","Vatican City, the world's smallest country, lies entirely inside Rome.","Lake Bracciano, north-west of the city, fills an old volcanic basin about 9 km across."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows through Rome?","a":"The Tiber","x":["The Arno","The Po"]}},"Q3870":{"t":"A highland capital with lions on its doorstep","s":"Nairobi sits on a high plateau at about 1,800 metres, just east of the Great Rift Valley. It began in 1899 as a depot on the railway from Mombasa to Lake Victoria, and its name comes from a Maasai phrase for 'cool water'. On its southern edge, open grassland is a national park where lions and giraffes roam within sight of town.","w":"A dense city with an open tawny plain — the national park — on its southern side; the Ngong Hills rise to the south-west.","f":["Nairobi National Park begins only about 7 km from the city centre.","West of the city the land drops sharply into the Great Rift Valley.","The city lies less than 150 km south of the equator, yet its altitude keeps nights cool."],"q":{"q":"What does the Maasai name behind 'Nairobi' mean?","a":"Cool water","x":["Red earth","High hill"]}},"Q85":{"t":"Where the green Nile valley opens into the delta","s":"Cairo marks the hinge of Egypt. South of the city the Nile runs in a narrow green strip through desert; just to the north it fans out into the delta. The city fills both banks, with the Giza plateau and its pyramids on the western edge, where the houses stop and the Sahara begins.","w":"A huge grey-beige city on the Nile, exactly where the thin green valley widens into the triangle of the delta.","f":["Cairo was founded as al-Qahira, 'the Victorious', by the Fatimid dynasty in 969.","The line between irrigated fields and bare desert around Cairo is razor-sharp, even from cruise height.","The Great Pyramid of Giza was the tallest human-made structure on Earth for more than 3,800 years."],"q":{"q":"Which dynasty founded Cairo in 969?","a":"The Fatimids","x":["The Ottomans","The Mamluks"]}},"Q90":{"t":"An oval city inside a ring, with a river in loops","s":"Paris proper is compact: a neat oval held inside the Boulevard Périphérique, with suburbs sprawling far beyond it. The Seine enters from the south-east, curves through the centre past two small islands, then leaves in a series of great loops towards the Channel. The Romans called the settlement Lutetia.","w":"An oval ring road enclosing the dense centre, with the Seine snaking through it and meandering west in big loops.","f":["The Boulevard Périphérique is about 35 km long and follows the line of the city's 19th-century fortifications.","Inside the ring, Paris covers only about 105 km².","The city began on the Île de la Cité, the larger of the two islands in the Seine."],"q":{"q":"About how long is the Boulevard Périphérique around Paris?","a":"About 35 km","x":["About 70 km","About 12 km"]}},"Q1899":{"t":"Green hills on one bank, flat plain on the other","s":"The Dnipro splits Kyiv in two, and its banks could hardly differ more: steep wooded hills on the west, where the old city grew, and low flat land to the east. Sandy islands and side channels lie between them. Upstream, the river swells into a long reservoir. A thousand years ago this was the capital of Kyivan Rus.","w":"A broad river with sandy islands running north–south; the hilly old city on the west bank, newer districts on the flat east bank.","f":["Just north of the city the Dnipro widens into the Kyiv Reservoir, more than 100 km long.","The Kyiv Pechersk Lavra, a cave monastery founded in 1051, is a UNESCO World Heritage site.","Large wooded islands in the Dnipro, such as Trukhaniv, sit right opposite the city centre."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows through Kyiv?","a":"The Dnipro","x":["The Dniester","The Southern Bug"]}},"Q612":{"t":"A palm tree drawn on the sea in sand","s":"Few cities are so easy to spot. Off Dubai's coast, a palm tree has been drawn on the sea in sand, its fronds ringed by a crescent breakwater. Further out, a scatter of islets imitates a map of the world. Onshore, towers line the coast for kilometres, and the old town hugs a winding creek.","w":"The palm-shaped island off a straight desert coast, with the cluster of 'World' islands further out to sea.","f":["Palm Jumeirah was built from sand dredged from the sea floor and is roughly 5 km across.","The World islands are an archipelago of about 300 artificial islands laid out like a world map.","Dubai Creek, a natural inlet, was the heart of the old pearling and trading town."],"q":{"q":"What shape is Dubai's best-known artificial island?","a":"A palm tree","x":["A seashell","A falcon"]}},"Q2807":{"t":"A capital on a high plateau, with snowy peaks behind","s":"Madrid lies almost at the centre of Spain, on a dry plateau more than 600 metres above the sea. Look for ring motorways drawing ever wider loops around it, and for the Sierra de Guadarrama to the north-west, often white-capped in winter. Philip II moved his royal court to this small town in 1561.","w":"A dense city on a tan plateau ringed by motorways, with the Guadarrama mountains rising to the north-west.","f":["At about 650 metres, Madrid is the highest capital city in the European Union.","The Puerta del Sol in the centre is Kilometre Zero for Spain's radial national roads.","The city's own river, the Manzanares, is so small it barely shows from the air."],"q":{"q":"Which mountains rise north-west of Madrid?","a":"Sierra de Guadarrama","x":["Sierra Nevada","Picos de Europa"]}},"Q71":{"t":"Where the Rhône slips out of a crescent lake","s":"Geneva sits at the south-western tip of Lake Geneva, where the Rhône leaves the lake and heads for France. The city is almost surrounded by French territory. On a clear day look south-east to the snowy Mont Blanc massif, and north-west to the long, dark wall of the Jura.","w":"The narrow pointed end of a large crescent-shaped lake, with a river leaving it; the Jura ridge lies to the north-west.","f":["The canton of Geneva shares about 100 km of border with France and only a few kilometres with the rest of Switzerland.","Underground, just west of the city, CERN's particle accelerator runs in a 27 km ring beneath the French border.","Lake Geneva is about 73 km long, curving like a crescent between Switzerland and France."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows out of Lake Geneva at Geneva?","a":"The Rhône","x":["The Rhine","The Aare"]}},"Q1754":{"t":"A city on islands where a lake meets the sea","s":"Stockholm is spread over fourteen islands at the point where Lake Mälaren drains into the Baltic. From above it is a jigsaw of water, granite and dark pine forest. To the east the archipelago takes over: tens of thousands of islands and skerries scattered towards the open sea. The city is first recorded in 1252.","w":"A jigsaw of islands and channels between a big lake to the west and a vast archipelago spreading east to the Baltic.","f":["The Stockholm archipelago contains roughly 30,000 islands, islets and rocks.","Lake Mälaren sits slightly higher than the Baltic, and its water drains out through the city's channels.","The land here is still rising after the last Ice Age, by around half a centimetre a year."],"q":{"q":"About how many islands make up the Stockholm archipelago?","a":"About 30,000","x":["About 300","About 3,000"]}},"Q3718":{"t":"The westernmost city of mainland Africa","s":"Dakar crowns the Cap-Vert peninsula, a hook of land pushing out into the Atlantic, the farthest west that mainland Africa reaches. From above, the city fills the whole triangle, with sea on three sides and the ochre Sahel behind. Just offshore lies the small island of Gorée, a UNESCO site and memorial to the Atlantic slave trade.","w":"A triangular peninsula jutting west into the Atlantic, built over almost entirely, with a small island off its south-eastern shore.","f":["Pointe des Almadies, on Dakar's peninsula, is the westernmost point of mainland Africa.","Two low volcanic hills, the Mamelles, rise near the western shore; one carries a lighthouse.","Gorée Island became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 1978."],"q":{"q":"Dakar stands on which peninsula?","a":"Cap-Vert","x":["Cap Bon","Sinai"]}},"Q34647":{"t":"A city built on gold, its old mine dumps in view","s":"Johannesburg exists because of gold. It sprang up in 1886, after a rich reef was found along the Witwatersrand, a long east–west ridge on the high veld. Look along the city's southern side for flat-topped yellowish mounds — old mine dumps — strung out in a line that traces the reef buried below.","w":"A sprawling city on a high grassy plateau; a line of pale yellow mine dumps runs east–west along its south.","f":["Johannesburg lies about 1,750 metres above sea level.","Witwatersrand means 'ridge of white waters' in Afrikaans.","Some Witwatersrand gold mines reach about 4 km below the surface, among the deepest in the world."],"q":{"q":"Which discovery led to the founding of Johannesburg in 1886?","a":"Gold","x":["Diamonds","Coal"]}},"Q727":{"t":"A canal city on land won from the water","s":"Amsterdam grew from a 13th-century dam on the river Amstel, and water still shapes everything you see: the broad IJ channel along its north side, the grey expanse of the Markermeer to the east, and a flat patchwork of polders all around. Many of those green rectangles lie below sea level.","w":"Flat green polders cut by straight ditches, the city on a broad channel, and a large grey lake to its east.","f":["Schiphol airport lies about 3 metres below sea level, on the bed of a lake drained in the mid-1800s.","The ring of 17th-century canals became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 2010.","The North Sea Canal, opened in 1876, links the city's harbour with the sea about 25 km to the west."],"q":{"q":"Which river gave Amsterdam its name?","a":"The Amstel","x":["The Maas","The IJssel"]}},"Q7903":{"t":"A white city strung along the Atlantic surf","s":"Casablanca stretches along a flat Atlantic shore, a pale spread of buildings pressed right up to the surf line. Look for the long breakwaters of its port, one of the largest in Africa, curving out to sea. The name is Spanish for 'white house', echoing the Arabic Dar al-Bayda, and from the air the city does look mostly white.","w":"A pale city along a straight Atlantic coast, with a big harbour enclosed by long breakwaters.","f":["The minaret of the Hassan II Mosque, on the seafront, is about 210 metres tall.","Part of the Hassan II Mosque stands on a platform built out over the Atlantic.","Casablanca is Morocco's largest city, though the capital is Rabat, about 90 km up the coast."],"q":{"q":"What does the name Casablanca mean?","a":"White house","x":["White sands","Blue harbour"]}},"Q64":{"t":"A flat city of lakes, forests and one big green void","s":"Berlin spreads across a flat, sandy plain left behind by Ice Age glaciers, and water is everywhere: the Spree winding through the centre, a chain of Havel lakes in the west, the broad Müggelsee in the east. Dark pine forests reach deep into the city. Look too for a vast open rectangle south of the centre — Tempelhof, an airport turned park.","w":"A huge flat city scattered with lakes and dark forest; a large open green rectangle lies just south of the centre.","f":["The A10 Berliner Ring motorway around the city is about 196 km long, one of Europe's longest ring roads.","Tempelhofer Feld, the old Tempelhof airport, is now an open park of about 300 hectares.","Berlin covers about 890 km², more than eight times the area of Paris inside its ring road."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows through the centre of Berlin?","a":"The Spree","x":["The Elbe","The Oder"]}},"Q3838":{"t":"Two capitals facing each other across a great river","s":"Kinshasa lies on the south bank of the Congo, where the river spreads into a vast, lake-like pool dotted with sandy islands. On the far bank is Brazzaville, the capital of another country. Just downstream, the river leaves the pool and plunges into a long run of rapids. Henry Morton Stanley set up a trading post here in 1881 and named it Léopoldville.","w":"A sprawling city on the south shore of a broad, island-studded widening of the Congo River, with another city on the opposite bank.","f":["Apart from Rome and the Vatican, Kinshasa and Brazzaville are the world's closest pair of capital cities.","Pool Malebo, the river's widening here, is about 35 km long and more than 20 km wide.","Below the city, the Livingstone Falls — a chain of rapids — lower the Congo by about 270 metres."],"q":{"q":"Which capital faces Kinshasa across the Congo River?","a":"Brazzaville","x":["Bangui","Luanda"]}},"Q1524":{"t":"A white city poured into a basin down to the sea","s":"Athens pours across a basin ringed by bare mountains, spilling down to the Saronic Gulf at the port of Piraeus. From above it is an almost unbroken white-and-beige carpet. Look at the mountain to the north-east: pale scars on its flanks are marble quarries, worked since antiquity, when they supplied the stone for the Parthenon.","w":"A dense white city in a mountain-rimmed basin opening south-west onto the sea, with ship wakes fanning out from Piraeus.","f":["The Parthenon's marble came from Mount Penteli, about 15 km north-east of the Acropolis.","Piraeus has served as the port of Athens since the 5th century BC, when the Long Walls linked the two.","The city takes its name from the goddess Athena, its patron in Greek myth."],"q":{"q":"Which mountain supplied the marble for the Parthenon?","a":"Mount Penteli","x":["Mount Olympus","Mount Parnassus"]}},"Q1530":{"t":"A city threaded on the loops of the Tigris","s":"Baghdad is threaded on the Tigris, which swings through it in great looping bends, with dark palm groves along both banks. To the west the Euphrates comes within a few dozen kilometres: the two rivers of Mesopotamia draw closer here than almost anywhere else. The Abbasid caliph al-Mansur founded the city in 762 as a perfectly round walled town.","w":"A flat, dust-coloured city on a wide, deeply looping river, with dark green palm groves along its banks.","f":["Al-Mansur named his new round city Madinat al-Salam, the City of Peace.","Near Baghdad the Tigris and the Euphrates run less than 50 km apart.","In the 9th century Baghdad's House of Wisdom drew scholars who translated Greek science into Arabic."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows through Baghdad?","a":"The Tigris","x":["The Euphrates","The Orontes"]}},"Q3624":{"t":"A capital in the clouds, ringed by eucalyptus hills","s":"Addis Ababa sits on the Ethiopian Highlands at about 2,350 metres, one of the highest capitals in the world. The hills to its north are dark with eucalyptus, a tree brought in during the 1890s to give the young city firewood. Emperor Menelik II and Empress Taytu founded the city in 1886, near a group of hot springs.","w":"A sprawling city on a high green plateau, with dark eucalyptus-covered hills rising along its northern edge.","f":["The African Union has its headquarters in Addis Ababa.","The eucalyptus around the city came from Australia and now covers much of the Entoto hills.","At this altitude water boils at about 92 °C rather than 100 °C."],"q":{"q":"What does the name Addis Ababa mean in Amharic?","a":"New flower","x":["High city","Spring water"]}},"Q3616":{"t":"A city tilting down from a wall of mountains","s":"Tehran lies at the foot of the Alborz, a steep mountain wall that shuts the city off from the Caspian Sea to the north. The whole city tilts: its northern districts sit hundreds of metres higher than the southern ones, which fade into dry plain. To the north-east, look for a lone white cone above the range — Damavand, the highest peak in Iran.","w":"A huge grey city pressed against a high mountain wall to its north; a lone snowy volcanic cone stands out to the north-east.","f":["Mount Damavand, about 70 km north-east of the city, reaches roughly 5,600 metres.","Tehran became the capital in 1786 under Agha Mohammad Khan, founder of the Qajar dynasty.","Damavand is a volcano, and the highest in Asia."],"q":{"q":"Which volcano rises north-east of Tehran?","a":"Damavand","x":["Ararat","Sabalan"]}},"Q1741":{"t":"Where the Danube runs in straight parallel lines","s":"Vienna's river is easy to find but strange to look at: the Danube passes the city in straight parallel channels with a long, thin island between them. That is the work of engineers; the old river once wandered freely across its floodplain. To the west, the wooded hills of the Vienna Woods are the last foothills of the Alps.","w":"The Danube as two straight parallel channels with a slim island between them, north-east of the centre; forested hills to the west.","f":["Danube Island, the thin strip between the channels, is about 21 km long and was built for flood protection.","Vienna has working vineyards inside its city limits, and wine-making here goes back centuries.","For centuries Vienna was the main seat of the Habsburg dynasty."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows through Vienna?","a":"The Danube","x":["The Elbe","The Vltava"]}},"Q3640":{"t":"A capital on the bare steppe of Anatolia","s":"Ankara spreads over a bowl of hills in the middle of the Anatolian plateau, nearly 900 metres up and ringed by dry, tawny steppe. Its old name, Angora, lives on in the soft wool of goats, cats and rabbits once bred here. Far to the south you may catch a gleam of white: Lake Tuz, a vast, shallow salt lake.","w":"A large city in a bowl of low hills on bare, tan steppe; far to the south, the white sheet of a salt lake.","f":["Ankara replaced Istanbul as Turkey's capital in 1923.","In Roman times the city was Ancyra, capital of the province of Galatia.","Lake Tuz is so shallow that in summer much of it dries into a crust of white salt."],"q":{"q":"Which old name of Ankara was given to a soft wool?","a":"Angora","x":["Merino","Cashmere"]}},"Q1781":{"t":"Buda's hills and Pest's plain, stitched by bridges","s":"The Danube runs straight north to south through Budapest and splits it into two characters: the wooded hills of Buda on the west bank and the flat, dense grid of Pest on the east. A slim, leafy island, Margaret Island, sits in mid-river near the centre. The towns on the two banks were only merged into one city in 1873.","w":"A broad river flowing south, green hills on its west bank, flat city to the east, and a narrow island in mid-stream.","f":["Budapest sits on a geological fault that feeds more than a hundred thermal springs.","South of the city the Danube splits around Csepel Island, which is nearly 50 km long.","Margaret Island is about 2.5 km long and once held a medieval convent."],"q":{"q":"In which year were Buda, Óbuda and Pest united?","a":"1873","x":["1848","1896"]}},"Q2471":{"t":"An ancient capital on a high mountain plateau","s":"Sanaa lies in a high basin among the mountains of Yemen, at about 2,250 metres. From above it is an earth-coloured patch between bare, folded ridges. Its old city, packed with tall tower houses of mud brick and stone trimmed in white, has been inhabited for more than 2,500 years.","w":"An earth-toned city in a broad upland basin, hemmed in by bare, rugged mountains on its east and west.","f":["The Old City of Sanaa has been a UNESCO World Heritage site since 1986.","West of the city rises Jabal an-Nabi Shu'ayb, at about 3,660 m the highest peak in the Arabian Peninsula.","Sanaa's tower houses are several storeys tall, with white gypsum friezes framing their windows."],"q":{"q":"Roughly how high above sea level is Sanaa?","a":"About 2,250 m","x":["About 750 m","About 4,000 m"]}},"Q1492":{"t":"A perfect grid between the hills and the sea","s":"Barcelona fills a coastal plain boxed in by two rivers and a line of wooded hills. Its heart is unmistakable: the Eixample, a vast chessboard of near-identical blocks laid out in the 1860s. Look for one broad avenue slicing across it at an angle, and for the hill of Montjuïc rising straight up from the port.","w":"A grid of square blocks between green hills and the Mediterranean, crossed by a long diagonal avenue, with a rounded hill by the port.","f":["The Eixample was planned in 1859 by the engineer Ildefons Cerdà.","Each Eixample block is about 113 metres across, with its corners cut off at an angle.","The Avinguda Diagonal cuts across the grid for about 11 km."],"q":{"q":"Which hill rises beside Barcelona's port?","a":"Montjuïc","x":["Tibidabo","Montserrat"]}},"Q19660":{"t":"A flat city with a string of lakes along its north","s":"Bucharest lies on the flat Wallachian plain, about 60 km north of the Danube. Look along its northern side for a chain of lakes strung along the little Colentina river, like beads on a thread. The first document to mention the city was signed here in 1459 by Vlad III — better known as Vlad the Impaler.","w":"A large, flat city on an open plain, with a curving chain of small lakes along its northern edge.","f":["Its elegant boulevards once earned Bucharest the nickname 'Little Paris'.","Bucharest became the capital of Wallachia in 1659.","The Dâmbovița river crosses the centre in a straight, walled channel."],"q":{"q":"Which ruler signed the first document naming Bucharest, in 1459?","a":"Vlad the Impaler","x":["Stephen the Great","Michael the Brave"]}},"Q41183":{"t":"One of the world's oldest cities, on a limestone plain","s":"Aleppo is among the oldest continuously inhabited cities on Earth, a stop on caravan routes between the Mediterranean and Mesopotamia for thousands of years. From above it is a large pale city on a rolling limestone plain. Its old centre grew up around a citadel on an ancient mound. South-east of the city lies a shallow salt lake, Jabbul.","w":"A large pale city on a dry, rolling plain; a shallow salt lake glints to the south-east.","f":["The Ancient City of Aleppo has been a UNESCO World Heritage site since 1986.","The city has long been famous for soap made with olive and laurel oil, known worldwide as Aleppo soap.","Aleppo lies roughly 100 km inland from the Mediterranean."],"q":{"q":"Which salt lake lies south-east of Aleppo?","a":"Lake Jabbul","x":["Lake Tuz","Lake Urmia"]}},"Q3766":{"t":"An ancient oasis city beneath a bare mountain","s":"Damascus owes its existence to one small river. The Barada flows down from the Anti-Lebanon mountains and spreads into the Ghouta, an oasis of orchards that has fed the city for thousands of years. The city lies at the foot of bare Mount Qasioun, and in winter the snowy ridge of Mount Hermon stands out to the south-west.","w":"A large city between green orchards and desert, backed by a bare mountain on its north-west side.","f":["From 661 to 750 Damascus was the capital of the Umayyad Caliphate, which stretched from Spain to Central Asia.","Mount Hermon, south-west of the city, rises to about 2,800 metres and often keeps snow into spring.","The Ancient City of Damascus has been a UNESCO World Heritage site since 1979."],"q":{"q":"Which fabric takes its name from Damascus?","a":"Damask","x":["Muslin","Calico"]}},"Q72":{"t":"A city at the end of a long, slender lake","s":"Zürich sits at the northern tip of Lake Zurich, a slim lake that curves about 40 km south-east towards the Alps. The river Limmat drains out of it straight through the centre. On a clear day, look past the lake to the snowy wall of the Glarus Alps. The Romans knew the settlement as Turicum.","w":"The northern end of a long, narrow lake curving south-east, a river leaving it through the city, and the Alps beyond.","f":["Lake Zurich is about 40 km long but nowhere more than about 4 km wide.","The Uetliberg, the wooded ridge on the city's western edge, rises to about 870 metres.","Zürich is Switzerland's largest city, although the federal government sits in Bern."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows out of Lake Zurich through the city?","a":"The Limmat","x":["The Aare","The Reuss"]}},"Q597":{"t":"Mainland Europe's westernmost capital, on a wide estuary","s":"Lisbon climbs its hills on the north shore of the Tagus, just where the river's broad inland lake narrows into a channel to the Atlantic. Two long bridges cross the water: a red suspension bridge at the narrows and, upstream, a thin line of a bridge that runs on for kilometres. No capital in mainland Europe lies further west.","w":"A hilly city on the north shore of a broad estuary, with a long bridge across its upper reaches and another at the narrows.","f":["The Vasco da Gama Bridge across the Tagus is about 12 km long, one of the longest in Europe.","The inland part of the estuary is so wide that locals call it the Mar da Palha, the 'Sea of Straw'.","Cabo da Roca, west of Lisbon, is the westernmost point of mainland Europe."],"q":{"q":"Which river forms the broad estuary at Lisbon?","a":"The Tagus","x":["The Douro","The Guadiana"]}},"Q1963":{"t":"Where the Blue Nile and the White Nile become one","s":"Khartoum stands at one of the great river meetings of the world. The Blue Nile, coming down from the Ethiopian Highlands, joins the White Nile, which has travelled all the way from Lake Victoria. Their waters can run side by side in two shades for a while before they mix. Around the confluence, three cities face each other across the rivers.","w":"Two large rivers meeting in a Y around a green island, with a city on each of the three banks.","f":["Tuti Island sits right at the confluence of the two Niles.","Most of the water in the Nile below Khartoum comes from the Blue Nile, especially in the flood season.","The three cities around the confluence are Khartoum, Omdurman and Khartoum North."],"q":{"q":"Which two rivers meet at Khartoum?","a":"The Blue Nile and White Nile","x":["The Nile and the Atbara","The White Nile and the Sobat"]}},"Q374365":{"t":"The Red Sea gateway, fringed by turquoise reefs","s":"Jeddah stretches for kilometres along the Red Sea, a long, narrow city pressed between desert and a band of turquoise shallows. Those pale greens and blues offshore are coral reefs. For centuries pilgrims bound for Mecca, about 70 km inland, have landed here, and the old port town grew rich on their arrival.","w":"A long city along a straight Red Sea coast, edged by bright turquoise reefs, with brown desert and hills behind.","f":["Historic Jeddah, an old quarter of houses built from coral stone, became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 2014.","King Fahd's Fountain, off the city's seafront, throws water more than 250 metres into the air.","The reefs along this coast are part of one of the warmest and saltiest seas on Earth."],"q":{"q":"Which sea does Jeddah face?","a":"The Red Sea","x":["The Arabian Sea","The Persian Gulf"]}},"Q5806":{"t":"A holy city in a valley of dark, bare mountains","s":"Mecca lies in narrow valleys among dark, jagged mountains, about 70 km inland from the Red Sea. For Muslims it is the holiest city of Islam: they turn towards it in prayer, and millions come each year on pilgrimage. East of the city, look for the Mina valley, carpeted with rows of white tents that house pilgrims during the Hajj.","w":"A city squeezed into winding valleys between bare black hills, with a valley of white tents stretching away to the east.","f":["The Prophet Muhammad was born in Mecca around AD 570.","The tent city in Mina holds tens of thousands of fireproof tents in neat rows.","The plain of Arafat, a key stop of the pilgrimage, lies about 20 km south-east of the city."],"q":{"q":"What covers the valley of Mina, east of Mecca?","a":"Rows of white tents","x":["Solar panels","Date palm groves"]}},"Q585":{"t":"A capital at the end of a long, island-dotted fjord","s":"Oslo sits at the head of the Oslofjord, where a long arm of the sea finally runs out between forested hills. The city fills a green amphitheatre, and dark woods scattered with lakes begin right at its edge. For three centuries it was called Christiania, after the Danish-Norwegian king Christian IV, who rebuilt it in 1624.","w":"The inner end of a long fjord scattered with small islands, with the city in a bowl of dark, forested hills.","f":["The Oslofjord runs about 100 km from the city south to the Skagerrak.","The city took back its medieval name, Oslo, in 1925.","Much of the municipality is protected forest, the Marka, laced with lakes and ski trails."],"q":{"q":"What was Oslo called from 1624 until 1925?","a":"Christiania","x":["Nidaros","Bjørgvin"]}},"Q5468":{"t":"A warm harbour city on the Indian Ocean","s":"Durban wraps around a big natural harbour on South Africa's east coast, sheltered by a long, wooded ridge called the Bluff. North of the port, a straight line of beaches faces the Indian Ocean, where the warm Agulhas Current keeps the water mild all year. Inland, rolling green hills are patched with sugar-cane fields.","w":"A near-enclosed bay with a narrow entrance, a green ridge along its south side, and a long straight beach to the north.","f":["The city was named in 1835 after Sir Benjamin D'Urban, then governor of the Cape Colony.","Durban's harbour is one of the busiest ports in Africa.","The warm Agulhas Current flows south-west along this coast, one of the fastest ocean currents on Earth."],"q":{"q":"Which ocean does Durban face?","a":"The Indian Ocean","x":["The Atlantic Ocean","The Southern Ocean"]}},"Q656":{"t":"Peter's city on the islands of the Neva delta","s":"Saint Petersburg spreads over the islands of the Neva delta at the eastern tip of the Gulf of Finland. Look for a thin line crossing the gulf to the west: a barrier about 25 km long, running through Kotlin Island, that shields the city from storm surges. Peter the Great founded the city on this marshy ground in 1703.","w":"A big city on a branching river delta at the head of a gulf, with a long dam crossing the water through an island.","f":["The Neva is only about 74 km long, yet it drains Lake Ladoga, the largest lake in Europe.","The city is built on dozens of islands, divided by rivers and canals.","Around midsummer, the 'white nights' keep the sky light even at midnight."],"q":{"q":"In which year did Peter the Great found Saint Petersburg?","a":"1703","x":["1682","1721"]}},"Q182984":{"t":"An ancient Sahel trading city on the savanna","s":"Kano spreads across the flat, dusty savanna of northern Nigeria, a vast carpet of low, earth-coloured roofs. For a thousand years it has been a trading city, a southern end of the caravan routes across the Sahara. In its old walled heart, cloth is still dyed in pits of indigo that have been in use since the 15th century.","w":"A huge low city of sandy browns on flat, dry savanna, with green fields and reservoirs scattered around it.","f":["The Kofar Mata dye pits have been worked for more than 500 years.","Kano's Kurmi Market dates back to the 15th century.","The rainy season here lasts only about four months, from June to September."],"q":{"q":"Which dye colours cloth in Kano's old dye pits?","a":"Indigo blue","x":["Saffron yellow","Madder red"]}},"Q270":{"t":"A capital on a river still left half wild","s":"Warsaw stands on the Vistula, and the river is the surprise: while most capitals have tamed theirs, here broad sandbanks, willow thickets and beaches line the water right through the city. The centre sits on a low escarpment on the west bank; the Praga district faces it across the river. The royal court moved here from Kraków around 1596.","w":"A wide river with pale sandbanks and green, wooded banks winding north through a large city on a flat plain.","f":["The Vistula, Poland's longest river, runs just over 1,000 km from the Carpathians to the Baltic.","Frédéric Chopin was born in 1810 at Żelazowa Wola, about 50 km west of Warsaw.","Warsaw's historic Old Town is a UNESCO World Heritage site."],"q":{"q":"From which city did Poland's royal court move to Warsaw around 1596?","a":"Kraków","x":["Gdańsk","Poznań"]}},"Q1960":{"t":"A 'haven of peace' on a sheltered harbour","s":"Dar es Salaam curls around a narrow, sheltered harbour on the Indian Ocean, with coral islets and turquoise reefs scattered just offshore. Sultan Majid of Zanzibar founded the town in the 1860s and gave it an Arabic name meaning 'haven of peace'. Today it is Tanzania's largest city, sprawling far inland across the coastal plain.","w":"A sprawling coastal city around a narrow-mouthed harbour, with small green islands and reefs just offshore to the north.","f":["Tanzania's official capital is Dodoma, but Dar es Salaam remains its largest city and main port.","Zanzibar Town lies about 70 km to the north, across a channel of the Indian Ocean.","The islands just north of the city, such as Bongoyo and Mbudya, are part of a marine reserve."],"q":{"q":"What does the name Dar es Salaam mean?","a":"Haven of peace","x":["Gate of the sea","House of spices"]}},"Q1761":{"t":"A river city between a round bay and green mountains","s":"Dublin rings the inner curve of Dublin Bay, with the Liffey running straight east through its heart into the sea. The rocky peninsula of Howth closes the bay to the north, and the Wicklow Mountains rise dark and green just to the south. Vikings set up a fortified harbour here in 841, beside a dark tidal pool — the dubh linn.","w":"A city around a rounded bay, a river running east through its centre, a knobbly peninsula to the north and hills to the south.","f":["Phoenix Park, on the city's western side, covers about 700 hectares — one of Europe's largest enclosed city parks.","The city's Irish name, Baile Átha Cliath, means 'town of the hurdled ford'.","Two long sea walls reach into the bay from the river mouth; the southern one was finished in the 1790s."],"q":{"q":"What does 'dubh linn', the source of Dublin's name, mean?","a":"Black pool","x":["Green hill","Fair harbour"]}},"Q3748":{"t":"A rain-soaked capital on a green cape","s":"Monrovia stretches along Cape Mesurado, a narrow peninsula between the Atlantic and a maze of lagoons and mangrove creeks. It is one of the rainiest capitals on Earth, and in the wet season the land around it glows a deep green. The city was founded in 1822 by freed African Americans and named after US president James Monroe.","w":"A narrow cape between the ocean and a mangrove-fringed river mouth, with the city running along its spine.","f":["Monrovia gets more than 4,500 mm of rain a year, several times as much as London.","The first settlers landed in 1822 on Providence Island, in the mouth of the Mesurado River.","The city's port lies on Bushrod Island, wrapped by the Saint Paul and Mesurado rivers."],"q":{"q":"Which US president was Monrovia named after?","a":"James Monroe","x":["Thomas Jefferson","James Madison"]}},"Q2634":{"t":"A city on a famous bay, beneath a volcano","s":"Naples curves around one of the most famous bays in the world, and its landmark is impossible to miss: the cone of Vesuvius, cradled in the broken rim of an older volcano just south-east of the city. To the west lies another volcanic area, the Phlegraean Fields, pitted with craters. Out in the bay float Capri and Ischia.","w":"A broad bay with a big volcanic cone on its south-eastern side, islands at its mouth, and the city along the north shore.","f":["Vesuvius rises to about 1,280 metres straight from the shore of the bay.","The Phlegraean Fields west of the city form a large volcanic caldera, partly under the sea.","Greek settlers named the city Neapolis, and its historic centre is now a UNESCO World Heritage site."],"q":{"q":"What does the Greek name Neapolis, Naples' origin, mean?","a":"New city","x":["Sea city","Holy city"]}},"Q490":{"t":"A flat city on the Po plain, the Alps on its horizon","s":"Milan lies in the flat heart of the Po plain, a city of rings around a medieval core, with motorways and railways fanning out in every direction. Look north on a clear day: the Alps rise like a white wall, and at their feet glint the great lakes, Como and Maggiore. In late Roman times, as Mediolanum, this was an imperial capital.","w":"A dense, roughly round city on a perfectly flat plain, with the Alps and long glacial lakes to the north.","f":["Milan Cathedral was begun in 1386 and took nearly six centuries to finish.","With no big river of its own, Milan dug canals, the Navigli, from the 12th century to reach the Ticino and Adda.","On clear days Monte Rosa, more than 4,600 metres high, can be seen from the city."],"q":{"q":"What was Milan called in Roman times?","a":"Mediolanum","x":["Augusta Taurinorum","Placentia"]}},"Q87":{"t":"A long ribbon of a city between sea and lake","s":"Alexandria stretches for kilometres along the Mediterranean, a thin ribbon of pale buildings squeezed between the sea and the shallow Lake Mariout behind it. Look for the curve of its eastern harbour. Alexander the Great founded the city in 331 BC, and its lighthouse, the Pharos, was one of the Seven Wonders of the ancient world.","w":"A narrow city strung along a straight coast, with a curved harbour and a flat lake and marshes just inland.","f":["The city runs more than 30 km along the coast but is only a few kilometres deep.","The ancient Library of Alexandria was one of the great centres of learning of the classical world.","The Pharos stood on an island at the harbour mouth for well over 1,500 years."],"q":{"q":"Who founded Alexandria in 331 BC?","a":"Alexander the Great","x":["Ptolemy I","Julius Caesar"]}},"Q1794":{"t":"A city on the Main, its airport carved from forest","s":"Frankfurt lies on the Main about 30 km before it joins the Rhine, with the dark, wooded Taunus hills rising to the north-west. South of the river a great forest spreads out, and cut into it is one of Europe's busiest airports, its runways pale stripes among the trees. For centuries this was where German kings were elected.","w":"A compact centre on a river running east–west, with a big forest to the south cut by long, straight airport runways.","f":["From 1356 the Holy Roman Emperors were elected in Frankfurt, and from 1562 they were crowned here too.","Johann Wolfgang von Goethe was born in Frankfurt in 1749.","The Taunus range to the north-west rises to about 880 metres at the Großer Feldberg."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows through Frankfurt?","a":"The Main","x":["The Neckar","The Mosel"]}},"Q1055":{"t":"A great port far up the Elbe, with a lake at its heart","s":"Hamburg lies about 100 km up the Elbe from the North Sea, yet it is one of Europe's largest ports. From above, look for the maze of docks and container terminals on the south bank, and for a bright lake right in the city centre: the Alster, a small river dammed centuries ago. Hamburg was a leading city of the Hanseatic League.","w":"A wide river with a maze of harbour basins on its south bank and a broad lake in the city centre just north of it.","f":["The Alster lakes were created in the Middle Ages by damming a small river to drive a mill.","The Speicherstadt, a warehouse district built on oak piles, is a UNESCO World Heritage site.","The tide reaches Hamburg: here the Elbe rises and falls by more than three metres."],"q":{"q":"Which lake lies in the centre of Hamburg?","a":"The Alster","x":["The Müritz","The Steinhuder Meer"]}},"Q1726":{"t":"A Bavarian city with the Alps on its doorstep","s":"Munich spreads across a flat gravel plain on the green Isar, with the wall of the Alps only about 60 km to the south. On clear days, especially when the warm, dry Föhn wind blows, the peaks look close enough to touch. South-west of the city, two big lakes, Starnberg and Ammersee, glint among fields and woods.","w":"A large city on a flat plain split by a green river valley, with lakes to the south-west and the Alps beyond.","f":["Munich's name comes from the monks, 'Mönche', who lived here before Henry the Lion founded the town in 1158.","The first Oktoberfest was held in 1810 to celebrate a royal wedding.","At about 520 metres, Munich is one of the highest large cities in Germany."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows through Munich?","a":"The Isar","x":["The Inn","The Lech"]}},"Q1085":{"t":"A river bending in S-curves through a hundred spires","s":"The Vltava gives Prague its shape. From above, the river swings through the city in a broad S, with the wooded rise of Petřín and the castle ridge on the west bank and the dense old town on the east. Green islands sit in the stream. About 30 km further north, the Vltava ends its journey in the Elbe.","w":"A river making big S-shaped bends through a dense city, wooded hills on its west bank and small islands mid-stream.","f":["Prague Castle is often listed as the largest ancient castle complex in the world.","Charles Bridge was begun in 1357 under Emperor Charles IV.","The historic centre of Prague has been a UNESCO World Heritage site since 1992."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows through Prague?","a":"The Vltava","x":["The Elbe","The Morava"]}},"Q35178":{"t":"A desert city curled around a wide, shallow bay","s":"Kuwait City wraps around the southern shore of Kuwait Bay, a broad inlet at the north-western corner of the Persian Gulf. Around it lies flat, almost featureless desert, crossed by ring roads that curve out from the old town in wide arcs. The name comes from the Arabic for 'little fort', and the town grew on pearling and seafaring.","w":"A city on the south shore of a wide bay, with semicircular ring roads arcing out into flat desert.","f":["The city's ring roads, numbered First to Seventh, curve out from the old centre in concentric arcs.","On Failaka Island, at the mouth of the bay, stand the remains of a Greek temple from the 3rd century BC.","Before oil, pearl diving was the backbone of Kuwait's economy."],"q":{"q":"What does the name Kuwait mean?","a":"Little fort","x":["Pearl bay","Sweet water"]}},"Q3761":{"t":"A capital right next door to the zero meridian","s":"Accra spreads along a flat stretch of the Gulf of Guinea, its low roofs running inland over a grassy coastal plain. Just to the east, at the port of Tema, the Greenwich meridian comes ashore, so you are very close to zero longitude — and only about 600 km north of the point where it crosses the equator.","w":"A broad, low city on a flat, gently curving coast, with a long ridge of hills rising inland to the north.","f":["The Greenwich meridian passes through Tema, about 25 km east of central Accra.","Accra's name is thought to come from 'nkran', an Akan word for ants.","Accra became the capital of the British Gold Coast colony in 1877."],"q":{"q":"Which port near Accra lies on the Greenwich meridian?","a":"Tema","x":["Takoradi","Lomé"]}},"Q3579":{"t":"An old Mediterranean port between sea and desert","s":"Tripoli spreads along the Libyan coast, a pale city on a low shore where a belt of gardens and palm groves gives way inland to desert. Phoenicians founded it as Oea in the 7th century BC. Its Greek name means 'three cities', after Oea and its neighbours Sabratha and Leptis Magna, both now famous Roman ruins.","w":"A pale city on a straight Mediterranean shore, a harbour at its old centre, green farmland fading into desert behind.","f":["The Arch of Marcus Aurelius, built around AD 165, still stands in Tripoli's old town.","Leptis Magna, some 120 km east of the city, is one of the best-preserved Roman cities in the Mediterranean.","Tripoli's walled old town sits right beside the harbour."],"q":{"q":"What does the Greek name Tripoli mean?","a":"Three cities","x":["Three hills","Sea gate"]}},"Q33935":{"t":"A white city on the dunes, an ancient port at its tip","s":"Tel Aviv lines a straight, sandy stretch of the Mediterranean, a dense band of pale buildings pressed right up to the beach. At its southern end, on a low rocky headland, is Jaffa, a port used for thousands of years. Tel Aviv itself is young: it was founded on the dunes north of Jaffa in 1909.","w":"A pale, dense city along a ruler-straight sandy coast, a small headland at its southern end, a river mouth to the north.","f":["Tel Aviv's 'White City' holds thousands of Bauhaus-style buildings and has been a UNESCO site since 2003.","The name Tel Aviv means 'spring hill' — spring as in the season.","The Yarkon River reaches the sea at the city's northern edge."],"q":{"q":"Which ancient port forms Tel Aviv's southern end?","a":"Jaffa","x":["Acre","Caesarea"]}},"Q956":{"t":"An imperial grid framed by mountains on two sides","s":"Beijing is laid out like a chessboard: straight avenues, a strict north–south axis and ring roads nested one inside another. Mountains curl around it on the west and north, while the plain opens towards the sea in the south-east. Its name means “northern capital”, and emperors ruled from here for most of the time since the 13th century.","w":"A vast grid on a flat plain, hemmed in by dark mountains to the north and west; at night, rectangular rings of light.","f":["The 2nd Ring Road traces the line of the old city walls, which were largely pulled down in the 20th century.","The Grand Canal, almost 1,800 km long, reaches its northern end on Beijing's eastern side.","The Forbidden City, finished in 1420, sits exactly on the city's north–south axis."],"q":{"q":"What does the name “Beijing” mean?","a":"Northern capital","x":["Southern capital","Western capital"]}},"Q3630":{"t":"Java's great sprawl, with a scatter of islands offshore","s":"Jakarta spreads over a flat, hazy plain where Java meets the shallow Java Sea, and the city seems to have no edge — it simply fades into its neighbours. Offshore, the Thousand Islands dot the water like crumbs. On a clear day, volcanic cones rise to the south. The Dutch called it Batavia and ran their East Indies trade from here.","w":"A huge grey sprawl on Java's north coast, a bay with small islands to the north, volcanoes on the southern horizon.","f":["Despite their name, the Thousand Islands off Jakarta number only a little over a hundred.","The Dutch East India Company founded Batavia here in 1619, on the site of the port of Jayakarta.","The old harbour of Sunda Kelapa still hosts wooden pinisi schooners."],"q":{"q":"What was Jakarta called under Dutch rule?","a":"Batavia","x":["Buitenzorg","Nieuw Amsterdam"]}},"Q8686":{"t":"Where the muddy Yangtze finally meets the sea","s":"Look for brown water turning blue: the Yangtze pours its silt into the East China Sea right beside Shanghai. The city lies flat on the south bank, cut in two by the winding Huangpu River. Out to sea, a hair-thin line runs across the water — a bridge more than 30 km long to an island container port.","w":"A giant flat city on the south side of the brown Yangtze estuary, with a long, low island in the river mouth.","f":["Chongming Island in the Yangtze mouth is built entirely of river silt and is about 80 km long.","Shanghai has been the world's busiest container port since 2010.","The name is usually read as “on the sea” or “upon the sea”."],"q":{"q":"Which river winds through the centre of Shanghai?","a":"The Huangpu","x":["The Pearl River","The Qiantang"]}},"Q1490":{"t":"A city that fills an entire plain, wrapped round a bay","s":"Tokyo does not so much end as run out of flat land: buildings cover the Kanto Plain, Japan's largest, from the mountains to the sea. The city wraps around Tokyo Bay, much of whose shore is reclaimed land. On a clear day Fuji's cone stands about 100 km to the south-west. Until 1868 this was Edo, the shoguns' city.","w":"Continuous city around a wide bay; Fuji's lone cone to the south-west; at night an unbroken carpet of lights.","f":["Odaiba is named after cannon batteries built in the bay in 1853–54, after Commodore Perry's ships arrived.","The name Tokyo means “eastern capital”; the emperor moved here from Kyoto in 1868–69.","The Aqua-Line runs out into Tokyo Bay and seems to stop at an island — the rest of the road is a tunnel under the seabed."],"q":{"q":"What was Tokyo called before 1868?","a":"Edo","x":["Naniwa","Heian-kyō"]}},"Q1156":{"t":"Seven islands stitched into one long peninsula","s":"Mumbai points south into the Arabian Sea like a thumb. It was once seven separate islands, joined from the 18th century onwards by causeways and landfill into one crowded strip. Harbour water lies to the east, open sea to the west. After dark, look for the bright curve of lights along Back Bay — locals call it the Queen's Necklace.","w":"A narrow finger of land jutting south into the sea, a wide harbour on its east side, a green forest patch in the north.","f":["Portugal handed Bombay to England in 1661 as part of Catherine of Braganza's dowry on her marriage to Charles II.","Sanjay Gandhi National Park, a forest with wild leopards, lies inside the city limits.","A sea bridge about 22 km long, opened in 2024, links the city with the mainland across the harbour."],"q":{"q":"Mumbai was formed by joining how many islands?","a":"Seven","x":["Three","Twelve"]}},"Q1348":{"t":"Former capital of British India on a delta river","s":"Kolkata stretches along the east bank of the Hooghly, a working arm of the Ganges, with Howrah facing it across the water. Beyond its eastern edge, a patchwork of ponds and marshes quietly cleans the city's wastewater and raises fish. Further south the land dissolves into the channels and mangroves of the Sundarbans. Until 1911, this was the capital of British India.","w":"A long city on a brown river running north–south; square fish ponds to the east, delta channels to the south.","f":["The East Kolkata Wetlands use the city's sewage to feed fish ponds and are a protected Ramsar site.","Kolkata's port lies on the river, well over 100 km from the open sea.","The Sundarbans to the south are the world's largest mangrove forest, shared with Bangladesh."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows through Kolkata?","a":"The Hooghly","x":["The Brahmaputra","The Mahanadi"]}},"Q3130":{"t":"A drowned valley of blue fingers, open to the Pacific","s":"Sydney's harbour is a drowned river valley, and from above it looks like a hand of blue fingers reaching deep into the city. Sandstone headlands guard its narrow mouth to the Pacific. South lies Botany Bay, where the airport's runways run out into the water. To the west, the Blue Mountains rise in a haze that really does look blue.","w":"A deep, branching harbour entering the coast between two headlands; a round bay with runways to the south.","f":["The blue haze over the Blue Mountains comes from oils evaporating from eucalyptus leaves.","James Cook landed at Botany Bay in 1770; the First Fleet settled at Sydney Cove in 1788.","The Harbour Bridge, opened in 1932, is nicknamed the Coathanger for the shape of its arch."],"q":{"q":"Where did James Cook land in 1770, on Sydney's south side?","a":"Botany Bay","x":["Moreton Bay","Port Phillip Bay"]}},"Q334":{"t":"An island nation ringed by hundreds of waiting ships","s":"Before the city, you notice the sea: hundreds of ships at anchor in the Singapore Strait, scattered like grains of rice. The diamond-shaped island at the tip of the Malay Peninsula has grown by roughly a quarter through land reclamation, and its straight new shorelines are easy to spot. The equator lies less than 150 km to the south.","w":"A diamond-shaped island just off the Malay Peninsula, joined by a causeway, with anchored ships speckling the strait.","f":["Stamford Raffles set up a British trading post here in 1819.","The name comes from the Malay Singapura, “lion city”.","Jurong Island, the refining hub in the south-west, was made by joining seven small islands with landfill."],"q":{"q":"What does the name “Singapura” mean?","a":"Lion city","x":["Tiger city","City by the sea"]}},"Q8646":{"t":"Green mountains, a crowded shore and a fragrant harbour","s":"From above, Hong Kong is surprisingly green: steep, forested hills and dozens of islands, with the city packed into thin strips along the shore. Victoria Harbour, a narrow blue channel, separates Kowloon from Hong Kong Island. Further west, look for the airport on a flat man-made platform and a long bridge striking out across the Pearl River estuary.","w":"Hilly green islands and a peninsula, dense city lining a narrow harbour; a rectangular airport island to the west.","f":["About 40 percent of Hong Kong's land is protected as country parks.","The bridge to Zhuhai and Macau is about 55 km long and dips into an undersea tunnel between two artificial islands.","The name means “fragrant harbour”, probably after the incense wood once shipped from here."],"q":{"q":"What does the name “Hong Kong” mean?","a":"Fragrant harbour","x":["Pearl harbour","Deep harbour"]}},"Q1353":{"t":"Seven cities in one, on the banks of the Yamuna","s":"Delhi has been rebuilt again and again on the same stretch of the Yamuna, and tradition counts at least seven historic cities layered here. From above, the river runs down the eastern side, braided with sandbars in the dry season. A low, scrubby ridge — the last tip of the ancient Aravalli hills — cuts through the west. At night the glow spreads far across the plain.","w":"A huge sprawl on a flat plain, a braided river along its east side, a dark wooded ridge through the west.","f":["Old Delhi, or Shahjahanabad, was built by Shah Jahan, creator of the Taj Mahal; its Red Fort was finished in 1648.","The Aravalli Range, whose last spur ends in Delhi, is one of the oldest mountain ranges on Earth.","The Iron Pillar near the Qutb Minar has stood for about 1,600 years with remarkably little rust."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows past Delhi?","a":"The Yamuna","x":["The Ganges","The Sutlej"]}},"Q269":{"t":"A green oasis city at the foot of the Tian Shan","s":"Tashkent spreads as a broad, leafy grid across an irrigated plain, fed by canals drawn from the Chirchiq River. To the north-east the land rises into the first ridges of the Tian Shan, snowy in winter and spring, and the turquoise Charvak Reservoir fills a mountain valley. Caravans on the Silk Road knew this oasis as Chach.","w":"A large green grid on a flat plain; mountains rising to the north-east with a turquoise reservoir in a valley.","f":["The name is usually read as “stone city” in Turkic.","Tashkent's metro, opened in 1977, was the first in Central Asia.","It is the most populous city in Central Asia."],"q":{"q":"What does the name “Tashkent” most likely mean?","a":"Stone city","x":["Green city","Sun city"]}},"Q1861":{"t":"City of angels on a looping brown river","s":"The Chao Phraya winds through Bangkok in lazy brown loops, and the whole city sits on a delta so flat it barely rises above the sea. Around it, rice fields and square fish ponds stretch to the Gulf of Thailand, about 30 km south. Thais rarely say Bangkok: they call it Krung Thep, the city of angels.","w":"A vast flat grey city on a looping brown river, with a grid of green fields and ponds between it and the gulf.","f":["Bangkok's full ceremonial name is one of the longest place names in the world.","It became the capital in 1782, when King Rama I moved the court across the river from Thonburi.","Its old network of canals earned Bangkok the nickname “Venice of the East”."],"q":{"q":"What do Thais usually call Bangkok?","a":"Krung Thep","x":["Ayutthaya","Sukhothai"]}},"Q8684":{"t":"A wide river and granite peaks inside one capital","s":"Seoul is a bowl of city among steep granite mountains, split by the broad Han River. Up to a kilometre wide here, the Han is crossed by more than 25 bridges that show as fine stitches across the water. Joseon kings made it their capital in 1394: mountains behind, a river in front — ideal by the rules of geomancy.","w":"A broad river curving west through a dense city ringed by rocky, wooded mountains; bridges cross it every kilometre or two.","f":["Joseon kings ringed their capital with a stone wall about 18 km long, begun in 1396.","Bukhansan, a national park of granite peaks, rises right on the city's northern edge.","In Korean, the word “Seoul” simply means “capital”."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows through Seoul?","a":"The Han","x":["The Nakdong","The Taedong"]}},"Q1461":{"t":"A great round bay, a river and a lake behind the city","s":"Manila lines the eastern shore of a broad, almost circular bay, famous for its sunsets. The Pasig River threads through the city, linking the bay to Laguna de Bay, the largest lake in the Philippines. For 250 years Spanish galleons sailed from here to Mexico, carrying Chinese silk and porcelain across the Pacific.","w":"A dense city on the east shore of a wide round bay; a river winds east to a huge lake.","f":["The Spanish founded walled Intramuros in 1571; the Manila galleons ran to Acapulco from 1565 to 1815.","The name comes from nilad, a mangrove shrub that once grew along the river.","Laguna de Bay is very shallow: on average it is only about three metres deep."],"q":{"q":"Where did the Manila galleons sail to across the Pacific?","a":"Acapulco","x":["Lima","San Francisco"]}},"Q16959":{"t":"The big city farthest from any sea","s":"No large city on Earth lies farther from the ocean than Ürümqi — the nearest coastline is about 2,500 km away. It fills a valley on the northern foot of the Tian Shan, with the snowy massif of Bogda Peak rising to the east. North of the city the land flattens into the dry Dzungarian Basin.","w":"A long city in a valley below snowy mountains; the glaciers of Bogda Peak to the east, dry basin to the north.","f":["Bogda Peak is 5,445 m high — and still more than 5 km below you.","The Gurbantünggüt, China's second-largest desert, fills the basin north of the city.","The name comes from Mongolian and means “beautiful pasture”."],"q":{"q":"Roughly how far is Ürümqi from the nearest sea?","a":"About 2,500 km","x":["About 900 km","About 5,000 km"]}},"Q30002":{"t":"A round city on a plain watered since 256 BC","s":"Chengdu sits on a remarkably flat, green plain on the western edge of the Sichuan Basin, its ring roads drawing concentric circles. Just west, mountains rise abruptly towards the Tibetan Plateau. The plain owes its fertility to Dujiangyan, an irrigation system built around 256 BC that still splits the Min River into a fan of canals.","w":"A round city of concentric ring roads on a green plain; a wall of mountains rising sharply to the west.","f":["An old saying claims Sichuan's dogs bark at the sun because they see it so rarely through the haze.","Dujiangyan, about 50 km north-west of the city, is a UNESCO World Heritage site.","Chengdu has had a giant panda breeding centre since 1987."],"q":{"q":"Which ancient irrigation system still waters the Chengdu Plain?","a":"Dujiangyan","x":["The Grand Canal","The Lingqu Canal"]}},"Q35765":{"t":"Japan's old kitchen, with an airport out in the bay","s":"Osaka fills the flat land at the head of Osaka Bay, where the Yodo River splits into channels on its way to the sea. The built-up area runs on without a break towards Kobe and Kyoto. Out in the bay, look for a neat rectangle joined to the shore by a single long bridge: Kansai Airport, built on a man-made island.","w":"A continuous city around the head of a bay; a rectangular airport island offshore, linked by one bridge.","f":["Osaka ruled the rice trade in the Edo period so completely that it was called “the nation's kitchen”.","Toyotomi Hideyoshi began building Osaka Castle in 1583.","In neighbouring Sakai, the keyhole-shaped burial mound of Emperor Nintoku is about 486 m long."],"q":{"q":"What was Osaka nicknamed in the Edo period?","a":"The nation's kitchen","x":["The nation's forge","The nation's garden"]}},"Q987":{"t":"A planned imperial capital inside a far older city","s":"Within Delhi's endless sprawl, look for a greener, looser patch south of the old city: that is New Delhi. It was planned from scratch after 1911, when Britain decided to move its Indian capital here from Calcutta. Architects Edwin Lutyens and Herbert Baker laid out broad avenues meeting at roundabouts, with a ceremonial axis running east from Raisina Hill.","w":"A greener, more open patch in the middle of the Delhi sprawl, a few kilometres west of the Yamuna.","f":["New Delhi was formally inaugurated as the capital in 1931, twenty years after the move was announced.","Its plan is built from triangles and hexagons, with avenues radiating out from roundabouts.","The presidential palace on Raisina Hill has about 340 rooms."],"q":{"q":"When was New Delhi formally inaugurated as the capital?","a":"1931","x":["1911","1947"]}},"Q1355":{"t":"A garden city of lakes on a cool high plateau","s":"Bengaluru sits about 900 m up on the Deccan Plateau, which keeps it noticeably cooler than India's coastal cities. From above, look for dozens of lakes glinting across the sprawl: most are old reservoirs, or tanks, made centuries ago by damming shallow valleys. A local chieftain, Kempe Gowda, founded the city with a mud fort in 1537.","w":"A sprawling city on rolling upland, freckled with lakes of all sizes; bare granite hills rise to the north.","f":["Its many software companies have earned it the nickname “India's Silicon Valley”.","Lalbagh, a botanical garden begun under Hyder Ali in the 18th century, helped earn it the name “Garden City”.","In 2014 the official name changed from Bangalore to Bengaluru, as it has always been called in Kannada."],"q":{"q":"Roughly how high above sea level is Bengaluru?","a":"About 900 m","x":["About 100 m","About 2,000 m"]}},"Q5838":{"t":"A high mountain valley where Babur chose to rest","s":"Kabul lies about 1,800 m up, in a bowl of bare brown mountains, with rocky ridges rising right through the city itself. The Kabul River winds east from here towards the Khyber Pass and, eventually, the Indus. In winter the snowy wall of the Hindu Kush shines to the north. Babur, founder of the Mughal Empire, loved this valley and chose to be buried here.","w":"A dense city in a high, dry basin with bare ridges poking through it; snowy mountains to the north.","f":["Babur's tomb stands in Bagh-e Babur, a terraced garden he laid out himself.","At about 1,800 m, Kabul stands higher than the summit of Ben Nevis, Britain's highest mountain.","The Kabul River flows east into Pakistan and joins the Indus at Attock."],"q":{"q":"Which Mughal emperor chose to be buried in Kabul?","a":"Babur","x":["Akbar","Aurangzeb"]}},"Q3141":{"t":"A city at the head of a vast, nearly closed bay","s":"Melbourne sits at the top of Port Phillip Bay, a huge sheet of water almost sealed off from the ocean. Its entrance, the Rip, is only about 3 km wide and has a fierce reputation among sailors. The Yarra River curls into the centre from the east, where the forested Dandenong Ranges rise. A gold rush in the 1850s made this one of the richest cities in the world.","w":"A sprawling city around the head of a huge, almost round bay; wooded hills to the east, flat plains to the west.","f":["From 1901 to 1927 Australia's federal parliament sat in Melbourne, until Canberra was ready.","The city was founded in 1835 on the banks of the Yarra.","Port Phillip Bay covers nearly 2,000 km², yet is nowhere deeper than about 24 m."],"q":{"q":"Roughly how wide is the entrance to Port Phillip Bay?","a":"About 3 km","x":["About 30 km","About 300 m"]}},"Q1867":{"t":"A city poured into a basin of green mountains","s":"Taipei fills a flat basin like water in a bowl, with forested mountains rising on every side. To the north, the dormant volcanoes of Yangmingshan steam quietly, feeding hot springs on the city's edge. Three rivers join in the basin and leave through a single gap to the north-west as the Tamsui, heading for the Taiwan Strait.","w":"A dense city filling a round basin, green mountains all around, a river snaking out north-west to the sea.","f":["Taipei 101, finished in 2004, was the world's tallest building until 2010.","Qixing Mountain in Yangmingshan, about 1,120 m high, is a dormant volcano within the city limits.","The hot springs of Beitou, on the north side of the city, are heated by the same volcanic group."],"q":{"q":"Which river drains the Taipei Basin to the sea?","a":"The Tamsui","x":["The Zhuoshui","The Gaoping"]}},"Q37100":{"t":"A city on a narrow neck between two seas","s":"Auckland is squeezed onto a narrow isthmus: the Waitematā Harbour opens east to the Pacific, the Manukau Harbour west to the Tasman Sea. The city sits on a field of about 50 small volcanoes, and many survive as green grassy cones among the suburbs. Just offshore, look for Rangitoto, a symmetrical island that rose from the sea only about 600 years ago.","w":"A city on a thin neck of land between two harbours, dotted with green cones; a round, symmetrical island just offshore.","f":["At its narrowest the isthmus is so thin that Māori once hauled canoes across it from one harbour to the other.","Auckland's Māori name, Tāmaki Makaurau, is often translated as “Tāmaki desired by many”.","Rangitoto is both the youngest and the largest volcano in the Auckland field."],"q":{"q":"Roughly how many volcanoes make up the Auckland volcanic field?","a":"About 50","x":["About 5","About 500"]}},"Q15174":{"t":"A boomtown along Hong Kong's northern edge","s":"Shenzhen runs in a long ribbon along the coast, facing the green hills of Hong Kong across a narrow river. Before 1980 this was a quiet border district of paddies and fishing villages; that year it became one of China's first Special Economic Zones and began growing at astonishing speed. At night its lights run almost unbroken from the Pearl River estuary to the eastern peninsulas.","w":"A long band of city along the coast just north of Hong Kong; a bay with a bridge on the west side.","f":["The Shenzhen River forms much of the boundary with Hong Kong.","A bridge about 5 km long crosses Shenzhen Bay to Hong Kong's New Territories.","Mangroves still line Shenzhen Bay at Futian, a protected refuge for migrating birds."],"q":{"q":"In which year did Shenzhen become a Special Economic Zone?","a":"1980","x":["1949","1997"]}},"Q198370":{"t":"A city of scattered towns, and football's ancient home","s":"Zibo is less one city than a loose cluster of towns, strung out where the hills of central Shandong fall away to the North China Plain. One of them, Linzi, was the capital of the ancient state of Qi and among the largest cities of its age. Another, Boshan, has been known for ceramics and coloured glass for centuries.","w":"Several separate urban patches spread along the edge of low hills, with the flat plain opening to the north.","f":["In 2004 FIFA recognised cuju, played in Linzi over 2,000 years ago, as the earliest known form of football.","Linzi remained the capital of Qi for centuries, until the Qin conquest in 221 BC."],"q":{"q":"Zibo's Linzi district is honoured as the birthplace of which sport?","a":"Football","x":["Table tennis","Badminton"]}},"Q1858":{"t":"A thousand-year capital on the rust-brown river","s":"Hanoi sits on the right bank of the Red River, whose silt-laden water really does look rusty from above. The city is freckled with lakes, the biggest being West Lake in the north. All around, the flat, green Red River Delta is laid out in paddies. The Lý dynasty made this their capital in 1010, calling it Thăng Long, “ascending dragon”.","w":"A city on the south-west bank of a broad brown river, with a large lake on its north side, amid flat green paddies.","f":["The name Hà Nội means roughly “inside the river”; the city received it in 1831.","West Lake, the largest lake in Hanoi, covers about 500 hectares."],"q":{"q":"What was Hanoi called when it became the capital in 1010?","a":"Thăng Long","x":["Phú Xuân","Gia Định"]}},"Q1854":{"t":"River loops, a vast delta and a maze of mangroves","s":"The Saigon River loops through the city in tight, shining bends before heading for the sea. South-east of the centre, the land gives way to Cần Giờ, a dark green maze of mangroves and tidal channels. To the south-west spreads the Mekong Delta, one of the great rice bowls of Asia. Many residents still call the city by its old name, Saigon.","w":"A dense city on a looping river; dark mangrove channels to the south-east, flat green delta to the south-west.","f":["The Cần Giờ mangrove forest is a UNESCO biosphere reserve.","Before Vietnamese settlers arrived in the 17th century, a Khmer port called Prey Nokor stood here."],"q":{"q":"Which mangrove forest lies between Ho Chi Minh City and the sea?","a":"Cần Giờ","x":["The Sundarbans","Bhitarkanika"]}},"Q11739":{"t":"A Mughal city of gardens on the Punjab plain","s":"Lahore spreads across the flat Punjab plain, with the Ravi River curving past its north-western edge. Irrigation canals draw straight lines through the fields around it. For centuries this was one of the great cities of the Mughal Empire, and Akbar kept his capital here for more than a decade. The old walled city is still a tight knot of lanes near the river.","w":"A large flat city with a river on its north-west side and straight canals running through green fields.","f":["Akbar held his court at Lahore from 1585 to 1598.","Lahore Fort and the Shalimar Gardens are listed together as one UNESCO World Heritage site.","The walled city was once entered through about a dozen gates."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows past Lahore?","a":"The Ravi","x":["The Jhelum","The Kabul"]}},"Q8660":{"t":"A megacity on a dry coast beside the Indus Delta","s":"Karachi sprawls over a dry, sand-coloured coast, with bare hills behind it and the Arabian Sea in front. Its harbour is sheltered by a low spit of land, and to the south-east the shore frays into mangrove creeks where the Indus Delta begins. In the 18th century this was a small fishing settlement; today it is Pakistan's largest city.","w":"A vast beige city on a straight dry coast; a sheltered harbour and mangrove creeks to the south-east, bare hills inland.","f":["Its old name was Kolachi, after a fishing village on this shore.","Karachi was Pakistan's capital from 1947 until 1959.","The Indus Delta mangroves to the south-east are among the largest mangrove forests in a dry climate."],"q":{"q":"What was Karachi's name as an 18th-century fishing village?","a":"Kolachi","x":["Thatta","Gwadar"]}},"Q18808":{"t":"A river-island city once called the capital of willows","s":"Pyongyang lies on both banks of the Taedong River, which flows here in wide, calm reaches around long, low islands. Beyond the city, flat farmland and low hills stretch towards the Yellow Sea, about 50 km to the south-west. The name means “flat land”, and in 427 the Goguryeo kingdom moved its capital here.","w":"A city on both banks of a broad river with long islands; the river winds south-west across flat farmland to the sea.","f":["An old name for the city is Ryugyong, “capital of willows”, after the trees along its riverbanks.","Goguryeo ruled from Pyongyang for more than two centuries, from 427 until 668."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows through Pyongyang?","a":"The Taedong","x":["The Han","The Yalu"]}},"Q5972":{"t":"Sumatra's biggest city, built on tobacco","s":"Medan spreads over the flat coastal plain of north-eastern Sumatra, a few kilometres inland from the Strait of Malacca. Around it, plantations lay out the land in vast, tidy grids. In the 19th century the leaf grown here, Deli tobacco, was prized worldwide as cigar wrapper, and a small village grew rich. To the south-west, volcanic cones rise towards the great caldera lake of Toba.","w":"A grey sprawl on a flat plain near the coast, ringed by gridded plantations; volcanic peaks on the south-west horizon.","f":["Medan is the largest city on Sumatra.","Lake Toba, south of the city, fills the caldera of a supervolcano that erupted about 74,000 years ago.","Deli tobacco, named after the local sultanate, was grown here for cigar wrappers from the 1860s."],"q":{"q":"Which crop made Medan rich in the 19th century?","a":"Tobacco","x":["Nutmeg","Cloves"]}},"Q1865":{"t":"A muddy river junction that tin turned into a capital","s":"Kuala Lumpur fills the Klang Valley, with the forested ridges of the Titiwangsa Mountains rising to the east. The city began in the 1850s as a rough camp of tin miners, and many of the lakes glinting around it are old flooded mine pits. To the south, look for Putrajaya, a planned government town wrapped around a large artificial lake.","w":"A dense city in a green valley, forested mountains to the east, scattered lakes around it and a planned town to the south.","f":["Kuala Lumpur means “muddy confluence”: it grew where the Gombak River meets the Klang.","The Petronas Twin Towers were the world's tallest buildings from 1998 to 2004.","Batu Caves, in a limestone hill on the city's northern edge, have been a Hindu shrine since the 1890s."],"q":{"q":"What does the name “Kuala Lumpur” mean?","a":"Muddy confluence","x":["River of tin","Town of hills"]}},"Q183584":{"t":"A long, thin city squeezed along the Yellow River","s":"Lanzhou is a city with nowhere to spread: it runs for tens of kilometres along a narrow valley of the Yellow River, hemmed in by bare, ochre loess hills. From above it looks like a grey ribbon laid in a crease of the land. For Silk Road caravans, this was the key crossing of the Yellow River on the way west into the Hexi Corridor.","w":"A narrow grey ribbon of city following a winding river between bare yellow-brown hills.","f":["Zhongshan Bridge, finished in 1909, was the first permanent bridge across the Yellow River.","Before bridges, travellers crossed the river here on rafts buoyed by inflated sheepskins."],"q":{"q":"Which river runs through the middle of Lanzhou?","a":"The Yellow River","x":["The Yangtze","The Wei River"]}},"Q179608":{"t":"A green subtropical city in the loops of the Yong","s":"Nanning coils around the wide meanders of the Yong River, deep in China's subtropical south. The countryside around it is a patchwork of green hills and fields, many of them planted with sugarcane. The border with Vietnam is less than 200 km to the south-west, and since 2004 the city has hosted a yearly trade fair with South-East Asia.","w":"A city spread around broad river loops, with green hills and fields all around.","f":["Guangxi, whose capital is Nanning, grows more than half of China's sugarcane.","Its year-round greenery has earned Nanning the nickname “Green City”.","The Yong River flows on into the Xi River and eventually reaches the Pearl River Delta."],"q":{"q":"Which crop makes Guangxi China's biggest producer?","a":"Sugarcane","x":["Tea","Cotton"]}}},"history":{}}